window.MED_BANK=[{"id":16,"module":"BCMH","title":"Anaphylaxis","scenario":"A 19-year-old man is brought to the emergency unit from a university event. Ten minutes after eating a dessert, he\ndeveloped intense itching, a widespread raised rash, swelling of his lips and tightness in his throat. He now feels\ndizzy and says it is becoming harder to breathe. He has a history of allergic rhinitis and childhood asthma. He has\nnever had a reaction this severe before. On examination, he looks frightened and acutely unwell.\n Finding Result\n Blood pressure 82/48 mmHg\n Heart rate 138/min\n Respiratory rate 32/min\n Oxygen saturation 90% room air\n Temperature 36.7 C\n\nHe has widespread urticaria, lip swelling, audible wheeze, cool peripheries and a hoarse voice.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest working diagnosis?","options":["Severe asthma exacerbation","Vasovagal collapse after food exposure","Acute urticaria with angioedema","Anaphylaxis"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"The key pattern is rapid onset after food exposure with skin involvement, angioedema, wheeze,\nthroat or voice symptoms and hypotension. That is anaphylaxis until proven otherwise. The revised distractors are\nplausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full\nstem.","point":"Acute rash plus airway, breathing or circulation compromise is anaphylaxis.","skill":"Recognition"},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which feature most clearly shows immediate danger?","options":["Recent food exposure","Low blood pressure","Diffuse itching","History of childhood asthma"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Low blood pressure indicates circulatory compromise. In anaphylaxis, danger comes from airway\nswelling, bronchospasm and distributive shock physiology. The revised distractors are plausible competing\ndiagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"In anaphylaxis, hypotension is shock until proven otherwise.","skill":"Safety/risk prioritisation"},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which immediate drug is most important?","options":["Adrenaline","Cetirizine","Salbutamol","Prednisone"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Adrenaline is the key emergency drug principle in anaphylaxis. It addresses life-threatening airway,\nbreathing and circulatory effects through adrenergic mechanisms. The revised distractors are plausible competing\ndiagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Adrenaline is central in anaphylaxis; antihistamines and bronchodilators are adjuncts.","skill":"Pharmacology role recognition"},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which hypersensitivity type best fits this reaction?","options":["Type II hypersensitivity","Type III hypersensitivity","Type I hypersensitivity","Type IV hypersensitivity"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Classic anaphylaxis is a Type I hypersensitivity reaction. It is immediate and IgE-mediated, with\nmast-cell and basophil activation after allergen exposure. The revised distractors are plausible competing\ndiagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Immediate systemic allergy = Type I hypersensitivity.","skill":"Applied immunology"},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Which mechanism triggers mediator release?","options":["IgG opsonisation","Immune complexes","T-cell cytotoxicity","IgE cross-linking"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"In sensitised patients, allergen cross-links IgE bound to mast cells and basophils. This triggers\nrelease of mediators such as histamine and leukotrienes. The revised distractors are plausible competing\ndiagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Allergen cross-linking of IgE is the trigger step in classic anaphylaxis.","skill":"Mechanism reasoning"},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"Which cell is most central acutely?","options":["Eosinophil recruitment","Mast-cell activation","Neutrophil oxidative burst","CD8+ T-cell cytotoxicity"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Mast cells are central to the immediate reaction. They carry surface-bound IgE and release\npreformed and newly generated mediators after allergen-triggered activation. The revised distractors are plausible\ncompeting diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Mast-cell degranulation drives acute Type I allergic reactions.","skill":"Cell-function identification"},{"number":7,"type":"SBA","q":"Which effect best explains his wheeze?","options":["Bronchoconstriction","Upper-airway mucosal oedema","Alveolar inflammatory exudate","Pulmonary vascular obstruction"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Histamine and leukotrienes can cause bronchial smooth-muscle contraction. This narrows airways\nand produces wheeze. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management\nroles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Wheeze in anaphylaxis reflects bronchospasm.","skill":"Applied physiology"},{"number":8,"type":"SBA","q":"Which shock category fits best?","options":["Cardiogenic shock","Obstructive shock","Distributive shock","Hypovolaemic shock"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Anaphylactic shock is a distributive shock pattern. Mediators cause vasodilation and increased\nvascular permeability, producing maldistributed blood flow and reduced effective circulating volume. The revised\ndistractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly\nsupported by the full stem.","point":"Anaphylaxis causes distributive shock through vasodilation and vascular leak.","skill":"Shock classification"},{"number":9,"type":"SBA","q":"Repeat assessment shows widespread urticaria, hoarse voice, wheeze, hypotension and raised tryptase. Which\ninterpretation is best?","options":["Isolated lower-airway bronchospasm","Isolated cutaneous urticaria","Viral exanthem with anxiety","Systemic mast-cell activation"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Urticaria, hoarse voice, wheeze, hypotension and raised tryptase support mast-cell activation in\nanaphylaxis. Tryptase is supportive, but emergency action should not wait for the result. The revised distractors are\nplausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full\nstem.","point":"Raised tryptase supports anaphylaxis, but anaphylaxis remains a clinical diagnosis.","skill":"Data interpretation"},{"number":10,"type":"SBA","q":"Which feature makes asthma alone unsafe?","options":["Urticaria","Diffuse wheeze","Tachypnoea","Previous childhood asthma"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Wheeze can occur in asthma and anaphylaxis. Urticaria points away from isolated asthma and\ntoward systemic allergy. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management\nroles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Wheeze plus rash or angioedema is not just asthma.","skill":"Differential discrimination"},{"number":11,"type":"SBA","q":"Why is salbutamol alone inadequate?","options":["It prevents allergen re-exposure","It mainly reverses bronchospasm","It blocks histamine at H1 receptors","It suppresses IgE production"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Salbutamol can help bronchospasm, so it may improve wheeze. But anaphylaxis also involves\nairway swelling, vasodilation, vascular leak and hypotension. The revised distractors are plausible competing\ndiagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Treat the syndrome, not only the wheeze.","skill":"Pharmacology discrimination"},{"number":12,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest system action?","options":["Treat the rash and arrange allergy-clinic referral","Observe after antihistamine if wheeze improves","Urgent escalation","Wait for tryptase before activating emergency care"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"This patient has airway symptoms, bronchospasm, hypoxia and hypotension. That is immediately\nlife-threatening. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but\nthey are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Anaphylaxis with airway or circulatory compromise requires immediate escalation.","skill":"Patient safety/escalation"},{"number":13,"type":"SBA","q":"Which later step helps prevent recurrence?","options":["Antihistamine pre-treatment before re-exposure","Reliever inhaler use before eating desserts","Avoidance of exertion after meals","Trigger identification and avoidance"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Once the emergency is stabilised, prevention requires identifying the likely trigger, documenting it\nclearly, educating the patient and reducing future exposure risk. Antibiotics, fluid restriction and bed rest do not\naddress recurrence from allergen re-exposure.","point":"Anaphylaxis care continues after resuscitation: prevent re-exposure.","skill":"Prevention/vertical theme"},{"number":14,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What hypersensitivity type causes classic IgE-mediated anaphylaxis?","options":[],"answer":"Type I hypersensitivity","accepted":[],"explain":"Type I hypersensitivity is immediate and IgE-mediated, involving mast cells and basophils.","point":"Type I = immediate IgE-mediated allergy.","skill":"Immunology terminology"},{"number":15,"type":"VSAQ","q":"Which emergency drug is central in anaphylaxis?","options":[],"answer":"Adrenaline","accepted":["Adrenaline","epinephrine"],"explain":"Adrenaline is the central emergency drug principle in anaphylaxis. Exact dose, route and repeat\ntiming remain protocol-level details unless supplied.","point":"Adrenaline is the life-saving anaphylaxis drug.","skill":"Pharmacology terminology"}],"source":"Student Tier 1 Teaching Bank"},{"id":35,"module":"BCMH","title":"HIV and opportunistic disease","scenario":"A 34-year-old man presents to a primary-care clinic with 2 months of weight loss, fatigue and intermittent low-grade\nfever. Over the past 3 weeks he has developed painful white patches in his mouth. He says the patches sometimes\ncome off when he brushes his tongue, leaving a sore red surface underneath.\nHe also has a dry cough and becomes short of breath when walking uphill. He has no known asthma, no chest\npain, no haemoptysis and no leg swelling. He has never had an HIV test. He has had several sexual partners and\nsays condom use has been inconsistent.\nOn examination, he appears thin and tired.\n Finding Result\n Temperature 37.9 degrees C\n Heart rate 106/min\n Blood pressure 112/70 mmHg\n Respiratory rate 24/min\n Oxygen saturation 92% room air\n\nThere are thick white plaques on the tongue and buccal mucosa. Some areas scrape off, exposing an\nerythematous base. There are also several small violaceous plaques on the hard palate and lower leg. Chest\nexamination reveals mild bilateral crackles without focal bronchial breathing.\nHe agrees to HIV testing after counselling. The initial test is reactive, and confirmatory testing is arranged\naccording to the local pathway.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest working pattern?","options":["HIV-associated disease","Pulmonary TB with unrelated oral ulcers","Inhaled-steroid oral thrush with asthma","Oral leukoplakia with chronic bronchitis"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Weight loss, chronic constitutional symptoms, oral candidiasis, possible opportunistic respiratory\ndisease and violaceous lesions suggest HIV-associated immunosuppression with opportunistic or HIV-associated\ndisease. Asthma does not explain scrapeable oral plaques or violaceous lesions. The revised distractors are\nplausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full\nstem.","point":"Oral thrush plus weight loss and chronic cough should trigger HIV/immunosuppression reasoning.","skill":"Recognition"},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the oral lesion most likely to be?","options":["Aphthous ulceration","Oral candidiasis","Oral hairy leukoplakia","Kaposi sarcoma"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Thick white plaques that can be scraped away to reveal an erythematous base are typical of\npseudomembranous oral candidiasis, also called oral thrush. Aphthous ulcers are discrete painful ulcers, not curdy\nplaques. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they\nare less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Scrapeable curdy white plaques with red base = oral candidiasis.","skill":"Oral-lesion recognition"},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which feature best supports candidiasis?","options":["Non-scrapeable corrugated lateral tongue plaque","Firm violaceous palatal plaque","Scrapeable white plaque with erythematous base","Grouped painful vesicles on the lip"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Pseudomembranous candidiasis forms superficial grey-white or curdy plaques that can be removed,\nexposing an inflamed erythematous surface. A non-scrapeable patch suggests leukoplakia-type reasoning. The\nrevised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less\ndirectly supported by the full stem.","point":"\"Scrapeable\" is a key oral-thrush clue.","skill":"Discriminating feature"},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which organism is most classically linked?","options":["Epstein-Barr virus","Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Human herpesvirus 8","Candida albicans"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Oral candidiasis is classically linked to Candida albicans. It is a commensal yeast that can become\npathogenic when local or systemic host defences are disrupted. The revised distractors are plausible competing\ndiagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Oral thrush is commonly Candida albicans overgrowth in a vulnerable host.","skill":"Microbiology linkage"},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Which mechanism best explains oral thrush here?","options":["Impaired mucosal host defence","IgE-mediated mast-cell activation","HHV-8 vascular proliferation","Rifampicin-resistant mycobacterial infection"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Candida may be part of normal flora. It becomes pathogenic when host defences or normal microbial\nbalance are disrupted, such as in HIV/AIDS, steroid exposure, antibiotics, diabetes, neutropenia or other\nimmunosuppressed states. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or\nmanagement roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Candidiasis is often endogenous infection after host defences are impaired.","skill":"Applied microbiology"},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"Which symptom suggests oesophageal candidiasis?","options":["Retrosternal burning after meals","Painful swallowing","Painless cervical nodes","Hoarse voice with lip swelling"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Dysphagia or odynophagia in a patient with oral candidiasis raises concern for oesophageal\ncandidiasis. That needs escalation and proper assessment rather than treating it as a minor mouth problem. The\nrevised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less\ndirectly supported by the full stem.","point":"Thrush plus odynophagia suggests oesophageal extension.","skill":"Complication recognition"},{"number":7,"type":"SBA","q":"What are the violaceous plaques most concerning for?","options":["Traumatic bruising from thrombocytopenia","Mucosal candidiasis extension","Kaposi sarcoma","Psoriatic plaque disease"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Violaceous plaques in an HIV-associated context are concerning for Kaposi sarcoma, an\nHHV-8-associated vascular neoplasm. Oral, cutaneous and pulmonary involvement may occur. The revised\ndistractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly\nsupported by the full stem.","point":"Violaceous lesions in HIV should raise concern for Kaposi sarcoma.","skill":"HIV-associated lesion recognition"},{"number":8,"type":"SBA","q":"Which mechanism best fits Kaposi sarcoma?","options":["EBV-driven epithelial hyperkeratosis","Candida mucosal overgrowth","HIV-associated platelet destruction","HHV-8-associated vascular tumour"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Kaposi sarcoma is an HHV-8-associated vascular neoplasm. It is conceptually different from\nopportunistic infection, although it occurs in the setting of HIV-associated immunosuppression. The revised\ndistractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly\nsupported by the full stem.","point":"Kaposi sarcoma is a vascular neoplasm linked to HHV-8, not a fungal infection.","skill":"Tumour mechanism"},{"number":9,"type":"SBA","q":"A biopsy report from one violaceous lesion states:\n Finding Report\n Cell pattern Spindle-cell proliferation\n Vascular spaces Slit-like spaces present\n Lesion type Vascular lesion\n Clinical context HIV reactive test\n\nWhich interpretation is best?","options":["Kaposi sarcoma","Bacillary angiomatosis","Tuberculous granuloma","Candidal mucositis"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Spindle-cell proliferation with slit-like vascular spaces in an HIV-associated violaceous lesion\nsupports Kaposi sarcoma. Oral candidiasis is a mucosal fungal infection with yeast/pseudohyphae. The revised\ndistractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly\nsupported by the full stem.","point":"KS histology is a vascular spindle-cell lesion with slit-like spaces.","skill":"Pathology/data interpretation"},{"number":10,"type":"SBA","q":"Which respiratory diagnosis is concerning in advanced HIV?","options":["Bacterial lobar pneumonia","Pneumocystis pneumonia","Pulmonary tuberculosis","Asthma exacerbation"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"In HIV-associated immunosuppression, subacute dry cough, exertional dyspnoea and hypoxaemia\nraise concern for opportunistic pneumonia such as Pneumocystis pneumonia. Exact diagnostic and treatment\nprotocols are not being tested here. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or\nmanagement roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Advanced HIV plus dry cough and hypoxaemia should trigger opportunistic pneumonia reasoning.","skill":"Opportunistic respiratory disease recognition"},{"number":11,"type":"SBA","q":"Which feature keeps pulmonary TB in the differential?","options":["Chronic cough with weight loss","Scrapeable oral plaques","Violaceous palatal lesions","Dry cough with exertional desaturation"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Chronic cough, weight loss, fever, night sweats, TB contact risk and upper-zone chest findings would\nkeep pulmonary TB high in the differential. HIV increases TB risk, so not every HIV-associated cough should be\nlabelled PCP. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but\nthey are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"HIV cough differential includes TB, bacterial pneumonia, PCP and Kaposi involvement.","skill":"Differential discrimination"},{"number":12,"type":"SBA","q":"Which action is safest after a reactive HIV test?","options":["Treat opportunistic disease first, then link later","Link to HIV care","Repeat the reactive test at a later visit only","Begin partner notification by clinic disclosure"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"A reactive HIV test requires confirmation according to the testing pathway, counselling,\nconfidentiality, assessment for opportunistic disease and linkage to HIV care. The immediate exam-level principle\nis safe linkage and integrated assessment, not independent ART-regimen selection. The revised distractors are\nplausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full\nstem.","point":"HIV diagnosis requires linkage to care, not just telling the patient the result.","skill":"Patient safety / systems care"},{"number":13,"type":"SBA","q":"Which counselling principle is most important?","options":["Non-directive disclosure support","Routine family counselling first","Confidential care","Immediate workplace notification"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"HIV testing and counselling require confidentiality, consent, non-judgemental communication and\nlinkage to ongoing care. Disclosure and partner notification need careful, patient-centred handling within legal and\nethical boundaries. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles,\nbut they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"HIV care must protect confidentiality while supporting safe linkage and partner-care processes.","skill":"Ethics / communication"},{"number":14,"type":"SBA","q":"Which finding needs urgent escalation?","options":["Painful oral plaques","Violaceous skin lesions","Chronic weight loss","Hypoxaemia with tachypnoea"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Hypoxaemia and tachypnoea in a patient with suspected HIV-associated pulmonary disease indicate\nrespiratory danger. This needs urgent supervised assessment and escalation. The revised distractors are plausible\ncompeting diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Opportunistic disease becomes urgent when respiratory compromise appears.","skill":"Danger recognition"},{"number":15,"type":"VSAQ","q":"Which fungus commonly causes oral thrush?","options":[],"answer":"Candida albicans","accepted":["Candida species","Candida"],"explain":"Candida albicans is a common cause of oral candidiasis, especially when host defences or normal\nflora are disrupted.","point":"Oral thrush is usually Candida overgrowth in a vulnerable host.","skill":"Microbiology terminology"},{"number":16,"type":"VSAQ","q":"Which virus is associated with Kaposi sarcoma?","options":[],"answer":"HHV-8","accepted":["Human herpesvirus 8","Kaposi sarcoma-associated herpesvirus"],"explain":"Kaposi sarcoma is associated with HHV-8 and is especially important in HIV-associated\nimmunosuppression.","point":"Kaposi sarcoma = HHV-8-associated vascular neoplasm.","skill":"Pathology terminology"}],"source":"Student Tier 1 Teaching Bank"},{"id":2,"module":"BCMH","title":"Sepsis","scenario":"A 58-year-old man presents to a district emergency unit with 2 days of fever, rigors, dysuria and worsening left\nflank pain. He has felt progressively weaker and has vomited twice today. His wife says he has been drinking very\nlittle.\nHe has type 2 diabetes and hypertension. He has no known chronic kidney disease. On arrival: temperature 38.9\nC, HR 112/min, BP 106/68 mmHg, RR 24/min, SpO2 96% room air. He is alert but tired, with dry mucous\nmembranes and left renal angle tenderness. Urine dipstick shows leukocytes and nitrites. A urine culture and blood\ncultures are sent.\nOver the next hour, he becomes more confused and passes very little urine.\n\nQuestions and teaching explanations","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest working diagnosis at this stage?","options":["Acute pyelonephritis without organ dysfunction","Sepsis from a suspected urinary source","Hypovolaemic AKI from vomiting alone","Viral febrile illness with incidental urinary symptoms"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"He has a likely urinary infection source plus evolving organ dysfunction shown by new confusion and\nreduced urine output. Sepsis is suspected infection with organ dysfunction due to a dysregulated host response,\nnot just fever plus abnormal observations.","point":"The shift from infection to sepsis occurs when organ dysfunction appears.","skill":"Recognition"},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which finding most strongly indicates organ dysfunction rather than simple fever?","options":["Temperature of 38.9 C","Left renal angle tenderness","Leukocytes and nitrites on urine dipstick","New confusion"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"New confusion is a marker of brain dysfunction in suspected infection. Fever, flank pain and urine\ndipstick support infection/source but not organ dysfunction by themselves.","point":"In suspected infection, altered mental status is a danger marker until proven otherwise.","skill":"Discriminating features"},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which mechanism best explains the organ dysfunction in sepsis?","options":["Ascending infection limited to the renal pelvis","Fever-driven tachycardia with preserved perfusion","Dysregulated host response causing endothelial dysfunction and hypoperfusion","Positive blood culture as the required mechanism of sepsis"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Sepsis results from a dysregulated host response to infection. Inflammatory/ endothelial dysfunction\ncan cause vasodilation, capillary leak, microcirculatory dysfunction, impaired tissue perfusion and organ\ndysfunction. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but\nthey are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Sepsis is a host-response and organ-dysfunction syndrome, not simply infection in the blood.","skill":"Applied mechanism / pathophysiology"},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which data-assessment bundle best matches the clinical problem?","options":["FBC, U&E/creatinine, lactate, blood and urine cultures","Urine dipstick, urine culture and renal ultrasound only","Temperature chart, CRP and repeat urine dipstick only","ECG, troponin, BNP and chest-pain assessment"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"This bundle assesses infection/source, renal organ dysfunction and hypoperfusion risk. It is a\nfocused bundle for suspected sepsis with organ dysfunction.","point":"In suspected sepsis, assess both source and organ function.","skill":"Investigation reasoning"},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Repeat data show temperature 39.2 C, HR 126/min, BP 92/58 mmHg, RR 30/min, confusion, minimal urine, rising\ncreatinine and raised lactate. What is the safest interpretation and implication?","options":["Acute pyelonephritis only; source-focused review is sufficient","Dehydration-related pre-renal AKI only; observe before escalation","Obstructive uropathy alone; prioritise renal imaging only","Sepsis with organ dysfunction; urgent escalation is needed"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"The pattern is suspected infection with worsening instability and organ dysfunction: confusion,\noliguria, rising creatinine and raised lactate. This is pattern recognition, not exact lactate- threshold recall.","point":"Falling BP, tachypnoea, confusion, oliguria, rising creatinine and raised lactate are sepsis danger\nsignals.","skill":"Data interpretation / pattern recognition"},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"Thirty minutes later, he becomes more drowsy. His hands are cool, pulse thready and urine output remains\nminimal. Which interpretation is safest?","options":["Expected rigors from febrile urinary infection","Worsening sepsis with circulatory hypoperfusion","Isolated delirium from poor sleep and fatigue","Mild dehydration suitable for low-acuity observation"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Worsening mental state, cool peripheries, thready pulse and persistent oliguria indicate critical\ndeterioration with impaired perfusion in suspected infection.","point":"Do not wait for formal threshold labels before recognising hypoperfusion.","skill":"Safety / danger recognition"},{"number":7,"type":"SBA","q":"Which action best reflects safe care at this point?","options":["Start oral antibiotics and review once cultures return","Repeat observations before escalating because BP is not profoundly low","Escalate urgently through a sepsis-capable emergency pathway","Independently select the full antimicrobial, fluid and vasopressor plan"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Suspected infection with organ dysfunction and circulatory hypoperfusion requires urgent\nsenior/emergency escalation through an appropriate sepsis-capable pathway. The student is not expected to\nindependently choose antimicrobial regimens, fluid volumes, vasopressors or ICU criteria.","point":"In sepsis with organ dysfunction, waiting for perfect diagnostic certainty is unsafe.","skill":"Patient safety / systems-level escalation"},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What renal syndrome is suggested by rising creatinine with reduced urine output in this septic patient?","options":[],"answer":"Acute kidney injury / AKI","accepted":[],"explain":"Rising creatinine with reduced urine output indicates AKI in this context. In sepsis, AKI may result\nfrom renal hypoperfusion, inflammatory injury and systemic circulatory dysfunction.","point":"AKI in sepsis is part of the organ-dysfunction pattern, not a separate minor lab abnormality.","skill":"Data interpretation / renal integration"}],"source":"Student Tier 1 Teaching Bank"},{"id":17,"module":"BCMH","title":"Neonatal sepsis","scenario":"A 5-day-old boy is brought to the emergency department by his mother because he has been feeding poorly since\nthe morning. He is sleepier than usual and has had two episodes where he stopped breathing for a few seconds\nbefore becoming floppy and pale. He was born at term by vaginal delivery. His mother reports that she had a fever\nduring labour but was discharged after delivery. She is unsure whether any cultures were taken. She says the baby\nhad been feeding well until today. On examination, the neonate looks unwell and lethargic.\n Finding Result\n Temperature 35.8 C\n Heart rate 178/min\n Respiratory rate Irregular, with pauses\n Oxygen saturation 91% room air\n Capillary refill Delayed\n Peripheral temperature Cool hands and feet\n\nThe anterior fontanelle is slightly full. He has reduced spontaneous movement and weak suck. No rash is seen.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest working diagnosis?","options":["Physiological reflux with feeding fatigue","Neonatal hypoglycaemia alone","Neonatal sepsis","Benign periodic breathing of the newborn"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Poor feeding, lethargy, apnoeic episodes, temperature instability, tachycardia, poor perfusion and\nreduced responsiveness in a neonate are dangerous. Neonatal sepsis must be assumed until proven otherwise.\nThe revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less\ndirectly supported by the full stem.","point":"In a neonate, poor feeding and lethargy are danger signs, not vague symptoms.","skill":"Recognition"},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which feature most clearly shows immediate danger?","options":["Apnoeic episodes","Maternal fever during labour","Term vaginal delivery","Absence of visible rash"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Apnoea in a neonate is a major danger sign. In the context of poor feeding, lethargy and poor\nperfusion, it strongly supports serious illness requiring urgent escalation. The revised distractors are plausible\ncompeting diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Neonatal sepsis may present subtly, but apnoea is never a benign sign.","skill":"Safety/risk prioritisation"},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which organism pathway is strongly supported here?","options":["Maternal Candida infection in a preterm intensive-care setting","Postnatal viral bronchiolitis","Food-borne Salmonella gastroenteritis","Group B Streptococcus invasive disease"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Group B Streptococcus can colonise the maternal genital or gastrointestinal tract and cause invasive\nneonatal infection. The verified teaching links GBS to neonatal pneumonia, bacteraemia and meningitis pathways.\nThe revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less\ndirectly supported by the full stem.","point":"Maternal GBS colonisation can lead to invasive neonatal infection.","skill":"Microbiology linkage"},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which mechanism best explains sepsis?","options":["Local infection without systemic dysfunction","Dysregulated host response causing organ dysfunction","Hypoglycaemia-induced lethargy alone","Immature respiratory control causing benign apnoea"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Sepsis is life-threatening organ dysfunction caused by a dysregulated host response to infection. In\nthis neonate, respiratory pauses, poor perfusion and lethargy suggest systemic dysfunction rather than\nuncomplicated infection. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management\nroles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Sepsis is infection plus organ dysfunction from a dysregulated host response.","skill":"Applied pathophysiology"},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Which finding best supports poor perfusion?","options":["Slightly full fontanelle","Weak suck reflex","Delayed capillary refill","Low oxygen saturation"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Delayed capillary refill is a bedside sign of impaired perfusion. In neonatal sepsis, shock may be\nrecognised through perfusion signs, temperature difference, heart rate, respiratory pattern, urine output and mental\nstatus. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are\nless directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Neonatal shock recognition depends on perfusion markers, not only blood pressure.","skill":"Perfusion assessment"},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"Which presentation suggests early invasive GBS disease?","options":["Pneumonia and bacteraemia","Bronchiolitis with isolated wheeze","Gastroenteritis with isolated diarrhoea","Localised conjunctivitis only"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Early neonatal GBS disease commonly presents with pneumonia and bacteraemia. The exact day\ncut-offs are not being tested here; the key idea is that early invasive neonatal disease often affects the lungs and\nbloodstream. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but\nthey are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Early GBS disease commonly involves pneumonia and bloodstream infection.","skill":"Organism-specific pattern"},{"number":7,"type":"SBA","q":"CSF shows raised white cells, neutrophil predominance, raised protein and low glucose. Which interpretation is\nbest?","options":["Early viral meningitis without bacterial features","Traumatic tap without infection","Normal neonatal CSF variation","Bacterial meningitis pattern"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Neutrophilic pleocytosis, raised protein and low glucose support bacterial meningitis. In this clinical\ncontext, neonatal sepsis with meningitis must be treated as time-critical. The revised distractors are plausible\ncompeting diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Neutrophils + high protein + low glucose in CSF supports bacterial meningitis.","skill":"CSF/data interpretation"},{"number":8,"type":"SBA","q":"Which diagnosis explains the full picture best?","options":["Neonatal hypoglycaemia alone","Neonatal meningitis","Physiological jaundice with poor feeding","Uncomplicated bronchiolitis"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Poor feeding, lethargy, apnoea, full fontanelle and a bacterial CSF pattern strongly support neonatal\nmeningitis as part of a neonatal sepsis presentation. Reflux and nasal congestion do not explain CSF abnormalities\nor poor perfusion. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles,\nbut they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Neonatal meningitis can present with non-specific systemic signs, not just obvious neck stiffness.","skill":"Integrated diagnosis"},{"number":9,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest immediate action?","options":["Observe after a monitored feed if oxygen improves","Arrange urgent outpatient paediatric review","Urgent neonatal escalation","Wait for CSF culture confirmation before treatment"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"This is an unwell neonate with apnoea, poor perfusion and possible meningitis. The immediate\npriority is urgent senior neonatal or emergency escalation. The revised distractors are plausible competing\ndiagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Neonatal sepsis is time-critical; do not wait for deterioration to become dramatic.","skill":"Patient safety/escalation"},{"number":10,"type":"SBA","q":"Which treatment principle is most appropriate?","options":["Empiric antimicrobial cover","Supportive fluids and oxygen only while awaiting cultures","Culture-confirmed narrow therapy as the initial step","Antiviral bronchiolitis pathway"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Suspected neonatal sepsis or meningitis requires prompt empiric antimicrobial therapy under\nsenior/protocol guidance. The cluster deliberately tests the principle, not specific drug combinations or doses. The\nrevised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less\ndirectly supported by the full stem.","point":"In suspected neonatal sepsis, antimicrobials are time-critical; exact regimens are protocol- level.","skill":"Pharmacology principle"},{"number":11,"type":"SBA","q":"Why are cultures important?","options":["Prove infection before treatment starts","Guide targeted therapy","Replace clinical assessment of sepsis","Determine whether apnoea is benign"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Blood and CSF cultures can identify the organism and guide later targeted therapy. They are\nimportant, but the baby's emergency care should not be delayed while waiting for results. The revised distractors\nare plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the\nfull stem.","point":"Cultures guide later therapy; they do not replace urgent clinical action.","skill":"Investigation reasoning"},{"number":12,"type":"SBA","q":"Which statement is safest?","options":["Fever must be present before neonatal sepsis is considered","Absence of rash makes invasive infection unlikely","Term delivery excludes early-onset infection","Apnoea may be sepsis"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Neonatal sepsis may present with temperature instability, poor feeding, lethargy, respiratory pauses\nor shock signs. Fever is not always present; hypothermia can also be dangerous. The revised distractors are\nplausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full\nstem.","point":"Neonates may be septic without fever.","skill":"Clinical reasoning trap"},{"number":13,"type":"SBA","q":"Which pattern suggests septic shock?","options":["Slightly full fontanelle","Delayed capillary refill","Weak suck alone","Isolated low temperature after environmental exposure"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Septic shock involves impaired perfusion and organ dysfunction. Delayed capillary refill supports\npoor perfusion, especially when combined with cool peripheries, tachycardia, altered responsiveness, oliguria or\nmetabolic acidosis. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles,\nbut they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"In neonates, perfusion markers are central to shock recognition.","skill":"Shock recognition"},{"number":14,"type":"VSAQ","q":"Which maternal organism can cause neonatal pneumonia, bacteraemia and meningitis?","options":[],"answer":"Group B Streptococcus","accepted":["Group B Streptococcus","Streptococcus agalactiae","GBS"],"explain":"Group B Streptococcus colonises the maternal genital and gastrointestinal tract and can cause\ninvasive neonatal infection.","point":"GBS is a major neonatal sepsis and meningitis organism.","skill":"Microbiology terminology"},{"number":15,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What CSF glucose pattern supports bacterial meningitis?","options":[],"answer":"Low glucose","accepted":["Low glucose","reduced CSF glucose"],"explain":"Bacterial meningitis classically produces low CSF glucose with raised protein and neutrophilic\npleocytosis.","point":"Low CSF glucose is a bacterial meningitis clue.","skill":"CSF terminology"}],"source":"Student Tier 1 Teaching Bank"},{"id":12,"module":"CVS","title":"Atrial fibrillation","scenario":"A 69-year-old woman presents with sudden awareness of a fast, irregular heartbeat that started 6 hours ago while\nresting. She feels short of breath when walking across the room and mildly light-headed when standing. She has\nhypertension and type 2 diabetes. She had a transient episode of right arm weakness 1 year ago that resolved\nbefore hospital assessment. She is not on anticoagulation. She takes amlodipine and metformin. She has no chest\npain. Her pulse is irregularly irregular. There are no basal crackles and no focal neurological deficit.\n Finding Result\n Blood pressure 118/76 mmHg\n Heart rate 142/min, irregular\n Respiratory rate 22/min\n Oxygen saturation 96% room air\n Temperature 36.8 C\n\nQuestions and teaching explanations","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the most likely rhythm diagnosis?","options":["Sinus tachycardia with frequent atrial ectopics","Atrial fibrillation","Atrial flutter with variable AV conduction","Paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"An irregularly irregular pulse with a fast ventricular rate strongly suggests AF before ECG\nconfirmation.","point":"Irregularly irregular pulse = AF until proven otherwise.","skill":"Recognition"},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which ECG feature would best support atrial fibrillation?","options":["Regular P wave before every QRS complex","Saw-tooth flutter waves with organised atrial activity","AV dissociation with independent atrial and ventricular rhythms","Absent distinct P waves with irregularly irregular RR intervals"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"AF classically shows absent or indiscernible P waves with irregularly irregular RR intervals.","point":"AF = absent P waves plus irregularly irregular ventricular response.","skill":"ECG pattern recognition"},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which mechanism best explains the irregular pulse?","options":["Chaotic atrial impulses reaching the AV node unpredictably","A single atrial re-entry circuit with organised flutter waves","Delayed AV nodal conduction with preserved sinus rhythm","Independent atrial and ventricular pacemaker activity"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Disorganised atrial impulses reach the AV node unpredictably, producing an irregularly irregular\nventricular response.","point":"AF is chaotic atrial activity with irregular AV conduction.","skill":"Applied electrophysiology"},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"ECG report: irregularly irregular rhythm, absent P waves, narrow QRS complexes, fast rate and no ST elevation.\nWhich interpretation is best?","options":["Sinus tachycardia with atrial ectopy","Atrial flutter with variable block","Atrial fibrillation","Monomorphic ventricular tachycardia"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Irregularly irregular rhythm with absent P waves and narrow QRS complexes strongly supports AF.","point":"AF ECG pattern = irregularly irregular rhythm with absent P waves.","skill":"Formal ECG/data interpretation"},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Why is AF clinically important beyond palpitations?","options":["Pulmonary embolism from leg-vein thrombosis","Embolic stroke","Syncope from fixed AV block","Myocardial infarction from coronary vasospasm"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Ineffective atrial contraction causes blood stasis, especially in the left atrium/appendage, increasing\nthromboembolic stroke risk.","point":"AF is dangerous because of stroke risk and haemodynamic compromise, not just symptoms.","skill":"Complication reasoning"},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"Which drug best fits rate-control reasoning in AF?","options":["Aspirin","Adenosine","Furosemide","Metoprolol"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Metoprolol is a beta-blocker. Beta-blockers can slow AV nodal conduction and reduce ventricular\nrate under supervised care.","point":"Rate-control drugs target AV nodal conduction.","skill":"Pharmacology role recognition"},{"number":7,"type":"SBA","q":"Which change would make this rhythm immediately unstable?","options":["Hypotension","Persistent palpitations with preserved blood pressure","Anxiety after seeing the monitor","Mild hypertension during tachycardia"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Hypotension suggests haemodynamic compromise. Other instability features include shock signs,\npulmonary oedema, altered mental state, severe ischaemic chest pain or collapse.","point":"In arrhythmias, first decide stable or unstable.","skill":"Safety / danger recognition"},{"number":8,"type":"SBA","q":"An hour later she develops central chest pressure, sweating and a BP of 82/50 mmHg. What is safest?","options":["Give outpatient rate-control advice and review the next day","Wait for troponin results before calling for senior help","Urgent escalation","Start routine anticoagulation work-up as the first priority"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"AF with hypotension and chest pressure is unstable. The safe action is urgent senior/emergency\nescalation.","point":"Unstable arrhythmia = call for help now.","skill":"Patient safety / escalation"},{"number":9,"type":"SBA","q":"Which rhythm is most consistent with a regular wide-complex tachycardia?","options":["Atrial fibrillation with narrow QRS complexes","Ventricular tachycardia","Atrial flutter with narrow QRS complexes","Complete heart block with slow escape rhythm"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"VT is a dangerous ventricular rhythm that often appears as a regular wide-complex tachycardia.\nTreat it as dangerous until senior help arrives.","point":"Regular wide-complex tachycardia should be treated as dangerous.","skill":"Dangerous rhythm discrimination"},{"number":10,"type":"SBA","q":"Which rhythm is most consistent with AV dissociation?","options":["Atrial flutter with fixed block","Sinus tachycardia with first-degree AV delay","Complete heart block","Atrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular response"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Complete heart block means atrial and ventricular activity are independent. It is a high-risk\nbradyarrhythmia when symptomatic or unstable.","point":"Complete heart block = AV dissociation.","skill":"Bradyarrhythmia recognition"},{"number":11,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What bedside pulse description is classically associated with atrial fibrillation?","options":[],"answer":"Irregularly irregular","accepted":[],"explain":"AF causes an irregularly irregular rhythm because chaotic atrial activity reaches the AV node\nunpredictably.","point":"Irregularly irregular is the classic AF pulse description.","skill":"Clinical sign terminology"},{"number":12,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What cardiac chamber activity is disorganised in atrial fibrillation?","options":[],"answer":"Atrial activity","accepted":[],"explain":"In AF, atrial electrical activation is chaotic and disorganised, eliminating coordinated atrial\ncontraction.","point":"AF is primarily atrial electrical disorganisation.","skill":"Mechanism terminology"}],"source":"Student Tier 1 Teaching Bank"},{"id":32,"module":"CVS","title":"Decompensated heart failure","scenario":"A 67-year-old man presents to the emergency department with worsening shortness of breath. Over the past week\nhe has become progressively breathless when walking short distances. Last night he had to sleep propped up on\nthree pillows and woke suddenly gasping for air. His ankles have become swollen.\nHe has long-standing hypertension and had a myocardial infarction 4 years ago. He stopped taking his\nblood-pressure tablets because he \"felt fine.\" He has no known asthma. He has no fever, purulent sputum, pleuritic\nchest pain, haemoptysis, calf pain or recent surgery.\nOn examination, he is distressed and sitting upright.\n Finding Result\n Temperature 36.8 degrees C\n Heart rate 118/min\n Blood pressure 168/96 mmHg\n Respiratory rate 32/min\n Oxygen saturation 88% room air\n\nHe has raised jugular venous pressure, bilateral basal crackles, a displaced apex beat and bilateral pitting ankle\noedema. Heart sounds are difficult to hear clearly in the noisy emergency unit, but an extra low-pitched sound is\nsuspected. There is no focal bronchial breathing and no unilateral absent breath sounds.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest working diagnosis?","options":["Acute asthma exacerbation","Pulmonary embolism","Acute heart failure","Community-acquired pneumonia"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Progressive exertional dyspnoea, orthopnoea, paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea, raised JVP, basal\ncrackles, displaced apex beat and peripheral oedema strongly suggest acute decompensated heart failure with\npulmonary congestion. Asthma would more strongly suggest diffuse wheeze and reversible bronchospasm. The\nrevised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less\ndirectly supported by the full stem.","point":"Dyspnoea plus crackles plus raised JVP and oedema is heart failure until proven otherwise.","skill":"Recognition"},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which symptom best supports orthopnoea?","options":["Breathless on exertion","Wakes suddenly coughing after meals","Pain worse on inspiration","Breathlessness worse lying flat"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Orthopnoea means breathlessness that worsens when lying flat and improves when sitting upright. It\noccurs because lying flat increases venous return and pulmonary congestion in heart failure. The revised\ndistractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly\nsupported by the full stem.","point":"Orthopnoea is positional breathlessness from pulmonary congestion.","skill":"Symptom terminology"},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which mechanism best explains the basal crackles?","options":["Alveolar fluid accumulation","Bronchial smooth-muscle constriction","Focal alveolar inflammatory exudate","Pulmonary arterial obstruction"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Left-sided cardiac dysfunction raises pulmonary venous pressure. Fluid then moves into the\ninterstitium and alveoli, producing pulmonary oedema and crackles. The revised distractors are plausible\ncompeting diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Pulmonary oedema is fluid in the lung from raised pulmonary venous pressure.","skill":"Applied pathophysiology"},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which finding best supports systemic venous congestion?","options":["Peripheral cyanosis","Raised JVP","Diffuse expiratory wheeze","Focal bronchial breathing"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Raised jugular venous pressure reflects elevated right-sided filling pressure or systemic venous\ncongestion. In heart failure, it often appears with peripheral oedema, hepatomegaly or ascites. The revised\ndistractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly\nsupported by the full stem.","point":"Raised JVP is a congestion sign, not a respiratory-infection sign.","skill":"Examination interpretation"},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"A chest X-ray report returns:\n CXR feature Report\n Cardiac silhouette Enlarged\n Lung fields Bilateral interstitial oedema\n Septal lines Kerley B lines present\n Pleural spaces Small bilateral effusions\n Pneumothorax Absent\n\nWhich interpretation is best?","options":["Lobar consolidation pattern","COPD hyperinflation pattern","Pulmonary oedema pattern","Pneumothorax pattern"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Enlarged cardiac silhouette, interstitial oedema, Kerley B lines and bilateral pleural effusions support\nheart failure with pulmonary venous congestion. This fits the clinical picture of orthopnoea, crackles, raised JVP\nand oedema. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but\nthey are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Kerley B lines and bilateral effusions support pulmonary venous congestion.","skill":"CXR/data interpretation"},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"Which previous condition increases his risk?","options":["Long-standing asthma","Previous pulmonary embolism","Previous pneumonia admission","Prior myocardial infarction"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Prior myocardial infarction can damage myocardium and reduce contractile function. Hypertension\nalso increases afterload and contributes to cardiac remodelling and heart failure. The revised distractors are\nplausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full\nstem.","point":"IHD and hypertension are major heart-failure risk pathways.","skill":"Risk-factor reasoning"},{"number":7,"type":"SBA","q":"Which physical sign suggests chronic cardiac enlargement?","options":["Displaced apex beat","Raised JVP","Bilateral basal crackles","Peripheral pitting oedema"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"A displaced apex beat suggests cardiac enlargement, often left ventricular enlargement. In this case\nit supports chronic hypertensive/ischaemic cardiac disease underlying the acute decompensation. The revised\ndistractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly\nsupported by the full stem.","point":"Displaced apex beat is a structural cardiac clue.","skill":"Cardiovascular examination"},{"number":8,"type":"SBA","q":"Which alternative pattern would support pneumonia?","options":["Raised JVP with ankle oedema","Fever with focal crackles","Orthopnoea and PND","Bilateral pleural effusions with cardiomegaly"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Pneumonia is suggested by fever, purulent sputum, focal crackles, bronchial breathing, pleuritic pain\nor focal CXR consolidation. Heart failure more strongly gives bilateral crackles, raised JVP, orthopnoea and\noedema. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they\nare less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Focal infective signs push toward pneumonia; congestion signs push toward heart failure.","skill":"Differential discrimination"},{"number":9,"type":"SBA","q":"Which alternative pattern would support pulmonary embolism?","options":["Orthopnoea with ankle oedema","Kerley B lines on CXR","Pleuritic pain with haemoptysis","Raised cardiothoracic ratio"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Pulmonary embolism is suggested by acute pleuritic chest pain, haemoptysis, tachycardia,\nunexplained dyspnoea and DVT risk factors. It may have a relatively clear chest examination. The revised\ndistractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly\nsupported by the full stem.","point":"PE often gives pleuritic/thromboembolic clues rather than congestive signs.","skill":"Differential discrimination"},{"number":10,"type":"SBA","q":"Which rhythm problem can precipitate decompensation?","options":["Sinus tachycardia from anxiety","First-degree AV block","Occasional atrial ectopic beats","Atrial fibrillation"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Atrial fibrillation can precipitate or worsen heart failure by reducing atrial contribution to ventricular\nfilling and causing rapid ventricular rates. Arrhythmias are therefore important triggers of acute decompensation.\nThe revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less\ndirectly supported by the full stem.","point":"Arrhythmias can precipitate acute heart failure.","skill":"Trigger recognition"},{"number":11,"type":"SBA","q":"A short rhythm strip shows an irregularly irregular rhythm without consistent visible P waves.\nWhich interpretation is best?","options":["Atrial fibrillation","Sinus rhythm with frequent ectopics","Atrial flutter with fixed block","Complete heart block"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"An irregularly irregular rhythm without consistent P waves is the classic atrial fibrillation pattern. In a\npatient with heart failure, AF may worsen symptoms and haemodynamics. The revised distractors are plausible\ncompeting diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Irregularly irregular rhythm without P waves = atrial fibrillation pattern.","skill":"ECG/rhythm data interpretation"},{"number":12,"type":"SBA","q":"Which treatment principle targets fluid overload?","options":["Bronchodilator therapy","Diuretic therapy","Antiplatelet therapy","Antibiotic therapy"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"In fluid-overloaded heart failure, diuretic therapy is a core treatment principle because it reduces\ncongestion. This question tests the broad therapeutic principle, not a drug dose, route, monitoring schedule or full\nacute-heart-failure protocol. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or\nmanagement roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Diuretics target congestion; they are not a substitute for full assessment and escalation.","skill":"Pharmacology / treatment principle"},{"number":13,"type":"SBA","q":"Which change suggests cardiogenic shock?","options":["Wheeze with urticaria","Dysuria with fever","Cold limbs with oliguria","Pleuritic pain with calf swelling"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Cold clammy peripheries, oliguria, altered mental state and falling blood pressure suggest poor\ntissue perfusion from pump failure when occurring in a heart-failure context. This is cardiogenic deterioration. The\nrevised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less\ndirectly supported by the full stem.","point":"Heart failure becomes immediately dangerous when congestion is joined by hypoperfusion.","skill":"Shock danger recognition"},{"number":14,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest immediate action?","options":["Treat as uncomplicated bronchospasm and discharge after inhaler response","Start oral antibiotics only and review after culture results","Arrange routine outpatient echocardiography","Urgent senior escalation"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"This patient has hypoxaemic acute decompensated heart failure with pulmonary oedema and\npossible arrhythmic trigger. He needs urgent supervised assessment, oxygenation support, cardiac evaluation and\ndecongestion planning. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management\nroles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Pulmonary oedema with hypoxaemia is an emergency presentation.","skill":"Patient safety / escalation"},{"number":15,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What term describes breathlessness that is worse when lying flat?","options":[],"answer":"Orthopnoea","accepted":[],"explain":"Orthopnoea is positional breathlessness that worsens when lying flat and improves sitting upright. It\nis a classic symptom of pulmonary congestion in heart failure.","point":"Orthopnoea is a heart-failure clue, especially with PND and oedema.","skill":"Symptom terminology"},{"number":16,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What chest X-ray line pattern suggests interstitial pulmonary oedema?","options":[],"answer":"Kerley B lines","accepted":["Septal lines","interlobular septal lines"],"explain":"Kerley B lines reflect interlobular septal thickening and are a classic CXR clue to interstitial\npulmonary oedema or pulmonary venous congestion.","point":"Kerley B lines are a pulmonary-oedema data clue.","skill":"CXR terminology"}],"source":"Student Tier 1 Teaching Bank"},{"id":1,"module":"CVS","title":"Acute coronary syndrome","scenario":"A 63-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with 2 hours of central chest discomfort. She\ndescribes it as a heavy pressure behind the sternum, but says it also feels like bad indigestion in the upper\nabdomen. It began while walking uphill from a taxi rank and has not fully settled with rest.\nShe feels nauseated, short of breath and sweaty. She delayed coming to hospital because she thought it was\nreflux.\nShe has type 2 diabetes, hypertension and dyslipidaemia. She stopped smoking 5 years ago. Her older sister had\na myocardial infarction in her fifties. She is pale, clammy and uncomfortable. Her lungs are initially clear. Peripheral\npulses are equal. There is no reproducible chest wall tenderness. There is mild epigastric tenderness without\nguarding.\nInitial observations Finding Result Blood pressure 148/92 mmHg Heart rate 104/min Respiratory rate 22/min\nOxygen saturation 96% on room air Temperature 36.9 C\n\nQuestions and teaching explanations","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the most likely working syndrome at this stage?","options":["Gastro-oesophageal reflux disease with exertional dyspepsia","Panic-related chest tightness with hyperventilation","Acute coronary syndrome","Musculoskeletal chest wall pain without reproducible tenderness"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Persistent central pressure-like discomfort, exertional onset, nausea, sweating, dyspnoea and major\nvascular risk factors make ACS the safest working syndrome. Epigastric discomfort can occur in myocardial\nischaemia, especially in diabetes.","point":"ACS may present as chest pressure, epigastric discomfort, nausea, diaphoresis or dyspnoea; do not\naccept the patient label of indigestion at face value.","skill":"Recognition"},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which feature most strongly supports myocardial ischaemia rather than a lower-risk non- cardiac cause?","options":["Persistent pressure discomfort with exertional onset","Mild epigastric tenderness","Normal oxygen saturation on room air","Absence of fever or productive cough"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Persistent central pressure discomfort is the strongest listed discriminator. The stem supplies the\nrisk-factor and autonomic context; the option itself does not need to bundle every clue. The revised distractors are\nplausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full\nstem.","point":"Normal oxygen saturation and absence of fever do not make chest pain safe.","skill":"Discriminating features"},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which mechanism best explains the acute myocardial injury pathway in this presentation?","options":["Fixed stable coronary narrowing causing demand-limited angina only","Transient coronary vasospasm without myocardial injury","Increased oxygen demand from sinus tachycardia alone","Plaque disruption with coronary thrombosis"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"ACS is commonly caused by atherosclerotic plaque disruption with superimposed thrombosis,\nacutely reducing coronary blood flow.","point":"ACS is not simply narrow arteries; the acute event is plaque disruption plus thrombus formation.","skill":"Applied pathology / mechanism"},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Her first troponin is not elevated. Pain began less than 2 hours before arrival and the ECG remains concerning.\nWhich interpretation is safest?","options":["Exclude ACS if oxygen saturation remains normal","Interpret with timing and repeat values because early results may lag injury","Treat troponin separately from ECG because they answer unrelated questions","Attribute any later rise to age-related delayed clearance unless chest pain persists\nolder patients"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"A single early troponin may not yet show a measurable rise. Troponin must be interpreted with\nsymptom timing, ECG findings and serial change.","point":"One early non-elevated troponin does not safely exclude evolving myocardial injury when the clinical\npicture and ECG are concerning.","skill":"Investigation reasoning"},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"ECG: ST elevation in V2-V4, no diffuse ST elevation. Troponin trend: 0 h not elevated, 3 h elevated, 6 h further\nincreased. What is the best interpretation and implication?","options":["Lateral myocardial injury; urgent ACS-capable escalation is needed","Acute pericarditis pattern; anti-inflammatory-focused care is the priority","Anterior myocardial injury; urgent ACS-capable escalation is needed","Stable exertional angina; elective outpatient risk stratification is appropriate"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"V2-V4 broadly localise to the anterior territory. Localised ST elevation plus a rising troponin in the\ncorrect clinical context supports acute anterior myocardial injury and urgent ACS-capable escalation.","point":"Anterior territory is broadly V1-V4; a rising troponin trend supports evolving myocardial injury in the right\nclinical context.","skill":"Data interpretation / ECG and biomarker integration"},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"Thirty minutes later, she becomes more drowsy and clammy. BP falls from 148/92 to 88/60 mmHg, HR rises from\n104 to 126/min, RR rises from 22 to 28/min, SpO2 changes from 96% to 94% on room air, chest examination\nchanges from clear to fine basal crackles, and urine output is minimal since arrival. Which interpretation is safest?","options":["Pain-related sympathetic activation with preserved perfusion","Mild dehydration with compensatory tachycardia","Respiratory stability because oxygen saturation remains near normal","Cardiogenic deterioration must be considered"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"In ACS, falling blood pressure, tachycardia, drowsiness, oliguria and new basal crackles are danger\nsigns. This pattern suggests impaired cardiac output and possible evolving cardiogenic shock or acute pump\nfailure.","point":"In ACS, hypotension plus poor perfusion plus pulmonary crackles is cardiogenic deterioration until\nproven otherwise.","skill":"Safety / danger recognition"},{"number":7,"type":"SBA","q":"Which action best reflects safe system-level care at this point?","options":["Escalate urgently through an ACS-capable pathway","Continue observation until serial troponins are complete","Choose the definitive reperfusion strategy independently","Admit for low-acuity monitoring and routine cardiology review"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Suspected ACS with high-risk ECG findings, rising troponin and clinical deterioration requires urgent\nescalation through the appropriate ACS system. The GEMP 2 task is not independent PCI-versus- thrombolysis\nselection or a full specialist regimen.","point":"The safe answer is urgent system activation, not delayed observation and not autonomous specialist\ndecision-making.","skill":"Patient safety / health systems escalation"},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What cellular process causes irreversible myocardial injury after prolonged ischaemia?","options":[],"answer":"Necrosis / coagulative necrosis","accepted":["Coagulative necrosis"],"explain":"Prolonged ischaemia causes irreversible cardiomyocyte injury and necrosis. Troponin rises because\ndamaged myocardial cells release intracellular proteins into the blood.","point":"Troponin is a marker of myocardial injury/necrosis, not a marker of chest- pain severity.","skill":"Pathology mechanism"}],"source":"Student Tier 1 Teaching Bank"},{"id":13,"module":"CVS","title":"Pulmonary embolism","scenario":"A 52-year-old woman presents with sudden shortness of breath and sharp right-sided chest pain that is worse\nwhen she breathes in deeply. Ten days ago, she had surgery for a fractured ankle and has been mostly immobile\nat home. Since yesterday, her left calf has been painful and swollen. She has no fever, no productive cough and no\nknown asthma. She has hypertension and obesity. Chest examination is largely clear. The trachea is central. There\nare no focal crackles or bronchial breath sounds. The left calf is swollen and tender.\n Finding Result\n Temperature 36.9 C\n Heart rate 122/min\n Blood pressure 108/70 mmHg\n Respiratory rate 30/min\n Oxygen saturation 89% room air\n\nQuestions and teaching explanations","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest working diagnosis?","options":["Pulmonary embolism","Community-acquired pneumonia","Panic-related hyperventilation","Acute asthma exacerbation"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Sudden dyspnoea, pleuritic chest pain, hypoxia, tachycardia, recent immobility and painful swollen\ncalf strongly suggest PE.","point":"Sudden dyspnoea + pleuritic pain + DVT risk = PE until proven otherwise.","skill":"Recognition"},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which feature most strongly supports venous thromboembolism risk?","options":["Clear chest examination","Normal temperature","Recent immobilisation after surgery","Pleuritic chest pain"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Immobility causes venous stasis, one arm of Virchow triad, increasing DVT risk and therefore PE\nrisk.","point":"Immobilisation increases PE risk by promoting venous stasis.","skill":"Risk-factor reasoning"},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which mechanism best explains this presentation?","options":["Alveolar consolidation causing shunt physiology","Bronchial smooth-muscle constriction","Pleural air causing lung collapse","DVT embolisation to pulmonary arteries"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"A thrombus can form in deep leg veins, detach and lodge in pulmonary arteries, causing vascular\nobstruction, V/Q mismatch, hypoxia and pleuritic pain.","point":"PE is usually thrombus travelling from the venous system to the pulmonary circulation.","skill":"Applied pathology"},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which part of Virchow triad is most directly represented by immobility?","options":["Endothelial injury","Venous stasis","Hypercoagulability","Airway inflammation"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Virchow triad consists of stasis, endothelial injury and hypercoagulability. Immobility mainly produces\nstasis.","point":"Virchow triad: stasis, endothelial injury, hypercoagulability.","skill":"Mechanism terminology"},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Initial data show hypoxia, sinus tachycardia, no CXR consolidation, proximal DVT on leg ultrasound and non-\nelevated troponin. Which interpretation is best?","options":["PE remains likely","ACS with primary myocardial injury is most likely","Pneumonia is confirmed despite absent consolidation","Asthma exacerbation is confirmed by clear chest examination"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Hypoxia, sinus tachycardia, clear/non-consolidating CXR and proven proximal DVT strongly support\nPE in this clinical context.","point":"PE can have a relatively clear chest examination and non-diagnostic CXR.","skill":"Data interpretation"},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"Which finding would suggest massive PE with obstructive shock?","options":["Calf tenderness","Pleuritic pain","Sinus tachycardia","Hypotension"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Hypotension suggests haemodynamic compromise. A large PE can acutely strain the right heart and\nreduce left-sided filling, producing obstructive shock.","point":"PE becomes immediately life-threatening when obstruction causes shock physiology.","skill":"Danger recognition"},{"number":7,"type":"SBA","q":"Which differential is most suggested by fever, sputum and focal crackles?","options":["Pulmonary embolism","Panic hyperventilation","Pneumonia","Acute pneumothorax"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Fever, purulent sputum and focal crackles point toward an infective consolidation pattern rather than\na vascular obstruction pattern.","point":"Pneumonia is an infective air-space pattern; PE is vascular obstruction.","skill":"Differential discrimination"},{"number":8,"type":"SBA","q":"Which finding most supports pneumothorax instead of PE?","options":["Painful swollen calf","Unilateral absent breath sounds","Recent immobilisation","Sinus tachycardia"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Pneumothorax reduces ventilation on the affected side. Tension pneumothorax becomes especially\ndangerous if hypotension, severe respiratory distress or tracheal deviation develop.","point":"Unilateral absent breath sounds shifts acute dyspnoea toward pneumothorax.","skill":"Dangerous differential discrimination"},{"number":9,"type":"SBA","q":"Which drug class best targets clot propagation in acute DVT/PE?","options":["Anticoagulants","Fibrinolytics","Antiplatelet drugs","Bronchodilators"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Anticoagulants reduce further clot propagation. Heparins are acute examples under supervision. The\nrevised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less\ndirectly supported by the full stem.","point":"Anticoagulants limit extension while endogenous fibrinolysis acts.","skill":"Pharmacology role recognition"},{"number":10,"type":"SBA","q":"A colleague suggests immediate thrombolysis for every suspected PE. Which correction is safest?","options":["Use thrombolysis for all suspected PE to dissolve clot early","Use thrombolysis only when pleuritic pain is severe","Avoid anticoagulation whenever thrombolysis is being considered","Reserve thrombolysis for selected high-risk PE contexts under supervision"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Thrombolysis can be relevant in selected high-risk PE contexts, but it has major bleeding risk and is\nnot a reflex treatment for every suspected PE.","point":"Thrombolysis is a high-risk supervised intervention, not a routine PE reflex.","skill":"Pharmacology safety-boundary reasoning"},{"number":11,"type":"SBA","q":"She becomes confused, hypotensive and persistently hypoxic despite oxygen. What is safest?","options":["Arrange outpatient imaging because the diagnosis is still uncertain","Urgent escalation","Observe until leg ultrasound confirms clot extension","Treat as panic if chest examination remains clear"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Confusion, hypotension and persistent hypoxia suggest unstable PE or another life-threatening\ndeterioration. This needs immediate senior/emergency escalation.","point":"PE with shock physiology is an emergency.","skill":"Patient safety / escalation"},{"number":12,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What is the term for a clot travelling through the circulation and lodging at a distant site?","options":[],"answer":"Embolus","accepted":[],"explain":"An embolus is material carried through the vascular system to a distant site. In PE, it is usually\nthrombotic material from a venous clot.","point":"Thrombus forms in place; embolus travels.","skill":"Pathology terminology"},{"number":13,"type":"VSAQ","q":"Name one component of Virchow triad.","options":[],"answer":"Venous stasis","accepted":[],"explain":"Accepted: endothelial injury; hypercoagulability; abnormal blood flow/stasis. These are the major\ncontributors to thrombosis.","point":"PE risk factors usually map back to Virchow triad.","skill":"Mechanism terminology"}],"source":"Student Tier 1 Teaching Bank"},{"id":3,"module":"CVS","title":"Undifferentiated shock","scenario":"A 67-year-old man is brought to the emergency unit after almost fainting while walking to the bathroom. He feels\nweak, light-headed and cold. His daughter says he has been increasingly tired for 2 days.\nHe has osteoarthritis and has been using over-the-counter anti-inflammatory tablets for worsening knee pain. He\nalso takes aspirin after a previous transient ischaemic attack. He has no known heart failure.\nOn arrival: BP 102/68 mmHg, HR 118/min, RR 24/min, SpO2 97% room air, temperature 36.7 C, delayed capillary\nrefill, anxious/mildly confused mental state, cool clammy peripheries. He looks pale; chest is clear. Abdomen is soft\nwith mild epigastric discomfort and no guarding.\nA nurse notes that he has passed almost no urine since arrival despite being in the unit for several hours. During\nassessment he reports black tarry stools for 2 days and later vomits a small amount of coffee-ground material.\n\nQuestions and teaching explanations","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest working interpretation before the bleeding source is fully clarified?","options":["Vasovagal near-syncope with full physiological recovery","Medication-related dizziness without perfusion compromise","Symptomatic anaemia without current shock physiology","Early/compensated shock"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Shock is about inadequate tissue perfusion, not just profound hypotension. The BP is not yet\ndramatically low, but tachycardia, tachypnoea, cool clammy peripheries, delayed capillary refill, mild confusion and\nminimal urine output are perfusion-warning signs. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses,\nmechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"A normal or low-normal BP does not exclude shock; early/compensated shock may present before\nsevere hypotension.","skill":"Recognition"},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which finding is the strongest bedside marker of tissue hypoperfusion?","options":["Tachycardia","Reduced urine output","Dizziness on standing","Normal oxygen saturation"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Reduced urine output suggests renal hypoperfusion or organ dysfunction. Tachycardia/dizziness\nsupport haemodynamic stress, but oliguria is a stronger bedside marker that organs are not being adequately\nperfused.","point":"Urine output is a high-yield perfusion marker in shock.","skill":"Perfusion marker recognition"},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"What immediate physiological problem is common to shock states?","options":["Inadequate tissue perfusion causing cellular hypoxia","Isolated arterial hypoxaemia causing breathlessness","Transient autonomic reflex causing brief collapse","Pain-mediated catecholamine surge causing tachycardia"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Shock states share inadequate tissue perfusion or oxygen delivery/utilisation at tissue level, causing\ncellular hypoxia and organ dysfunction.","point":"Shock is a tissue-perfusion problem; oxygen saturation may be normal while oxygen delivery fails.","skill":"Applied physiology"},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"After black stools, coffee-ground vomiting and falling haemoglobin become available, which shock pattern best fits\nthe case?","options":["Cardiogenic shock from occult myocardial dysfunction","Distributive shock from inflammatory vasodilation","Haemorrhagic hypovolaemic shock","Obstructive shock from impaired venous return"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Black tarry stools, coffee-ground vomiting, pallor, falling haemoglobin and poor perfusion point to\nblood loss reducing effective circulating volume.","point":"When the volume loss is blood, classify it as haemorrhagic hypovolaemic shock.","skill":"Shock classification"},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Repeat data show BP 102/68 to 86/58, HR 118 to 132, RR 24 to 30, mental state mildly confused to more drowsy,\nminimal urine output, low and falling haemoglobin, urea\ndisproportionately raised, black stools and coffee-ground vomit. What is the best interpretation and implication?","options":["Cardiogenic shock; escalate through an emergency cardiac pathway","Worsening haemorrhagic shock; escalate through an emergency shock pathway","Distributive shock; escalate through sepsis/anaphylaxis pathway","Chronic anaemia; prioritise outpatient bleeding investigation"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"The trend shows worsening shock and bleeding-source data: falling BP, rising HR/RR, worsening\nmental state, oliguria, falling Hb, melaena and coffee-ground vomiting.","point":"In shock data interpretation, trends matter: falling BP plus rising HR/RR plus oliguria and altered mental\nstate means worsening perfusion failure.","skill":"Data interpretation / shock trend recognition"},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"Which change best indicates progression from compensated or early shock to decompensated shock?","options":["Preserved oxygen saturation despite tachycardia","Persistent postural dizziness with low-normal blood pressure","Pale appearance with stable mentation and urine output","Falling blood pressure with worsening confusion and oliguria"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Early shock may maintain BP through compensation. Falling BP with worsening mental status and\nurine output indicates compensatory failure and decompensation.","point":"Hypotension is a late danger sign, not the starting point of shock recognition.","skill":"Clinical progression / compensated versus decompensated shock"},{"number":7,"type":"SBA","q":"Which action best reflects safe care at this point?","options":["Escalate urgently through an emergency shock-capable pathway","Observe while repeating haemoglobin to confirm active bleeding","Treat as syncope with outpatient gastrointestinal referral","Independently decide transfusion threshold and endoscopy timing before escalation"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Shock physiology with suspected active bleeding and deterioration requires urgent\nemergency/shock-capable escalation. The student should not wait for perfect confirmation or independently decide\nspecialist protocols.","point":"In shock, delayed escalation kills; recognise and activate the correct system.","skill":"Patient safety / systems-level escalation"},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What immediate tissue-level problem results from shock?","options":[],"answer":"Tissue hypoperfusion / cellular hypoxia / inadequate oxygen delivery","accepted":["Cellular hypoxia","inadequate oxygen delivery"],"explain":"Shock causes inadequate tissue perfusion and oxygen delivery, producing cellular hypoxia and\norgan dysfunction.","point":"The final common pathway of shock is tissue hypoperfusion.","skill":"Physiology / mechanism"}],"source":"Student Tier 1 Teaching Bank"},{"id":26,"module":"Respiratory","title":"Acute asthma","scenario":"A 22-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with worsening shortness of breath and wheeze. She\nhas had asthma since childhood and usually uses a salbutamol inhaler when symptomatic. Over the past 2 days\nshe has had a runny nose and cough. This morning she became progressively more breathless and used her\nreliever inhaler repeatedly with only brief improvement.\nShe denies chest pain, haemoptysis, calf swelling, rash, lip swelling or recent food exposure. She has no known\nheart disease. She has never been intubated.\nOn examination, she is anxious, sitting upright and speaking in short phrases.\n Finding Result\n Temperature 37.2 °C\n Heart rate 128/min\n Blood pressure 126/78 mmHg\n Respiratory rate 34/min\n Oxygen saturation 91% room air\n\nChest examination shows widespread expiratory wheeze with prolonged expiration. There are no focal crackles.\nBreath sounds are equal bilaterally. There is no urticaria or angioedema.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest working diagnosis?","options":["Asthma exacerbation","Pulmonary embolism","Community-acquired pneumonia","Spontaneous pneumothorax"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Known asthma, acute wheeze, prolonged expiration, viral-trigger symptoms, reliever overuse and\nshort-phrase speech support an acute asthma exacerbation. The key danger is increased work of breathing and\nimpaired airflow. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles,\nbut they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Known asthma plus acute wheeze and increased work of breathing is asthma exacerbation until proven\notherwise.","skill":"Recognition"},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which feature best supports asthma?","options":["Focal bronchial breathing","Unilateral absent breath sounds","Diffuse expiratory wheeze","Pleuritic pain with pleural rub"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Widespread expiratory wheeze with prolonged expiration supports airflow obstruction from\nbronchoconstriction. This fits asthma better than a focal alveolar process. The revised distractors are plausible\ncompeting diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Asthma is an obstructive wheeze pattern, not a focal consolidation pattern.","skill":"Examination discrimination"},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which mechanism best explains the wheeze?","options":["Alveolar inflammatory exudate","Bronchial narrowing","Pleural air causing lung collapse","Pulmonary arterial obstruction"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Asthma causes variable airflow limitation due to bronchial smooth-muscle constriction, airway\ninflammation, mucosal oedema and mucus. Narrowed airways produce wheeze, especially during expiration. The\nrevised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less\ndirectly supported by the full stem.","point":"Wheeze is sound from narrowed airways, not from fluid-filled alveoli.","skill":"Applied pathophysiology"},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which drug principle provides rapid bronchodilation?","options":["Short-acting beta-agonist effect","Corticosteroid anti-inflammatory effect","Antihistamine H1-blocking effect","Antibiotic treatment of infective trigger"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"A short-acting inhaled beta-agonist such as salbutamol relaxes bronchial smooth muscle and gives\nrapid bronchodilation. This addresses acute bronchoconstriction but does not replace assessment of severity and\nescalation. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they\nare less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Salbutamol is a rapid bronchodilator, not an anti-inflammatory controller.","skill":"Pharmacology principle"},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Which feature is most concerning?","options":["Mild cough only","Normal temperature","Absence of rash or angioedema","Short-phrase speech"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Speaking only in short phrases shows significant breathlessness and increased work of breathing. In\nacute asthma, inability to speak normally is a severity marker and should trigger escalation. The revised distractors\nare plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the\nfull stem.","point":"Speech limitation is a clinical severity clue in acute breathlessness.","skill":"Severity recognition"},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"Which finding would shift concern toward anaphylaxis?","options":["Reduced peak flow with wheeze","Diffuse wheeze after viral symptoms","Urticaria with hypotension","Partial reliever response"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Wheeze can occur in both asthma and anaphylaxis. Urticaria, angioedema, hypotension and rapid\nonset after allergen exposure shift the pattern toward systemic Type I hypersensitivity/anaphylaxis. The revised\ndistractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly\nsupported by the full stem.","point":"Wheeze plus rash, angioedema or hypotension is anaphylaxis until proven otherwise.","skill":"Differential discrimination"},{"number":7,"type":"SBA","q":"Initial bedside assessment shows:\n Parameter Result\n Peak expiratory flow Much lower than personal best\n Oxygen saturation Low on room air\n Respiratory rate High\n Heart rate High\n Chest examination Widespread wheeze\n\nWhich interpretation is best?","options":["Mild asthma with anxiety-driven symptoms","Severe exacerbation pattern","Stable viral upper-respiratory infection","Isolated panic hyperventilation"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Markedly reduced peak flow compared with personal best, hypoxaemia, tachypnoea, tachycardia\nand widespread wheeze indicate a severe asthma exacerbation pattern. Exact percentage thresholds are\ndeliberately not being tested. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or\nmanagement roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Peak flow is interpreted with the clinical picture, not as an isolated number.","skill":"Data interpretation"},{"number":8,"type":"SBA","q":"A CXR is requested because she is not improving as expected.\n CXR feature Report\n Lung volumes Hyperinflated\n Focal consolidation Absent\n Pneumothorax Absent\n Pleural effusion Absent\n\nWhich interpretation fits best?","options":["Lobar consolidation pattern","Pleural effusion pattern","Obstructive air-trapping pattern","Pneumothorax pattern"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Hyperinflation without focal consolidation, pneumothorax or effusion supports an obstructive\nair-trapping pattern. In this case, that is consistent with asthma exacerbation. The revised distractors are plausible\ncompeting diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Hyperinflation supports obstructive physiology; consolidation supports pneumonia.","skill":"Imaging/data interpretation"},{"number":9,"type":"SBA","q":"After treatment starts, her wheeze becomes quieter but she is more drowsy and exhausted.\nWhat is the safest interpretation?","options":["Airflow may be worsening","Bronchospasm has fully resolved","Anxiety has settled after treatment","Pneumonia is now the dominant diagnosis"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"A quieter chest in a tiring asthmatic patient can be dangerous. It may mean less air is moving\nthrough critically narrowed airways, not that the asthma has improved. The revised distractors are plausible\ncompeting diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Silent or quiet chest in severe asthma can mean impending respiratory failure.","skill":"Danger recognition"},{"number":10,"type":"SBA","q":"Repeat blood gas shows worsening ventilation compared with the earlier result.\n Parameter Earlier Repeat\n pH Near normal Lower\n CO2 Low-normal Rising\n Work of breathing High Exhausted\n\n Parameter Earlier Repeat\n Mental state Alert Drowsy\n\nWhich interpretation is safest?","options":["Improving respiratory alkalosis from hyperventilation","Simple viral bronchitis without airflow danger","Stable mild obstruction with fatigue","Impending respiratory failure"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"In acute asthma, early tachypnoea may keep CO2 low or normal. A rising CO2 with falling pH,\nexhaustion and drowsiness suggests ventilatory failure and critical deterioration. The revised distractors are\nplausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full\nstem.","point":"Rising CO2 in a tiring asthmatic is a late danger sign.","skill":"Data-trend interpretation"},{"number":11,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the main role of corticosteroids?","options":["Airway inflammation control","Immediate bronchial smooth-muscle relaxation","Bacterial killing effect","Histamine-receptor blockade"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Corticosteroids reduce airway inflammation and help prevent ongoing deterioration or relapse. They\nare not the fastest bronchodilator; that is the role of inhaled beta-agonist therapy. The revised distractors are\nplausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full\nstem.","point":"Beta-agonists relieve bronchospasm rapidly; steroids treat the inflammatory component.","skill":"Pharmacology principle"},{"number":12,"type":"SBA","q":"Which finding would support pneumonia instead?","options":["Equal bilateral breath sounds","Viral-trigger history","Widespread expiratory wheeze","Focal crackles with fever"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Fever with focal crackles, bronchial breathing, consolidation or air bronchograms would shift concern\ntoward pneumonia. Pneumonia and asthma can coexist, but the focal infective pattern is different from diffuse\nbronchospasm. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but\nthey are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Pneumonia is suggested by focal infective signs, not just any cough.","skill":"Differential discrimination"},{"number":13,"type":"SBA","q":"Which finding would support tension pneumothorax?","options":["Bilateral equal wheeze","Mild nasal congestion","Unilateral absent breath sounds","Prolonged expiration"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Sudden deterioration with unilateral absent breath sounds, hypotension or mediastinal-shift features\nshould raise concern for tension pneumothorax. This must not be confused with ordinary asthma wheeze. The\nrevised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less\ndirectly supported by the full stem.","point":"Unilateral absent breath sounds in acute dyspnoea is a pneumothorax danger clue.","skill":"Emergency differential discrimination"},{"number":14,"type":"SBA","q":"After stabilisation, which prevention step is most useful?","options":["Avoid all physical activity permanently","Check inhaler technique","Use reliever only when symptoms are severe","Take antibiotics at the start of every viral illness"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"After acute stabilisation, asthma care should include checking inhaler technique, adherence, triggers,\nreliever overuse and controller/preventer plan. Poor inhaler technique is a common reason treatment fails. The\nrevised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less\ndirectly supported by the full stem.","point":"Preventing repeat exacerbations requires technique, adherence and trigger review.","skill":"Communication / chronic-care safety"},{"number":15,"type":"VSAQ","q":"Name the rapid reliever drug commonly used for acute bronchospasm in asthma.","options":[],"answer":"Salbutamol","accepted":["Short-acting beta-agonist","SABA"],"explain":"Salbutamol is a short-acting beta-agonist that relaxes bronchial smooth muscle and rapidly improves\nairflow obstruction.","point":"Salbutamol is the rapid reliever, not the long-term inflammation controller.","skill":"Pharmacology terminology"},{"number":16,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What bedside measurement estimates expiratory airflow in asthma?","options":[],"answer":"Peak expiratory flow","accepted":["Peak flow","PEF"],"explain":"Peak expiratory flow estimates expiratory airflow and helps assess obstruction and response to\ntreatment. It must be interpreted with clinical severity and patient effort.","point":"Peak flow supports severity assessment but does not replace clinical judgement.","skill":"Investigation terminology"}],"source":"Student Tier 1 Teaching Bank"},{"id":33,"module":"Respiratory","title":"COPD exacerbation","scenario":"A 66-year-old man presents to the emergency department with worsening breathlessness over 3 days. He has had\na chronic productive cough for years and usually brings up white sputum in the mornings. He has smoked one pack\nper day for more than 40 years.\nThis week his cough has worsened and his sputum has become thicker and more yellow. He is more breathless\nthan usual and has been using a borrowed inhaler from a neighbour with partial relief. He has no pleuritic chest\npain, haemoptysis, calf swelling, urticaria or lip swelling.\nHe says he can usually walk to the shop slowly, but today he became breathless walking from the bedroom to the\nbathroom.\nOn examination, he is sitting forward and using accessory muscles.\n Finding Result\n Temperature 37.8 degrees C\n Heart rate 112/min\n Blood pressure 136/78 mmHg\n Respiratory rate 30/min\n Oxygen saturation 88% room air\n\nChest examination shows diffuse expiratory wheeze, prolonged expiration and reduced breath sounds bilaterally.\nThere are scattered coarse crackles but no focal bronchial breathing. His chest appears hyperexpanded. There is\nmild ankle oedema.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest working diagnosis?","options":["COPD exacerbation","Acute decompensated heart failure with cardiac wheeze","Community-acquired pneumonia superimposed on chronic cough","Pulmonary embolism"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Long smoking history, chronic productive cough, progressive exertional breathlessness,\nhyperexpanded chest, reduced breath sounds, wheeze and acute worsening with increased sputum point toward\nCOPD exacerbation. Heart failure can cause dyspnoea and oedema, but the dominant pattern here is chronic\nobstructive lung disease. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management\nroles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Smoker plus chronic productive cough plus progressive dyspnoea plus acute worsening = COPD\nexacerbation pattern.","skill":"Recognition"},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which feature best supports COPD rather than asthma?","options":["Acute wheeze","Long smoking history with chronic productive cough","Partial reliever response","Breathlessness today"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Asthma and COPD can both cause wheeze and bronchodilator response. A long smoking history\nwith chronic productive cough and progressive exertional breathlessness makes COPD more likely. The revised\ndistractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly\nsupported by the full stem.","point":"Smoking exposure and chronic productive cough are major COPD clues.","skill":"Differential discrimination"},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which mechanism best explains fixed airflow limitation?","options":["Focal alveolar exudate","Pleural air causing lung collapse","Small-airway obstruction with loss of elastic recoil","Pulmonary arterial obstruction"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"COPD involves chronic airway inflammation, mucus hypersecretion, small-airway narrowing and\nemphysematous loss of elastic recoil. These mechanisms produce persistent airflow limitation. The revised\ndistractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly\nsupported by the full stem.","point":"COPD is chronic airflow limitation from airway disease and emphysema.","skill":"Applied pathophysiology"},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which symptom change supports exacerbation?","options":["Isolated chronic morning cough at baseline","Pleuritic pain with haemoptysis","Orthopnoea with paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea","Increased sputum purulence and breathlessness"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Acute worsening of dyspnoea, cough or sputum volume/purulence supports COPD exacerbation.\nRespiratory infection is a common trigger. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms\nor management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"COPD exacerbation is an acute worsening beyond the patient's usual baseline.","skill":"Exacerbation recognition"},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"His chest X-ray report states:\n CXR feature Report\n Lung volumes Hyperinflated\n Diaphragms Flattened\n Focal consolidation Absent\n Pneumothorax Absent\n Cardiac silhouette Not enlarged\n\nWhich interpretation is best?","options":["COPD-type hyperinflation","Lobar consolidation pattern","Pulmonary oedema pattern","Pneumothorax pattern"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Hyperinflated lungs and flattened diaphragms support obstructive air trapping, which fits COPD. The\nabsence of focal consolidation, pneumothorax and cardiomegaly makes pneumonia, pneumothorax and\nheart-failure patterns less likely on this film. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses,\nmechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Hyperinflation and flattened diaphragms support obstructive lung disease.","skill":"CXR/data interpretation"},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"Which finding would shift concern toward pneumonia?","options":["Reduced breath sounds bilaterally","Focal bronchial breathing","Prolonged expiration","Hyperexpanded chest"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Focal bronchial breathing, focal crackles, fever, pleuritic pain or consolidation on CXR would shift\nconcern toward pneumonia. COPD and pneumonia can coexist, so the presence of COPD does not exclude\ninfection. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they\nare less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Focal chest signs suggest consolidation, not simple diffuse obstruction.","skill":"Differential discrimination"},{"number":7,"type":"SBA","q":"Which finding would shift concern toward heart failure?","options":["Yellow sputum","Long smoking history","Raised JVP with orthopnoea","Prolonged expiration"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Raised JVP, orthopnoea, paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea, basal crackles, cardiomegaly and\nperipheral oedema support heart failure or cor pulmonale depending context. COPD patients may also develop\nright-heart strain, so venous congestion must not be ignored. The revised distractors are plausible competing\ndiagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"JVP and oedema force you to consider cardiac involvement, not only bronchospasm.","skill":"Differential discrimination"},{"number":8,"type":"SBA","q":"Which finding would shift concern toward pneumothorax?","options":["Diffuse bilateral wheeze","Chronic productive cough","Gradual sputum change","Sudden unilateral absent breath sounds"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"COPD patients are at risk of pneumothorax, especially with emphysematous lung disease. Sudden\ndeterioration with unilateral absent breath sounds should trigger concern for pneumothorax. The revised distractors\nare plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the\nfull stem.","point":"COPD does not protect against pneumothorax; unilateral signs are a danger clue.","skill":"Emergency differential discrimination"},{"number":9,"type":"SBA","q":"Which treatment principle gives bronchodilation?","options":["Inhaled bronchodilator effect","Controlled oxygen therapy","Corticosteroid anti-inflammatory effect","Antibiotic treatment of infective trigger"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Inhaled bronchodilators reduce airway smooth-muscle constriction and improve airflow in obstructive\ndisease. This is a treatment principle, not a dose-regimen question. The revised distractors are plausible competing\ndiagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Bronchodilators target airflow obstruction.","skill":"Pharmacology principle"},{"number":10,"type":"SBA","q":"Why should oxygen be used carefully?","options":["It has no role in COPD exacerbations","Some patients are at risk of CO2 retention","It treats the infective trigger directly","It reverses emphysematous lung destruction"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Some COPD patients are vulnerable to hypercapnia during oxygen therapy. The safe principle is\nsupervised oxygen assessment and monitoring rather than careless high-flow use. The revised distractors are\nplausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full\nstem.","point":"In COPD, correct hypoxaemia but monitor for CO2 retention risk.","skill":"Therapy safety principle"},{"number":11,"type":"SBA","q":"A blood gas shows:\n Parameter Result\n pH Low\n CO2 Raised\n Bicarbonate Raised\n Oxygenation Low\n\nWhich interpretation is best?","options":["Pure metabolic alkalosis","Normal ventilation with chronic compensation","Acute-on-chronic hypercapnic respiratory failure","Primary respiratory alkalosis from anxiety"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Raised CO2 with low pH indicates respiratory acidosis. Raised bicarbonate suggests chronic renal\ncompensation, so a COPD patient with worsening acidosis may have acute-on-chronic hypercapnic respiratory\nfailure. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are\nless directly supported by the full stem.","point":"COPD can produce acute-on-chronic hypercapnic respiratory failure.","skill":"ABG/data interpretation"},{"number":12,"type":"SBA","q":"Which feature suggests impending respiratory failure?","options":["Yellow sputum","Mild ankle swelling","Chronic morning cough","Drowsiness"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Drowsiness in a breathless COPD patient may reflect hypercapnia, exhaustion, hypoxaemia or\nsepsis. It is a major escalation clue. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or\nmanagement roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Drowsiness in COPD exacerbation is not reassurance; it is danger.","skill":"Danger recognition"},{"number":13,"type":"SBA","q":"Which prevention action is highest yield long-term?","options":["Smoking cessation","Avoid all physical activity","Stop inhalers once symptoms settle","Use antibiotics for every cough episode"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Smoking cessation is the most important long-term intervention for slowing COPD progression and\nreducing future risk. It also reduces cardiovascular and cancer risk. The revised distractors are plausible competing\ndiagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Smoking cessation is central COPD disease-modification counselling.","skill":"Prevention / counselling"},{"number":14,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest immediate action?","options":["Discharge with cough syrup after one bronchodilator dose","Urgent supervised escalation","Reassure as anxiety if chest X-ray has no consolidation","Wait for sputum culture before treating the exacerbation"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"This patient has hypoxaemia, tachypnoea, accessory-muscle use and possible hypercapnia. He\nneeds supervised emergency assessment, bronchodilation, careful oxygenation, infection assessment and\nescalation if poor response or drowsiness develops. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses,\nmechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"COPD exacerbation with hypoxaemia or exhaustion is an emergency pattern.","skill":"Patient safety / escalation"},{"number":15,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What smoking-related chronic lung disease causes persistent airflow limitation?","options":[],"answer":"Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease","accepted":["COPD"],"explain":"COPD is a chronic disease characterised by persistent airflow limitation, commonly associated with\nsmoking exposure, chronic bronchitis and emphysema.","point":"COPD is persistent airflow limitation, not simply \"old asthma.\"","skill":"Core terminology"},{"number":16,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What blood-gas abnormality means raised arterial carbon dioxide?","options":[],"answer":"Hypercapnia","accepted":["Hypercarbia","raised CO2"],"explain":"Hypercapnia means increased carbon dioxide in arterial blood. In COPD exacerbation, it suggests\ninadequate ventilation and can cause drowsiness, acidosis and respiratory failure.","point":"Drowsy COPD patient with raised CO2 has dangerous ventilatory failure physiology.","skill":"ABG terminology"}],"source":"Student Tier 1 Teaching Bank"},{"id":11,"module":"Respiratory","title":"Pneumonia","scenario":"A 72-year-old man presents to the emergency department with 3 days of worsening cough, fever and shortness of\nbreath. His sputum has become thick and yellow-green. He has sharp right-sided chest pain that worsens when he\ncoughs or takes a deep breath. He has a 45-pack-year smoking history and was previously told he has early\nCOPD, but he has not attended clinic regularly. He uses a salbutamol inhaler occasionally when wheezy. He has\nhypertension and type 2 diabetes. On examination, he looks acutely unwell. Chest examination shows reduced\nexpansion on the right lower chest, bronchial breath sounds and coarse crackles at the right base. There is no\nperipheral oedema. The trachea is central. His JVP is not raised.\n Finding Result\n Temperature 38.7 C\n Heart rate 116/min\n Blood pressure 104/66 mmHg\n Respiratory rate 30/min\n Oxygen saturation 88% on room air\n\nQuestions and teaching explanations","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest working diagnosis at this stage?","options":["COPD exacerbation with infective sputum change","Acute pulmonary oedema","Community-acquired pneumonia","Pulmonary embolism with pleuritic pain"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"The pattern is acute fever, productive sputum, pleuritic chest pain, focal bronchial breathing/crackles,\ntachypnoea and hypoxia. That is pneumonia with respiratory compromise until proven otherwise.","point":"Acute cough + fever + focal signs + hypoxia = pneumonia until proven otherwise.","skill":"Recognition"},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which finding most strongly indicates that this is not just an uncomplicated cough illness?","options":["Purulent sputum","Room-air hypoxia","Smoking history","Pleuritic chest pain"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Hypoxia indicates impaired gas exchange and physiological compromise. Sputum, smoking history\nand pleuritic pain support the diagnosis, but oxygenation identifies danger.","point":"In acute dyspnoea, oxygenation and work of breathing determine urgency.","skill":"Safety / risk prioritisation"},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which mechanism best explains the bronchial breath sounds and focal crackles?","options":["Obstructive air trapping from narrowed bronchi","Hydrostatic fluid transudation into alveoli","Pleural air separating lung from chest wall","Alveolar inflammatory exudate"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Pneumonia fills alveoli with inflammatory exudate. Patent bronchi within consolidated lung transmit\nbronchial breath sounds and can produce air bronchograms on CXR.","point":"Consolidation means alveolar filling, not simply collapsed lung.","skill":"Applied pathology / mechanism"},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Before interpreting the opacity on a frontal CXR, which check is most appropriate?","options":["Film adequacy and projection","Whether the clinical diagnosis is already likely","Cardiothoracic ratio before technical quality","Immediate CT before reading the plain film"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"CXR interpretation starts with technical adequacy: rotation, inspiration, penetration, projection and\npositioning. A poor film can mimic or hide pathology.","point":"First decide whether the X-ray is interpretable; then interpret systematically.","skill":"CXR interpretation framework"},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"The CXR report states: right lower-zone air-space opacity with visible air bronchograms, no pneumothorax, no\nlarge pleural effusion and non-enlarged cardiac size. Which interpretation best fits the clinical picture?","options":["COPD hyperinflation pattern","Pulmonary oedema pattern","Lobar consolidation pattern","Pneumothorax pattern"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Focal air-space opacity with air bronchograms in a febrile patient with productive cough and focal\nsigns supports lobar consolidation from pneumonia.","point":"Air bronchograms within focal opacity are a consolidation clue.","skill":"Formal CXR/data interpretation"},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"Which alternative CXR pattern would more strongly support COPD/emphysema as the dominant explanation?","options":["Focal opacity with air bronchograms","Hyperinflation with flattened diaphragms","Bilateral interstitial oedema with Kerley B lines","Pleural line with absent peripheral lung markings"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Hyperinflation, flattened diaphragms and reduced peripheral vascular markings support obstructive\nair-trapping/COPD-type physiology.","point":"COPD on CXR is a hyperinflation pattern, not focal consolidation.","skill":"Differential CXR interpretation"},{"number":7,"type":"SBA","q":"A colleague suggests repeated salbutamol only because of the COPD history. Which response is safest?","options":["Bronchodilator response excludes pneumonia","Salbutamol should replace antibiotics if COPD is known","COPD history means focal consolidation is expected","Treat the pneumonia pathway as well as any bronchospasm"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Salbutamol may help bronchospasm, but it does not treat alveolar consolidation from pneumonia.\nHypoxic pneumonia requires infection-focused treatment and escalation.","point":"A drug can be correct for one mechanism and inadequate for another.","skill":"Pharmacology / treatment-role discrimination"},{"number":8,"type":"SBA","q":"He becomes drowsier, tachypnoeic, persistently hypoxic despite oxygen, hypotensive and oliguric. What is the\nsafest interpretation?","options":["Severe pneumonia with systemic deterioration; urgent escalation","Expected fever course; continue observation only","Panic hyperventilation because chest signs are subjective","Stable COPD flare because salbutamol use is part of the history"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Persistent hypoxia despite oxygen, rising work of breathing, falling BP, drowsiness and oliguria\nindicate severe respiratory and systemic deterioration.","point":"In pneumonia, worsening oxygenation, mental state, BP and urine output are danger signals.","skill":"Deterioration recognition / escalation"},{"number":9,"type":"SBA","q":"Which finding would most strongly shift the differential toward pneumothorax rather than pneumonia?","options":["Productive cough with fever","Air bronchograms in focal opacity","Unilateral absent breath sounds","Basal crackles with bronchial breathing"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Pneumothorax places air in the pleural space and reduces ventilation on the affected side. Tension\npneumothorax becomes especially dangerous if hypotension, severe respiratory distress or tracheal deviation\ndevelop.","point":"Unilateral absent breath sounds shifts acute dyspnoea toward pneumothorax.","skill":"Dangerous differential discrimination"},{"number":10,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What radiological term describes alveolar air-space filling that produces focal opacity in pneumonia?","options":[],"answer":"Consolidation","accepted":[],"explain":"Consolidation means alveoli are filled with material such as inflammatory exudate, pus, blood or fluid.\nIn pneumonia, inflammatory exudate is the classic cause.","point":"Pneumonia classically produces consolidation.","skill":"Core CXR terminology"},{"number":11,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What CXR sign describes visible air-filled bronchi running through surrounding consolidated lung?","options":[],"answer":"Air bronchogram","accepted":[],"explain":"Air bronchograms occur when bronchi remain air-filled while surrounding alveoli are opacified by\nconsolidation.","point":"Air bronchograms support alveolar consolidation.","skill":"CXR sign terminology"}],"source":"Student Tier 1 Teaching Bank"},{"id":7,"module":"Respiratory","title":"Pulmonary tuberculosis","scenario":"A 39-year-old woman presents to a community clinic in Johannesburg with a 5-week history of cough, night sweats,\nintermittent fever and unintentional weight loss. Over the past week she has noticed small streaks of blood in her\nsputum. She feels tired and has reduced appetite.\nShe lives with six relatives in a two-room house. Her brother was recently started on treatment for tuberculosis. She\nwas diagnosed with HIV 4 years ago but stopped collecting her antiretroviral medication several months ago\nbecause of transport costs and long clinic queues.\nOn examination, she is thin and tired-looking but alert.\n Parameter Finding\n\n Temperature 38.0 C\n\n Heart rate 104 beats/min\n\n Blood pressure 106/68 mmHg\n\n Respiratory rate 22 breaths/min\n\n Oxygen saturation 94% on room air\n\nChest examination reveals coarse crackles in the right upper zone. There is no peripheral oedema and no neck\nstiffness.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"Which diagnosis best accounts for the overall pattern of symptoms and risk factors?","options":["Post-viral bronchitis with prolonged cough","Pulmonary tuberculosis","Asthma with infective trigger","Lung malignancy with constitutional symptoms"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"The key pattern is chronic cough with constitutional symptoms: night sweats, fever, weight loss,\nfatigue, reduced appetite and haemoptysis. The risk is strengthened by close household exposure, crowding and\nuncontrolled HIV infection. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or\nmanagement roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"","skill":"Clinical reasoning"},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which feature most strongly increases this patient's risk of progressing from infection to active tuberculosis\ndisease?","options":["Female sex","Mild tachycardia at presentation","Streaky haemoptysis after prolonged coughing","Stopping ART with impaired cell-mediated immunity"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Control of TB depends heavily on cell-mediated immunity. HIV weakens CD4+ T-cell function,\nreducing effective macrophage activation and granuloma maintenance. The revised distractors are plausible\ncompeting diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"","skill":"Clinical reasoning"},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which immune mechanism is most important for containing Mycobacterium tuberculosis?","options":["CD4+ T-cell activation of macrophages","IgE-mediated mast-cell degranulation","Antibody neutralisation of extracellular toxin","Neutrophil-dominant acute suppuration"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"TB is an intracellular mycobacterial infection. Control depends on T-cell- mediated activation of\nmacrophages, allowing granuloma formation and intracellular killing. The revised distractors are plausible\ncompeting diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"","skill":"Clinical reasoning"},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Sputum GeneXpert MTB/RIF Ultra returns:\n Test component Result\n\n MTB complex Detected\n\n Rifampicin resistance Not detected\n\nWhat is the most appropriate interpretation?","options":["Latent TB infection is confirmed because resistance was not detected","Active TB is supported, with no rifampicin resistance detected on this test","Smear microscopy must be positive before this result is meaningful","TB is excluded as a public-health risk if rifampicin resistance is absent"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"In a symptomatic patient, MTB detected on GeneXpert supports active TB. \"Rifampicin resistance\nnot detected\" means this test has not identified rifampicin resistance, so standard first-line treatment pathway\nreasoning is appropriate under supervision. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses,\nmechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"","skill":"Clinical reasoning"},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Why is rifampicin resistance specifically important when interpreting this result?","options":["It confirms higher infectiousness than drug-susceptible TB","It proves resistance to all first-line TB drugs","It distinguishes active TB from latent TB infection","It suggests possible drug-resistant TB requiring a different supervised pathway\nsupervised pathway"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Rifampicin resistance is clinically important because it signals possible drug-resistant TB. If detected,\nthe patient should not simply be treated as uncomplicated drug-susceptible TB; the result must trigger urgent\nsupervised drug-resistant TB assessment and further susceptibility-guided management. The revised distractors\nare plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the\nfull stem.","point":"","skill":"Clinical reasoning"},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"Which pharmacological principle best explains why TB treatment is given as combination therapy rather than\nsingle-drug therapy?","options":["To reduce emergence of resistant organisms during prolonged treatment","To relieve fever before microbiological control is achieved","To allow monotherapy once symptoms improve","To avoid public-health contact tracing if adherence is good"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"TB organisms can persist in different physiological states and treatment is prolonged. Using a single\ndrug creates strong selection pressure for resistant mutants. The revised distractors are plausible competing\ndiagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"","skill":"Clinical reasoning"},{"number":7,"type":"SBA","q":"Which next action best addresses the public-health risk in this case?","options":["Advise household contacts to attend only if they develop symptoms","Initiate contact tracing and screening of close contacts","Treat only the index patient because adults are usually already exposed","Give BCG to adult household contacts as definitive prevention"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"TB is transmissible, and this patient lives in a crowded household with close contacts. The correct\npublic-health action is contact tracing and screening, especially for household members and vulnerable contacts.\nThe revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less\ndirectly supported by the full stem.","point":"","skill":"Clinical reasoning"},{"number":8,"type":"SBA","q":"Two days later, she returns with worsening breathlessness, confusion and oxygen saturation of 86% on room air.\nWhat is the safest interpretation?","options":["Expected early inflammatory response after starting TB treatment","Await culture confirmation before changing the plan","Clinical deterioration requiring urgent escalation","Reassuring outpatient TB course if blood pressure is normal"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Hypoxia and confusion are danger signs. Even if TB is the underlying diagnosis, the immediate issue\nis deterioration with impaired oxygenation and possible severe infection, respiratory failure or another complication.\nThe revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less\ndirectly supported by the full stem.","point":"","skill":"Clinical reasoning"},{"number":9,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What type of necrosis is characteristically associated with tuberculosis granulomas?","options":[],"answer":"Caseous necrosis","accepted":[],"explain":"TB granulomas classically show caseous necrosis, reflecting a cheese-like necrotic centre within\ngranulomatous inflammation.\nPrimary skill tested: Pathology terminology","point":"TB granuloma = caseating granulomatous inflammation.","skill":"Clinical reasoning"},{"number":10,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What is the public-health term for identifying and evaluating people exposed to an infectious TB case?","options":[],"answer":"Contact tracing","accepted":[],"explain":"Contact tracing identifies and evaluates people who may have been exposed, especially household\nor close contacts.\nPrimary skill tested: Public-health terminology","point":"TB control requires finding exposed contacts, not only treating the index patient.","skill":"Clinical reasoning"}],"source":"Student Tier 1 Teaching Bank"},{"id":5,"module":"Renal","title":"Acute kidney injury","scenario":"A 64-year-old woman presents to the emergency unit with 3 days of vomiting, poor oral intake and progressively\nreduced urine output. She has felt dizzy when standing and has been unable to keep fluids down.\nShe has hypertension and osteoarthritis. For the past week, she has been taking frequent over-the-counter NSAID\ntablets for knee pain. She has no known chronic kidney disease. Her clinic records show that her creatinine was\nnormal 2 months ago.\nOn examination: BP 98/64 mmHg, HR 112/min, RR 22/min, temperature 36.9 C, SpO2 97% room air, dry mucous\nmembranes, cool peripheries, soft abdomen with no suprapubic fullness, alert but tired. She has passed only a\nsmall amount of dark urine since arrival.\nAn urgent renal-function result returns: her creatinine is raised compared with her recent normal baseline, and urea\nis raised. No full electrolyte, bicarbonate, urine- sediment or repeat-trend data are available yet.\n\nQuestions and teaching explanations","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest working renal diagnosis?","options":["Volume depletion without renal injury","Acute kidney injury","Chronic kidney disease with acute symptoms","Lower urinary tract infection with reduced intake"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"The previous normal creatinine makes this an acute change. Reduced urine output plus an acute rise\nin creatinine indicates AKI. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or\nmanagement roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"AKI is suggested by acute deterioration in renal function, especially when reduced urine output\naccompanies creatinine rise.","skill":"Recognition"},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which feature most strongly shows renal dysfunction rather than vomiting alone?","options":["Vomiting for 3 days","Dry mucous membranes","Acute creatinine rise with reduced urine output","Dizziness on standing"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Vomiting, dry mucosa and postural dizziness support volume depletion, but renal dysfunction is\nshown most strongly by creatinine rise plus oliguria.","point":"Volume depletion is a cause; creatinine rise and oliguria are the renal injury signal.","skill":"Discriminating features"},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which AKI pattern best fits the case at this stage?","options":["Pre-renal AKI from reduced renal perfusion","Established acute tubular injury as the dominant initial pattern","Post-renal AKI from urinary obstruction","Chronic kidney disease progression"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Vomiting, poor intake, postural symptoms, dry mucosa, low-normal BP, oliguria and NSAID use point\nto reduced renal perfusion worsened by impaired autoregulation.","point":"Pre-renal AKI is a perfusion problem.","skill":"AKI classification"},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"How can NSAIDs worsen renal function in this setting?","options":["Direct tubular necrosis as the dominant early mechanism","Reduced angiotensin-II-mediated efferent arteriolar tone","Mechanical obstruction of urine flow at the bladder neck","Reduced prostaglandin-mediated afferent arteriolar vasodilation"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"During hypovolaemia the kidney relies partly on prostaglandin-mediated afferent vasodilation to\npreserve renal blood flow and GFR. NSAIDs inhibit prostaglandin synthesis.","point":"NSAIDs are dangerous in volume depletion because they impair afferent arteriolar compensation.","skill":"Applied renal physiology"},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Renal data: baseline creatinine normal; arrival creatinine raised and later further raised; urea raised; potassium\nmildly raised then higher; bicarbonate mildly low then lower; urine output reduced then minimal; urinalysis\nconcentrated/bland with no heavy blood/protein\nand no muddy granular casts; context vomiting, poor intake, NSAID use, dry mucosa. What is the best\ninterpretation and implication?","options":["Volume depletion without AKI; oral rehydration review is sufficient","Pre-renal AKI with biochemical danger; urgent reassessment and escalation are needed","Established intrinsic tubular injury is the primary pattern","Post-renal obstruction is most likely; focus on obstruction work-up only"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"The pattern supports pre-renal AKI with danger: acute creatinine rise, oliguria, vomiting/poor intake,\ndry mucosa, NSAID exposure, bland/concentrated urine, rising potassium and low bicarbonate.","point":"Pre-renal AKI is a whole-pattern diagnosis; urine findings are supportive, not standalone proof.","skill":"Formal data interpretation / renal pattern recognition"},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"Urinalysis is concentrated and bland, with no heavy haematuria/proteinuria and no muddy granular casts reported.\nWhich interpretation is most appropriate?","options":["It proves pre-renal AKI regardless of the clinical context","It supports established acute tubular necrosis as the main pattern","It supports pre-renal AKI and makes intrinsic tubular injury less likely","It excludes AKI because there is no heavy blood or protein"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Bland/concentrated urine can support a pre-renal pattern when it fits context, but it does not prove\nmechanism alone. Muddy granular casts/tubular epithelial cells would support intrinsic tubular injury if supplied.","point":"Urine sediment is supportive, not absolute.","skill":"Urinalysis / sediment interpretation"},{"number":7,"type":"SBA","q":"Which additional finding would most strongly shift concern toward post-renal AKI?","options":["Distended bladder with inability to void","Dry mucous membranes after vomiting","NSAID use during poor intake","Bland concentrated urine sediment"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Post-renal AKI is caused by obstruction to urine flow. Retention, bladder distension or\nhydronephrosis would shift concern toward obstruction.","point":"Post-renal AKI needs obstruction clues.","skill":"Differential pattern recognition"},{"number":8,"type":"SBA","q":"Profiles: 1 = vomiting/poor intake/NSAID use with oliguria, concentrated/bland urine and recent normal creatinine\nnow raised. 2 = prolonged hypotension/nephrotoxin exposure with muddy granular casts or tubular epithelial cells.\n3 = poor stream/suprapubic fullness with distended bladder or hydronephrosis. Which labelling is most accurate?","options":["1 = intrinsic renal AKI; 2 = pre-renal AKI; 3 = post-renal AKI","1 = post-renal AKI; 2 = intrinsic renal AKI; 3 = pre-renal AKI","1 = pre-renal AKI; 2 = post-renal AKI; 3 = intrinsic renal AKI","1 = pre-renal AKI; 2 = intrinsic renal AKI; 3 = post-renal AKI"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Profile 1 fits reduced renal perfusion with NSAID autoregulatory impairment; profile 2 fits intrinsic\ntubular injury; profile 3 fits obstruction.","point":"AKI classification is pattern-based: perfusion, renal parenchymal problem or outflow obstruction.","skill":"Formal data interpretation / AKI profile comparison"},{"number":9,"type":"SBA","q":"Which finding is the most concerning AKI danger pattern?","options":["Mild nausea after vomiting","Rising potassium with worsening oliguria","Stable creatinine with normal potassium","Dry mucous membranes alone"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"AKI can impair potassium excretion. Rising potassium with worsening oliguria is safety- critical and\nneeds urgent attention/escalation. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or\nmanagement roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"In AKI, rising potassium plus falling urine output is dangerous even before a treatment protocol is\nselected.","skill":"Danger recognition"},{"number":10,"type":"SBA","q":"Which action best reflects safe care at this point?","options":["Escalate urgently through a senior emergency/renal-capable pathway","Stop NSAIDs, treat vomiting and repeat renal function routinely later","Decide independently whether dialysis is indicated","Arrange outpatient renal review if urine output improves slightly"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"AKI with worsening oliguria, rising creatinine and potassium/acidosis danger requires urgent\nsupervised escalation. GEMP 2 action is danger recognition and escalation, not independent\ndialysis/fluid/potassium protocol decisions.","point":"AKI with oliguria and biochemical danger is not routine outpatient dysfunction.","skill":"Patient safety / systems-level escalation"},{"number":11,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What renal syndrome is suggested by an acute rise in creatinine with reduced urine output?","options":[],"answer":"Acute kidney injury / AKI","accepted":[],"explain":"An acute rise in creatinine with reduced urine output indicates AKI; exact staging thresholds are not\nrequired here.","point":"The syndrome name is AKI: acute deterioration in kidney function, often with oliguria.","skill":"Renal terminology"},{"number":12,"type":"VSAQ","q":"Which arteriole is affected when NSAIDs reduce prostaglandin-mediated renal vasodilation?","options":[],"answer":"Afferent arteriole / afferent renal arteriole","accepted":["Afferent renal arteriole"],"explain":"Prostaglandins help maintain afferent arteriolar vasodilation when effective circulating volume is low.\nNSAIDs inhibit this protective mechanism and lower renal perfusion/GFR.","point":"NSAIDs impair afferent arteriolar compensation; ACEi/ARBs are the common efferent-side comparison.","skill":"Applied renal physiology"}],"source":"Student Tier 1 Teaching Bank"},{"id":24,"module":"Renal","title":"Urinary retention","scenario":"A 68-year-old man presents with 8 months of worsening urinary symptoms: weak stream, hesitancy, incomplete\nemptying and nocturia. Over the past 12 hours, he has been unable to pass urine despite a painful urge. He has\nsuprapubic discomfort but no flank pain, visible haematuria, weight loss, bone pain or fever. On examination, he is\nuncomfortable but alert.\n Finding Result\n Temperature 36.9 C\n Heart rate 96/min\n Blood pressure 138/82 mmHg\n Respiratory rate 18/min\n Oxygen saturation 98% room air\n\nThe lower abdomen is distended and tender suprapubically. Digital rectal examination shows a symmetrically\nenlarged, smooth, rubbery, non-tender prostate with a palpable median sulcus.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest working diagnosis?","options":["Acute prostatitis with obstructive symptoms","Benign prostatic hyperplasia","Prostate cancer with bladder-outlet obstruction","Ureteric stone causing acute obstruction"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Progressive weak stream, hesitancy, nocturia, incomplete emptying and acute urinary retention in an\nolder man strongly suggest BPH. The smooth, rubbery, symmetrically enlarged non-tender prostate supports this.","point":"Older man + obstructive LUTS + smooth enlarged prostate = BPH pattern.","skill":"Recognition"},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which symptom best reflects bladder-outlet obstruction?","options":["Weak urinary stream","Dysuria with frequency","Colicky flank-to-groin pain","Painless visible haematuria"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Weak stream, hesitancy, straining, intermittency and incomplete emptying are obstructive lower\nurinary tract symptoms. They suggest impaired urinary flow through the bladder outlet. The revised distractors are\nplausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full\nstem.","point":"Weak stream and hesitancy are obstructive LUTS.","skill":"Symptom discrimination"},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which anatomical zone is most relevant?","options":["Peripheral zone","Central zone around ejaculatory ducts","External urethral sphincter","Transition zone"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"BPH arises mainly from the transition/periurethral region of the prostate. Enlargement here\ncompresses the urethra and produces obstructive urinary symptoms.","point":"BPH is a transition-zone enlargement problem.","skill":"Applied anatomy"},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"What mechanism explains the weak stream?","options":["Detrusor overactivity without outlet resistance","Ureteric smooth-muscle spasm","Urethral compression","Glomerular filtration-barrier injury"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Nodular enlargement of the transition zone narrows the prostatic urethra. This increases outlet\nresistance and produces weak stream, hesitancy and incomplete bladder emptying.","point":"BPH causes symptoms by compressing the urethra.","skill":"Applied pathophysiology"},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Which DRE finding best fits BPH?","options":["Hard irregular nodules","Smooth rubbery enlargement","Boggy tender prostate","High-riding prostate after pelvic trauma"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"BPH classically gives smooth, firm/rubbery, symmetrical enlargement. Hard irregular nodules\nsuggest cancer; boggy tenderness suggests prostatitis; absent anal tone suggests neurological/spinal pathology.","point":"BPH feels smooth and enlarged; cancer feels hard or irregular.","skill":"Examination interpretation"},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"Which finding suggests prostate cancer instead?","options":["Symmetric smooth enlargement","Acute painful retention alone","Nocturia with weak stream","Hard irregular nodule"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"A hard, irregular or nodular prostate on DRE is concerning for prostate carcinoma. BPH is usually\nsmooth and rubbery.","point":"Hard irregular prostate is a cancer red flag, not typical BPH.","skill":"Differential discrimination"},{"number":7,"type":"SBA","q":"Which finding suggests acute prostatitis?","options":["Tender boggy prostate","Smooth enlarged gland","Chronic painless nocturia","Palpable median sulcus"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Acute prostatitis is suggested by fever, dysuria, pelvic/perineal pain and a tender boggy prostate.\nVigorous prostate manipulation should be avoided when acute prostatitis is suspected.","point":"Tender boggy prostate points away from uncomplicated BPH.","skill":"Differential discrimination"},{"number":8,"type":"SBA","q":"Assessment shows a distended tender bladder, no urine for 12 hours, mildly raised creatinine, negative urine\nleukocytes and smooth enlarged prostate. Which interpretation is best?","options":["Chronic LUTS without acute retention","Infective prostatitis with sepsis","Acute urinary retention","Glomerular nephritic syndrome"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Inability to pass urine with a painful distended bladder supports acute urinary retention. Mild\ncreatinine rise suggests obstruction may already be affecting renal function or perfusion and should not be ignored.","point":"A painful distended bladder with inability to void is urinary retention.","skill":"Data interpretation"},{"number":9,"type":"SBA","q":"Which complication can follow chronic obstruction?","options":["Acute pyelonephritis only without obstruction","Nephrotic syndrome","Renal colic from a migrating ureteric stone","Hydronephrosis"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Chronic bladder-outlet obstruction can cause urinary stasis, bladder hypertrophy/trabeculation, UTI,\nhydronephrosis and renal dysfunction.","point":"BPH can affect the upper tract through chronic obstruction.","skill":"Complication reasoning"},{"number":10,"type":"SBA","q":"Which statement about BPH is correct?","options":["It compresses the prostatic urethra","It is histologically malignant","It is a premalignant precursor of prostate cancer","It primarily arises in the peripheral zone"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"BPH is benign prostatic enlargement that can compress the prostatic urethra and cause obstructive\nurinary symptoms. It is not premalignant, although BPH and prostate cancer may coexist in older men.","point":"BPH is benign and obstructive; it is not a premalignant lesion.","skill":"Concept clarification"},{"number":11,"type":"SBA","q":"Which finding would make blind urethral catheterisation unsafe?","options":["Smooth enlarged prostate","Blood at the meatus","Chronic nocturia only","Painful full bladder"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Blood at the meatus after trauma suggests possible urethral injury. Blind urethral catheterisation is\nunsafe in that setting and requires urgent urology/senior escalation.","point":"Suspected urethral injury is a catheterisation stop-sign.","skill":"Procedural safety"},{"number":12,"type":"SBA","q":"Which catheter step is safest?","options":["Inflate the balloon once resistance is felt","Continue advancing firmly against resistance","Confirm urine flow before balloon inflation","Leave foreskin retracted after catheterisation"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Balloon inflation should occur only after urine flow confirms that the catheter tip is in the bladder.\nResistance should not be forced, and the foreskin should be restored after catheterisation to avoid paraphimosis.","point":"Never inflate the catheter balloon before confirming bladder placement.","skill":"Procedural safety"},{"number":13,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest immediate action?","options":["Discharge with alpha-blocker advice only","Routine PSA screening as the first priority","Delay review until outpatient urology clinic","Supervised bladder drainage"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"This man has acute urinary retention with a painful distended bladder. The safe immediate principle\nis supervised bladder drainage and assessment of renal function, infection and cause.","point":"Acute urinary retention needs prompt supervised decompression, not outpatient delay.","skill":"Patient safety/initial management"},{"number":14,"type":"VSAQ","q":"Which prostate zone is classically enlarged in BPH?","options":[],"answer":"Transition zone","accepted":["Transition zone","periurethral zone"],"explain":"BPH arises mainly from the transition zone around the urethra, causing compression and obstructive\nLUTS.","point":"BPH = transition-zone enlargement.","skill":"Anatomy terminology"},{"number":15,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What term means inability to pass urine despite a full bladder?","options":[],"answer":"Urinary retention","accepted":[],"explain":"Urinary retention is failure to void despite bladder filling. In older men, BPH is common, but urethral\nstricture, medications, neurological disease and prostate cancer must also be considered.","point":"Retention is an obstructive emergency pattern when painful and acute.","skill":"Core terminology"}],"source":"Student Tier 1 Teaching Bank"},{"id":34,"module":"Renal","title":"Nephritic / nephrotic syndrome","scenario":"A 15-year-old boy is brought to the clinic because his face has become puffy over the past 4 days. His mother first\nnoticed swelling around his eyes in the morning. He now has swollen ankles and says his urine has become dark\nbrown, \"like cola.\" He has passed less urine than usual today.\nTwo weeks ago, he had a sore throat that settled without medical review. He has no diarrhoea, no visible skin\nabscess, no trauma and no known kidney disease.\nOn examination, he looks tired but is alert.\n Finding Result\n Temperature 36.9 degrees C\n Heart rate 96/min\n Blood pressure 152/94 mmHg\n Respiratory rate 20/min\n Oxygen saturation 97% room air\n\nThere is periorbital oedema and bilateral pitting ankle oedema. Chest examination is clear. There is no rash, no\njoint swelling and no abdominal guarding.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest working renal syndrome?","options":["Pure nephrotic syndrome","Lower urinary tract infection with haematuria","Nephritic syndrome","Biliary obstruction causing dark urine"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Dark cola-coloured urine, oedema, hypertension and reduced urine output suggest nephritic\nsyndrome. The key pattern is glomerular inflammation causing haematuria, some proteinuria, salt/water retention\nand reduced renal filtration. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or\nmanagement roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Cola urine plus hypertension plus oedema = nephritic syndrome until proven otherwise.","skill":"Recognition"},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which feature best supports nephritic syndrome?","options":["Frothy urine without haematuria","Dysuria with urinary frequency","Pale stools with jaundice","Cola-coloured urine"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Cola-coloured urine suggests glomerular haematuria. In nephritic syndrome, inflamed glomeruli allow\nred cells to enter the urine, producing smoky or cola-coloured urine. The revised distractors are plausible\ncompeting diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Cola-coloured urine is a glomerular haematuria clue.","skill":"Symptom discrimination"},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which mechanism best explains the oedema?","options":["Salt-water retention from reduced filtration","Low oncotic pressure from massive albumin loss alone","Portal hypertension from liver disease","Venous thrombosis from hypercoagulability"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"In nephritic syndrome, glomerular inflammation reduces filtration and promotes sodium and water\nretention. This causes oedema and hypertension. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses,\nmechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Nephritic oedema is driven mainly by salt-water retention from glomerular inflammation.","skill":"Applied renal physiology"},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which finding best reflects glomerular inflammation?","options":["Nitrites with leukocytes","Red-cell casts","Calcium oxalate crystals","Muddy granular casts"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Red-cell casts form when red cells pass through inflamed glomeruli and become trapped in tubular\ncast material. They strongly support a renal/glomerular source of haematuria. The revised distractors are plausible\ncompeting diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Red-cell casts are a glomerulonephritis clue.","skill":"Urine microscopy interpretation"},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Urine and blood results return:\n Test/finding Result\n Urine blood Positive\n Urine protein Positive\n Urine microscopy Red-cell casts\n Creatinine Mildly raised\n Serum albumin Mildly reduced\n Blood pressure Raised\n\nWhich interpretation is best?","options":["Isolated lower UTI","Pure nephrotic syndrome","Nephritic-pattern disease","Uncomplicated renal colic"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Haematuria, red-cell casts, hypertension and raised creatinine support nephritic-pattern glomerular\ndisease. Proteinuria may occur in nephritic syndrome, but the dominant pattern is inflammatory haematuria with\nrenal dysfunction and hypertension. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or\nmanagement roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Red-cell casts plus hypertension points to nephritic syndrome.","skill":"Urinalysis/data interpretation"},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"Which finding would suggest nephrotic syndrome instead?","options":["Red-cell casts","Cola-coloured urine","Hypertension with oliguria","Heavy proteinuria"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Nephrotic syndrome is dominated by heavy proteinuria, hypoalbuminaemia and oedema.\nHyperlipidaemia and lipiduria may also occur. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses,\nmechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Nephrotic syndrome is primarily a heavy-protein-loss syndrome.","skill":"Differential discrimination"},{"number":7,"type":"SBA","q":"Which lab pattern best fits nephrotic syndrome?","options":["Heavy proteinuria with low serum albumin","Haematuria with red-cell casts and hypertension","Pyuria with nitrites and dysuria","Rising creatinine with muddy granular casts"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Nephrotic syndrome causes large urinary protein loss, leading to hypoalbuminaemia and oedema.\nHyperlipidaemia is also typical. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or\nmanagement roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Proteinuria + hypoalbuminaemia + oedema is the nephrotic pattern.","skill":"Laboratory pattern recognition"},{"number":8,"type":"SBA","q":"Why can nephrotic syndrome cause oedema?","options":["Salt-water retention from nephritic inflammation only","Reduced plasma oncotic pressure","Raised venous pressure from heart failure","Portal hypertension from cirrhosis"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Heavy albumin loss in urine lowers plasma oncotic pressure. Fluid then shifts into the interstitial\nspace, producing oedema. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or\nmanagement roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Nephrotic oedema is driven by protein loss and low oncotic pressure.","skill":"Applied physiology"},{"number":9,"type":"SBA","q":"Which complication is more associated with nephrotic syndrome?","options":["Red-cell casts","Severe hypertensive encephalopathy","Venous thrombosis","Post-infectious glomerular haematuria"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Nephrotic syndrome increases thrombotic risk because anticoagulant proteins may be lost in urine\nand the liver increases production of clotting factors. Infection risk can also rise because immunoglobulins may be\nlost. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are\nless directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Nephrotic syndrome is oedema plus thrombosis and infection risk, not just \"puffy ankles.\"","skill":"Complication reasoning"},{"number":10,"type":"SBA","q":"Which complication is urgent in this nephritic case?","options":["Venous thrombosis","Nephrotic protein loss","Stable microscopic haematuria","Pulmonary oedema"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Nephritic syndrome can cause salt-water retention, hypertension and reduced urine output. If fluid\noverload worsens, pulmonary oedema can develop and become an emergency. The revised distractors are\nplausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full\nstem.","point":"Nephritic syndrome can become dangerous through hypertension, oliguria, AKI and fluid overload.","skill":"Danger recognition"},{"number":11,"type":"SBA","q":"Which history supports post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis?","options":["Recent sore throat","Recurrent wheeze pattern","New sexual partner","Fatty-meal right-upper-quadrant pain"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"A recent throat or skin infection can precede post-infectious glomerulonephritis. The timing in this\ncase, followed by cola urine, oedema and hypertension, supports that pathway. The revised distractors are\nplausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full\nstem.","point":"Nephritic syndrome after sore throat or skin infection suggests post-infectious glomerulonephritis.","skill":"Aetiology-pattern recognition"},{"number":12,"type":"SBA","q":"Which investigation bundle is most relevant?","options":["Oliguria with rising creatinine and potassium","Isolated mild ankle oedema with normal urine","Frothy urine without systemic features","Transient dark urine after dehydration"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Urine microscopy assesses haematuria, casts and sediment. Urea, electrolytes and creatinine\nassess kidney function and biochemical danger. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses,\nmechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"In glomerular disease, assess urine sediment plus renal function.","skill":"Investigation selection"},{"number":13,"type":"SBA","q":"Which finding would make this presentation more dangerous?","options":["Normal creatinine","Mild ankle oedema","Rising potassium","Normal oxygenation"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Rising potassium in renal dysfunction is dangerous because it can cause life-threatening\narrhythmias. Worsening oliguria, rising creatinine, acidosis, pulmonary oedema or severe hypertension would also\nrequire urgent escalation. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management\nroles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Hyperkalaemia is one of the major renal emergency signals.","skill":"Renal danger recognition"},{"number":14,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest immediate action?","options":["Discharge with reassurance after urine colour improves","Treat as simple cystitis because there is blood in urine","Ignore blood pressure if oxygen saturation is normal","Urgent senior renal review"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"This patient has nephritic syndrome with hypertension, reduced urine output and raised creatinine.\nHe needs urgent supervised assessment and renal-capable review. The revised distractors are plausible\ncompeting diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Nephritic syndrome with hypertension, oliguria or renal dysfunction needs urgent escalation.","skill":"Patient safety / escalation"},{"number":15,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What urine microscopy finding strongly supports glomerulonephritis?","options":[],"answer":"Red-cell casts","accepted":["RBC casts","erythrocyte casts"],"explain":"Red-cell casts form when red cells enter the nephron through inflamed glomeruli and become\nincorporated into casts. They point to a glomerular source of haematuria.","point":"Red-cell casts are a nephritic/glomerular clue.","skill":"Urine microscopy terminology"},{"number":16,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What syndrome is characterised by heavy proteinuria, hypoalbuminaemia and oedema?","options":[],"answer":"Nephrotic syndrome","accepted":[],"explain":"Nephrotic syndrome is a protein-loss syndrome. It classically causes heavy proteinuria,\nhypoalbuminaemia, oedema, hyperlipidaemia and increased thrombotic/infection risk.","point":"Nephrotic syndrome is primarily heavy protein loss through the kidney.","skill":"Core terminology"}],"source":"Student Tier 1 Teaching Bank"},{"id":23,"module":"Renal","title":"Renal colic","scenario":"A 34-year-old man presents with sudden severe left-sided flank pain that began 3 hours ago. The pain comes in\nwaves and radiates toward his groin. He cannot keep still and has vomited twice. He noticed that his urine looked\npink earlier today. He had a similar but milder episode last year that resolved before he reached hospital. He has\nno known trauma. On examination, he is restless and sweating.\n Finding Result\n Temperature 37.1 C\n Heart rate 104/min\n Blood pressure 128/78 mmHg\n Respiratory rate 22/min\n Oxygen saturation 98% room air\n\nThere is left renal-angle tenderness. The abdomen is soft, with no guarding or rebound tenderness. The testes are\nnormal on examination.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest working diagnosis?","options":["Testicular torsion","Acute appendicitis","Ureteric stone","Acute pyelonephritis"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Sudden severe colicky flank pain radiating to the groin with haematuria, vomiting and restlessness is\nthe classic renal-colic pattern from a stone moving through the ureter.","point":"Colicky flank-to-groin pain plus haematuria = ureteric stone until proven otherwise.","skill":"Recognition"},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which feature best supports renal colic?","options":["Board-like abdominal guarding","Pain radiating from flank to groin","Dysuria with suprapubic pain only","Diffuse abdominal pain with diarrhoea"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"A ureteric stone can cause severe colicky flank pain that radiates toward the groin as it travels along\nthe ureteric pathway. Guarding, crushing chest pain and ankle swelling point to different presentations.","point":"Ureteric pain often travels from flank toward groin.","skill":"Discriminating feature"},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which urine finding supports the diagnosis?","options":["Leukocytes and nitrites without haematuria","Heavy proteinuria with red-cell casts","Bilirubinuria with pale stools","Microscopic haematuria"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Stones can irritate and injure urinary tract mucosa, causing microscopic or visible haematuria.\nGlucosuria, ketonuria and bilirubinuria point to other pathways.","point":"Haematuria supports urinary tract stone disease.","skill":"Urine interpretation"},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which mechanism best explains the pain?","options":["Ureteric obstruction with smooth-muscle spasm","Glomerular inflammation with red-cell leakage","Bladder-outlet obstruction from prostatic enlargement","Renal parenchymal infection with capsular tenderness"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"A stone moving into the ureter can obstruct urine flow and trigger ureteric smooth-muscle spasm.\nThat produces severe colicky pain.","point":"Renal colic is pain from ureteric obstruction and spasm.","skill":"Applied anatomy/physiology"},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Which anatomical site is a common ureteric narrowing?","options":["Urethral external sphincter","Prostatic transition zone","Pelvi-ureteric junction","Bladder trigone away from ureteric orifices"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Stones commonly lodge at ureteric constriction sites: pelvi-ureteric junction, crossing of common iliac\narteries and vesico-ureteric junction.","point":"Ureteric narrowing sites are stone-impaction sites.","skill":"Applied urinary anatomy"},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"Urine blood is positive, urine leukocytes and nitrites are negative, creatinine is normal and temperature is normal.\nWhich interpretation is best?","options":["Lower UTI pattern","Renal-colic pattern","Infected obstruction pattern","Glomerulonephritis pattern"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Haematuria without strong infection markers, normal temperature and normal creatinine fits\nuncomplicated renal colic in this clinical context. Infection and renal function must still be actively assessed.","point":"Urine blood in flank colic supports renal calculi, but infection and renal function must still be checked.","skill":"Data interpretation"},{"number":7,"type":"SBA","q":"Which finding suggests infected obstruction?","options":["Microscopic haematuria alone","Normal renal function","Pain radiating to groin","Fever with rigors"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Fever, rigors, sepsis physiology, pyuria or worsening renal function in suspected obstruction raises\nconcern for an infected obstructed urinary system. That is dangerous and requires urgent escalation.","point":"Stone plus infection features is not routine renal colic.","skill":"Danger recognition"},{"number":8,"type":"SBA","q":"A scan report says: stone at the vesico-ureteric junction with mild upstream dilatation. What does this imply?","options":["Lower ureteric obstruction","Bladder-outlet obstruction from BPH","Glomerular filtration-barrier disease","Testicular torsion with referred pain"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"The vesico-ureteric junction is where the ureter enters the bladder. A stone there can obstruct urine\nflow and cause upstream ureteric or collecting-system dilatation.","point":"A VUJ stone is a distal ureteric obstruction.","skill":"Imaging/anatomy interpretation"},{"number":9,"type":"SBA","q":"Which stone type is most common overall?","options":["Cystine stones","Struvite stones","Calcium stones","Uric-acid stones"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Calcium oxalate and calcium phosphate are taught as the dominant stone types. This is the broad\nstone-composition principle, not a metabolic work-up question.","point":"Most urinary calculi are calcium-based.","skill":"Core pathology recall"},{"number":10,"type":"SBA","q":"Which association best fits struvite stones?","options":["Hypercalciuria without infection","Recurrent Proteus UTI","Inherited cystine transport defect","Persistently acidic urine with gout"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Struvite stones are linked to recurrent urinary tract infection, especially urease-producing organisms\nsuch as Proteus mirabilis. They can form large staghorn calculi.","point":"Proteus infection is the classic struvite/staghorn stone association.","skill":"Microbiology-pathology linkage"},{"number":11,"type":"SBA","q":"Which complication fits a staghorn calculus?","options":["Isolated lower cystitis without obstruction","Glomerular nephritic syndrome","Testicular vascular compromise","Recurrent pyelonephritis and hydronephrosis risk"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Staghorn calculi can extend into the renal pelvis and calyces. They are associated with obstruction,\nhydronephrosis, recurrent pyelonephritis and a poorly functioning infected kidney.","point":"Staghorn calculus can obstruct and become an infection reservoir.","skill":"Complication recognition"},{"number":12,"type":"SBA","q":"Which presentation suggests glomerular disease instead?","options":["Proteinuria with red-cell casts","Colicky groin radiation","Visible ureteric stone on imaging","Vomiting during severe pain"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Proteinuria, red-cell casts, hypertension or renal dysfunction would push haematuria reasoning\ntoward glomerular disease rather than simple stone disease.","point":"Haematuria is not one diagnosis; glomerular clues change the pathway.","skill":"Differential discrimination"},{"number":13,"type":"SBA","q":"Which management principle is safest?","options":["Treat as routine colic without urine or renal-function assessment","Assess pain, urine, renal function and sepsis features","Delay review unless haematuria becomes visible","Avoid escalation if the patient is afebrile at first assessment"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Renal colic care requires pain-control principles, urine assessment, renal function assessment and\nactive screening for infection or obstruction danger. Exact analgesic and imaging pathways are protocol-sensitive.","point":"In renal colic, always check for infection and obstruction risk.","skill":"Management principle/safety"},{"number":14,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What symptom term describes blood in the urine?","options":[],"answer":"Haematuria","accepted":[],"explain":"Haematuria may be visible or microscopic. Renal calculi are one important cause, but infection,\nmalignancy, glomerular disease and trauma remain important differentials.","point":"Haematuria must be interpreted by pattern and context.","skill":"Core terminology"},{"number":15,"type":"VSAQ","q":"Name one common site of ureteric stone impaction.","options":[],"answer":"Pelvi-ureteric junction","accepted":["Pelvi-ureteric junction","vesico-ureteric junction","crossing of common iliac arteries"],"explain":"Stones can lodge at natural ureteric narrowing sites: pelvi-ureteric junction, crossing of the common\niliac arteries and vesico-ureteric junction.","point":"Ureteric constrictions are clinically important stone-impaction sites.","skill":"Applied anatomy terminology"}],"source":"Student Tier 1 Teaching Bank"},{"id":19,"module":"GIT","title":"Cirrhosis and ascites","scenario":"A 54-year-old man presents to a district hospital with progressive abdominal swelling over 3 weeks. His trousers no\nlonger fit, his ankles are swollen, and he feels full after small meals. Over the past 2 days, his wife says he has\nbecome more forgetful and sleeps during the day. He has a long history of heavy alcohol use. He previously tested\npositive for hepatitis B but stopped attending follow-up. He has not vomited blood and has not noticed black stools.\nHe reports mild diffuse abdominal discomfort but no severe focal pain. On examination, he looks chronically unwell.\n Finding Result\n Temperature 37.8 C\n Heart rate 108/min\n Blood pressure 102/66 mmHg\n Respiratory rate 20/min\n Oxygen saturation 96% room air\n\nHe has scleral icterus, spider naevi, palmar erythema, bilateral ankle oedema and a distended abdomen. Shifting\ndullness is present. He is drowsy but rousable. Asterixis is present. There is mild diffuse abdominal tenderness\nwithout guarding.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest working pattern?","options":["Functional bloating with constipation","Decompensated cirrhosis","Malignancy-related ascites without liver failure","Isolated congestive cardiac failure"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"The pattern is chronic liver disease with ascites, jaundice, oedema, altered mental status and\nasterixis. These are decompensation features rather than simple abdominal bloating. The revised distractors are\nplausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full\nstem.","point":"Ascites plus jaundice and encephalopathy is decompensated cirrhosis until proven otherwise.","skill":"Recognition"},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which finding best supports ascites?","options":["Palmar erythema","Early satiety","Bilateral ankle oedema","Shifting dullness"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Shifting dullness is a bedside sign of free fluid in the peritoneal cavity. In this clinical context, it\nsupports ascites. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles,\nbut they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Shifting dullness and fluid thrill are ascites signs.","skill":"Clinical examination interpretation"},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which mechanism best explains cirrhotic ascites?","options":["Portal hypertension with renal sodium-water retention","Low oncotic pressure from nephrotic protein loss alone","Right-heart venous congestion alone","Bowel gas accumulation from constipation"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Cirrhosis distorts hepatic architecture and increases portal pressure. This contributes to splanchnic\nvasodilatation, reduced effective arterial blood volume, neurohormonal activation and sodium- water retention,\nproducing ascites. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles,\nbut they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Cirrhotic ascites is driven by portal hypertension plus sodium-water retention.","skill":"Applied pathophysiology"},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Labs show raised bilirubin, low albumin, raised INR, low platelets and mildly raised creatinine. Which interpretation\nbest fits?","options":["Normal liver function","Isolated renal failure","Synthetic dysfunction","Simple iron deficiency"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Low albumin and raised INR suggest impaired hepatic synthetic function. Raised bilirubin supports\nliver disease or cholestasis context. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or\nmanagement roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Albumin and INR are synthetic-function clues, not just generic LFTs.","skill":"Laboratory/data interpretation"},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Which result best reflects clotting synthesis?","options":["Raised INR and low albumin suggesting impaired hepatic synthetic function","Isolated raised ALP/GGT with normal INR and albumin","Isolated unconjugated bilirubin with high reticulocytes","Normal liver tests with abdominal distension only"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"INR/PT reflects hepatic clotting-factor synthesis. In cirrhosis, impaired synthesis can increase\nbleeding risk and signal decompensation. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms\nor management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"INR is a key hepatic synthetic-function marker.","skill":"Liver-function interpretation"},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"Which statement about SAAG is safest?","options":["It replaces clinical review","It confirms every cause","It treats infected ascites","It suggests portal cause"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"SAAG is conceptually used to suggest a portal-hypertensive cause of ascites and distinguish it from\nnon-portal causes. This cluster deliberately avoids exact cut-offs because threshold details require audit. The\nrevised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less\ndirectly supported by the full stem.","point":"SAAG is a mechanism-based ascites discriminator, not a treatment.","skill":"Investigation concept"},{"number":7,"type":"SBA","q":"Which change suggests SBP?","options":["Painless ankle swelling","Long alcohol history","Fever with tenderness","Stable abdominal size"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"In a patient with cirrhosis and ascites, fever, abdominal pain or tenderness, worsening\nencephalopathy, renal dysfunction or sepsis physiology should raise concern for spontaneous bacterial peritonitis.\nAnkle swelling and alcohol history support chronic disease context but do not specifically suggest infected ascites.\nThe revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less\ndirectly supported by the full stem.","point":"Ascites plus fever or abdominal tenderness is SBP until assessed.","skill":"Infection danger recognition"},{"number":8,"type":"SBA","q":"Which feature supports hepatic encephalopathy?","options":["Asterixis","Fluid thrill","Ankle oedema","Spider naevi"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Asterixis with drowsiness or confusion supports hepatic encephalopathy in a patient with cirrhosis.\nThe mechanism relates to liver failure, portosystemic shunting and neurotoxin accumulation, especially ammonia\nassociation. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but\nthey are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Confusion plus asterixis in cirrhosis is hepatic encephalopathy until proven otherwise.","skill":"Neurological complication recognition"},{"number":9,"type":"SBA","q":"Which finding suggests variceal bleeding?","options":["Pale stools","Pruritus","Early satiety","Haematemesis or black tarry stool"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Melaena or haematemesis in cirrhosis raises concern for variceal upper gastrointestinal bleeding.\nVarices arise from portal hypertension and can bleed severely. The revised distractors are plausible competing\ndiagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Cirrhosis plus haematemesis or melaena means variceal bleeding must be considered.","skill":"Portal-hypertension complication recognition"},{"number":10,"type":"SBA","q":"Which finding suggests hepatorenal danger?","options":["Stable appetite after small meals","Clear urine colour alone","Rising creatinine or oliguria","No ankle pain"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Renal dysfunction in decompensated cirrhosis is dangerous. Rising creatinine, oliguria or worsening\nperfusion should trigger urgent reassessment and escalation because hepatorenal physiology or other AKI causes\nmay be present. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles,\nbut they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"In cirrhosis, worsening renal function is a red flag, not a minor lab abnormality.","skill":"Renal complication recognition"},{"number":11,"type":"SBA","q":"Which treatment principle targets fluid retention?","options":["Diuretic therapy","Antihistamine itch relief alone","Bronchodilator therapy","Antiplatelet therapy"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Ascites management includes fluid-retention principles such as diuresis and salt-related\nmanagement under supervision. This question tests the broad therapeutic principle, not exact diuretic choice, dose\nor monitoring protocol. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management\nroles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Diuresis is an ascites-treatment principle, but exact regimens are protocol-level.","skill":"Therapy principle"},{"number":12,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest immediate action?","options":["Routine outpatient review after lifestyle counselling","Urgent senior escalation","Reassure and discharge if oxygen saturation is normal","Treat ascites symptomatically and delay review of confusion"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"This patient has decompensated cirrhosis with ascites, encephalopathy, possible infection and renal\ndysfunction. He needs urgent supervised assessment and escalation. The revised distractors are plausible\ncompeting diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Decompensated cirrhosis with confusion, fever or renal dysfunction is not routine follow- up.","skill":"Patient safety/escalation"},{"number":13,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What is abnormal fluid accumulation in the peritoneal cavity called?","options":[],"answer":"Ascites","accepted":[],"explain":"Ascites is free fluid in the peritoneal cavity, commonly caused by cirrhosis with portal hypertension\nbut also by malignancy, heart failure, tuberculosis, pancreatic disease and other causes.","point":"Ascites is peritoneal fluid accumulation.","skill":"Core terminology"},{"number":14,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What pressure abnormality drives varices and cirrhotic ascites?","options":[],"answer":"Portal hypertension","accepted":[],"explain":"Portal hypertension is increased pressure in the portal venous system. In cirrhosis, it contributes to\nascites and portosystemic collaterals such as oesophageal and gastric varices.","point":"Portal hypertension links ascites, varices and portosystemic shunting.","skill":"Mechanism terminology"},{"number":15,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What flapping tremor is associated with hepatic encephalopathy?","options":[],"answer":"Asterixis","accepted":[],"explain":"Asterixis is a flapping tremor seen in hepatic encephalopathy and other metabolic encephalopathies.\nIn this cluster, it supports altered mental status from decompensated liver disease.","point":"Asterixis is a bedside clue to metabolic encephalopathy.","skill":"Clinical sign terminology"}],"source":"Student Tier 1 Teaching Bank"},{"id":14,"module":"GIT","title":"Jaundice","scenario":"A 46-year-old woman presents with yellow eyes, dark urine and pale stools for 4 days. She has developed\ngeneralised itching. Over the past month she has had recurrent right upper-quadrant pain after fatty meals; today\nthe pain is more persistent. She feels nauseated but has not vomited. She has no confusion and no haematemesis\nor melaena. She drinks alcohol rarely and has not started new medication. On examination, she is alert and\nuncomfortable with scleral icterus and mild right upper-quadrant tenderness. There is no guarding, ascites,\nasterixis or peripheral oedema.\n Finding Result\n Temperature 37.6 C\n Heart rate 98/min\n Blood pressure 118/74 mmHg\n Respiratory rate 18/min\n Oxygen saturation 98% room air\n\nQuestions and teaching explanations","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest working pattern?","options":["Haemolytic jaundice","Obstructive jaundice","Acute hepatocellular hepatitis","Gilbert-type unconjugated jaundice"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Dark urine, pale stools, itching and biliary-type right upper-quadrant pain point toward conjugated\nhyperbilirubinaemia from cholestasis or obstruction.","point":"Dark urine + pale stools + pruritus = conjugated/cholestatic pattern until proven otherwise.","skill":"Recognition"},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which symptom most strongly supports cholestasis?","options":["Nausea","Fatigue","Pale stools","Mild right-upper-quadrant pain"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Pale stools suggest reduced bile pigment reaching the intestine because bile flow into the gut is\nimpaired.","point":"Pale stool is a strong obstructive/cholestatic clue.","skill":"Discriminating features"},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Why is the urine dark in this patient?","options":["Conjugated bilirubin","Myoglobin from muscle injury","Unconjugated bilirubin","Haemoglobinuria from intravascular haemolysis"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Conjugated bilirubin is water-soluble and can be excreted in urine when it accumulates in blood.","point":"Urine bilirubin implies conjugated hyperbilirubinaemia.","skill":"Applied biochemistry"},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Labs show raised total bilirubin, raised direct bilirubin, markedly raised ALP, raised GGT, mildly raised ALT/AST,\nnormal albumin and normal INR. Which interpretation best fits?","options":["Haemolytic pattern","Hepatocellular injury pattern","Isolated synthetic failure","Cholestatic pattern"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Marked ALP/GGT elevation with raised direct bilirubin supports cholestasis. Mild ALT/AST elevation\ncan occur secondary to biliary obstruction.","point":"ALP/GGT-dominant jaundice is cholestatic until proven otherwise.","skill":"Formal LFT interpretation"},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Which result best reflects liver synthetic function?","options":["GGT","ALP","INR","Bilirubin"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"INR/PT reflects clotting-factor synthesis and can signal impaired hepatic synthetic function. Albumin\nalso provides synthetic-function context, especially chronicity.","point":"LFTs include injury markers, cholestasis markers and synthetic-function markers.","skill":"Liver-function data reasoning"},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"Which pattern would better fit haemolysis?","options":["Raised ALP/GGT","Raised indirect bilirubin","Raised INR with low albumin","Raised direct bilirubin with pale stools"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Haemolysis increases bilirubin production before hepatic conjugation, so unconjugated/indirect\nbilirubin rises.","point":"Pre-hepatic haemolysis mainly raises unconjugated bilirubin.","skill":"Comparator reasoning"},{"number":7,"type":"SBA","q":"Which change would create emergency concern for ascending cholangitis?","options":["Persistent pruritus with stable observations","Pale stools and dark urine alone","Recurrent fatty-meal right-upper-quadrant pain","Fever with confusion"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Fever, worsening pain, hypotension or confusion in a jaundiced patient suggests infected obstruction\nwith systemic deterioration and requires urgent escalation.","point":"Jaundice plus systemic toxicity is not routine biliary colic.","skill":"Safety / danger recognition"},{"number":8,"type":"SBA","q":"Which imaging test is the best initial modality for suspected gallstone obstruction?","options":["Abdominal ultrasound","Plain abdominal X-ray","CT brain","Peak expiratory flow"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Abdominal ultrasound is a sensible first-line imaging modality for gallstones, biliary dilatation and\ngallbladder pathology.","point":"Suspected biliary obstruction starts with ultrasound-level reasoning.","skill":"Investigation selection"},{"number":9,"type":"SBA","q":"She becomes drowsy. INR is raised and asterixis is present. What is the safest interpretation?","options":["Stable biliary colic","Isolated cholestatic pruritus","Liver failure risk","Resolved obstruction after stone passage"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Drowsiness and asterixis suggest encephalopathy. Raised INR suggests impaired synthetic function.\nThe revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less\ndirectly supported by the full stem.","point":"Jaundice with encephalopathy or raised INR is dangerous.","skill":"Deterioration recognition"},{"number":10,"type":"SBA","q":"A colleague suggests giving only antihistamines for the itch and discharging her. Which response is safest?","options":["Assess the obstructive jaundice pattern","Treat pruritus and arrange routine review only","Discharge if pain improves","Defer assessment until bilirubin is repeated"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Symptomatic itch relief may help comfort, but it does not address the cause of cholestasis. The\nobstruction pattern still needs assessment and escalation where appropriate.","point":"Symptom treatment must not replace investigation of obstructive jaundice.","skill":"Pharmacology / symptom-control reasoning"},{"number":11,"type":"VSAQ","q":"Which enzyme pattern suggests cholestasis on LFTs?","options":[],"answer":"Raised ALP and GGT","accepted":[],"explain":"ALP and GGT rise in cholestasis or biliary obstruction. GGT helps support a hepatobiliary source for\nALP elevation.","point":"ALP/GGT = cholestatic marker pair.","skill":"Core laboratory terminology"},{"number":12,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What type of bilirubin is water-soluble and appears in urine?","options":[],"answer":"Conjugated bilirubin","accepted":[],"explain":"Conjugated bilirubin is water-soluble and can be excreted in urine. Unconjugated bilirubin is\nalbumin-bound and not water-soluble.","point":"Urine bilirubin means conjugated bilirubin.","skill":"Biochemistry terminology"}],"source":"Student Tier 1 Teaching Bank"},{"id":21,"module":"GIT","title":"Acute peritonitis","scenario":"A 43-year-old man presents with sudden severe upper abdominal pain that started 6 hours ago. At first the pain\nwas central and burning, but it rapidly became sharp and generalised. Every movement makes it worse. For the\npast month, he has had intermittent epigastric burning pain, especially when hungry. He has been taking ibuprofen\nfrequently for back pain. He vomited once today and has not passed stool or flatus since the pain began. On\nexamination, he looks acutely unwell and lies very still.\n Finding Result\n Temperature 38.1 C\n Heart rate 124/min\n Blood pressure 94/58 mmHg\n Respiratory rate 26/min\n Oxygen saturation 97% room air\n\nThe abdomen is rigid. There is generalised guarding and rebound tenderness. Bowel sounds are reduced.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest working pattern?","options":["Uncomplicated peptic ulcer flare","Acute pancreatitis","Perforated viscus","Bowel obstruction without perforation"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Sudden severe abdominal pain with rigidity, guarding, rebound tenderness, fever, tachycardia,\nhypotension and reduced bowel sounds suggests peritonitis from a perforated hollow viscus until proven otherwise.\nHis preceding epigastric burning pain and NSAID use make perforated peptic ulcer a likely pathway. The revised\ndistractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly\nsupported by the full stem.","point":"Sudden severe pain plus guarding/rebound = acute abdomen until proven otherwise.","skill":"Recognition"},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which finding most clearly indicates peritonism?","options":["Board-like guarding","Mild nausea only","Hunger-related epigastric burning","Single episode of vomiting"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Board-like guarding reflects involuntary abdominal wall rigidity from peritoneal irritation. Nausea,\nepigastric pain and reduced appetite are common but non-specific and do not carry the same emergency\nimplication as guarding with rebound tenderness.","point":"Guarding and rebound tenderness are peritoneal signs, not routine dyspepsia signs.","skill":"Physical examination interpretation"},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"What explains the pain becoming sharp and generalised?","options":["Ongoing gastric acid secretion","Gallbladder distension during biliary colic","Visceral bowel spasm from obstruction","Parietal peritoneal irritation"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Visceral pain is often vague and poorly localised. Once inflammation or leaked gastrointestinal\ncontents irritate the parietal peritoneum, pain becomes sharper, movement worsens it, and guarding or rebound\ncan appear.","point":"Peritoneal irritation converts vague visceral pain into sharp somatic pain.","skill":"Applied anatomy/physiology"},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"An erect chest/abdominal radiograph report shows free gas under the right hemidiaphragm, no major bowel\ndilatation, clear lung bases and no pneumothorax. Which interpretation is best?","options":["Basal atelectasis","Pneumoperitoneum","Gastric bubble under the left hemidiaphragm","Dilated bowel loops from obstruction"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Free gas under the diaphragm on an erect film suggests pneumoperitoneum, commonly from a\nperforated hollow viscus. In this clinical context, it strongly supports perforation with peritonitis.","point":"Free subdiaphragmatic gas = pneumoperitoneum until proven otherwise.","skill":"Imaging/data interpretation"},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Which source pathway best fits his history?","options":["Viral gastroenteritis","Acute cholecystitis","Peptic ulcer disease","Irritable bowel syndrome"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Recurrent epigastric burning pain and frequent NSAID use support peptic ulcer disease. A major\ncomplication is perforation, which can cause sudden severe pain and peritonitis.","point":"NSAID-associated ulcer disease can perforate and cause peritonitis.","skill":"Cause-pattern matching"},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"Which imaging finding supports perforation?","options":["Free subdiaphragmatic gas","Air-fluid levels in dilated bowel loops","Gallstones with gallbladder wall thickening","Renal tract calcification"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Free air under the diaphragm suggests gas has escaped from the gastrointestinal tract into the\nperitoneal cavity. That supports hollow-viscus perforation.","point":"Pneumoperitoneum is a radiological clue to hollow-viscus perforation.","skill":"Imaging sign recognition"},{"number":7,"type":"SBA","q":"Which alternative pattern suggests pancreatitis?","options":["Suprapubic pain with dysuria","Flank pain with haematuria","RUQ pain after fatty meals","Epigastric pain radiating to the back"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Acute pancreatitis classically presents with severe epigastric pain radiating to the back, often with\nvomiting and raised pancreatic enzymes. Severe cases can deteriorate into shock, AKI, ARDS or DIC.","point":"Epigastric pain radiating to the back is a pancreatitis clue.","skill":"Differential discrimination"},{"number":8,"type":"SBA","q":"Which alternative pattern suggests appendicitis?","options":["Epigastric pain relieved by food","Migrating right-iliac-fossa pain","Colicky flank-to-groin pain","RUQ pain with Murphy-type tenderness"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Appendicitis often begins as vague periumbilical pain and later localises to the right iliac fossa as\nparietal peritoneum becomes involved. Fever, anorexia, nausea and local tenderness support the pattern. The\nrevised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less\ndirectly supported by the full stem.","point":"Pain migration to the right iliac fossa is a classic appendicitis pattern.","skill":"Differential discrimination"},{"number":9,"type":"SBA","q":"Which pattern suggests bowel obstruction?","options":["Distension with obstipation","Board-like rigidity with rebound","RUQ pain with fever","Epigastric pain radiating to the back"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Bowel obstruction commonly presents with colicky abdominal pain, vomiting, distension and\nconstipation or obstipation. Reduced or absent bowel sounds may occur in severe or late presentations.","point":"Obstruction = pain, vomiting, distension and failure to pass stool/flatus.","skill":"Differential discrimination"},{"number":10,"type":"SBA","q":"Which pattern suggests acute cholecystitis?","options":["Haematuria with flank pain","Melaena after NSAID use","RUQ pain with fever","Generalised guarding with free air"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Acute cholecystitis is suggested by right upper-quadrant pain, fever and gallbladder tenderness,\nusually related to gallstones. It differs from simple biliary colic because inflammatory/systemic features are present.","point":"RUQ pain plus fever is inflammatory biliary disease, not simple indigestion.","skill":"Differential discrimination"},{"number":11,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest immediate action?","options":["Trial antacid therapy and observe for symptom relief","Urgent surgical escalation","Routine outpatient endoscopy referral","Oral fluids and repeat abdominal exam later"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Peritonitis with suspected perforation and shock physiology requires urgent senior/surgical\nescalation. The GEMP 2 task is to recognise danger and escalate safely, not independently choose operative\ndetails or antibiotic protocols.","point":"Peritonitis is a surgical emergency pattern.","skill":"Patient safety/escalation"},{"number":12,"type":"SBA","q":"Two hours later, HR rises from 124 to 138/min, BP falls from 94/58 to 82/50, urine output becomes minimal, mental\nstate becomes drowsy and lactate rises further. What is the best interpretation?","options":["Pain-related sympathetic response only","Dehydration without organ hypoperfusion","Stable perforation awaiting imaging confirmation","Evolving shock"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Worsening tachycardia, hypotension, oliguria, drowsiness and rising lactate indicate worsening\ntissue hypoperfusion. In perforation/peritonitis, this may reflect septic and/or hypovolaemic deterioration and needs\nurgent escalation.","point":"In acute abdomen, worsening perfusion markers mean shock until proven otherwise.","skill":"Trend/data interpretation"},{"number":13,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What term describes inflammation of the peritoneum?","options":[],"answer":"Peritonitis","accepted":[],"explain":"Peritonitis is inflammation of the peritoneum. Clinically it produces guarding, rebound tenderness,\npain worsened by movement and systemic deterioration when severe.","point":"Peritonitis is a danger pattern, not just abdominal tenderness.","skill":"Core terminology"},{"number":14,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What term describes free gas within the peritoneal cavity?","options":[],"answer":"Pneumoperitoneum","accepted":[],"explain":"Pneumoperitoneum means free intraperitoneal gas. In an acute abdomen, it raises concern for\nhollow-viscus perforation.","point":"Pneumoperitoneum is the imaging word for free intraperitoneal air.","skill":"Imaging terminology"}],"source":"Student Tier 1 Teaching Bank"},{"id":6,"module":"GIT","title":"Upper GI bleeding","scenario":"A 48-year-old man presents to the emergency department after vomiting dark \"coffee- ground\" material twice\novernight. He also reports passing a black, sticky, offensive stool earlier that morning. For the past 2 weeks he has\nhad burning epigastric discomfort, worse when hungry and partly relieved by antacids.\nHe has been taking ibuprofen frequently for back pain. He drinks alcohol most weekends. His partner says he\nlooked sweaty and almost fainted when standing up.\nOn examination, he is pale and anxious.\n Parameter Finding\n\n Temperature 36.8 C\n\n Heart rate 118 beats/min\n\n Blood pressure 92/58 mmHg\n\n Respiratory rate 22 breaths/min\n\n Oxygen saturation 97% on room air\n\nHis hands are cool. Capillary refill is delayed. Abdominal examination shows epigastric tenderness but no guarding\nor rebound tenderness. There is no obvious jaundice or ascites.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"Which interpretation best explains the combination of coffee-ground vomiting and black, tarry stool?","options":["Distal lower gastrointestinal bleeding with rapid transit","Haemolysis causing dark stool from excess bilirubin pigment","Upper gastrointestinal bleeding with blood altered by acid and intestinal transit","Obstructive jaundice causing pale-stool misinterpretation"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Coffee-ground vomitus suggests blood that has been exposed to gastric acid. Melaena is black, tarry\nstool caused by digested blood, classically from an upper gastrointestinal source. The revised distractors are\nplausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full\nstem.","point":"","skill":"Clinical reasoning"},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which feature in this presentation most strongly indicates immediate physiological danger?","options":["Cool hands with tachycardia and hypotension","Burning epigastric discomfort partly relieved by antacids","Weekend alcohol intake without chronic liver signs","Frequent ibuprofen use for back pain"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"The dangerous issue is not just that he has bled; it is that he has signs of impaired perfusion.\nTachycardia, hypotension, cool peripheries, delayed capillary refill and near-syncope suggest haemodynamic\ncompromise from significant blood loss. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or\nmanagement roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"","skill":"Clinical reasoning"},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which mechanism best explains how frequent ibuprofen use can contribute to this patient's suspected ulcer\nbleeding?","options":["Direct histamine-receptor stimulation of acid secretion","Reduced lower oesophageal sphincter tone causing reflux alone","Autoimmune parietal-cell destruction with achlorhydria","Inhibition of prostaglandin-mediated mucosal protection"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"NSAIDs predispose to peptic ulcer disease by impairing prostaglandin- mediated mucosal defence.\nProstaglandins normally help maintain mucus, bicarbonate secretion and mucosal blood flow. The revised\ndistractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly\nsupported by the full stem.","point":"","skill":"Clinical reasoning"},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"If this patient instead had known cirrhosis with ascites and splenomegaly, which bleeding source would become\nespecially important to consider?","options":["Bleeding haemorrhoids from portal-systemic congestion","Oesophageal or gastric varices","NSAID-related gastric erosions only","Ischaemic colitis from low-flow state"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Cirrhosis can cause portal hypertension. Portal hypertension drives formation of portosystemic\ncollaterals, including oesophageal and gastric varices. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses,\nmechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"","skill":"Clinical reasoning"},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Initial blood results return:\n Test Result\n\n Haemoglobin 8.2 g/dL\n\n Platelets 240 x10^9/L\n\n INR 1.1\n\n Urea Raised\n\n Creatinine Mildly raised\n\n ALT/AST Not significantly raised\n\nWhich interpretation is most appropriate?","options":["Normal INR excludes clinically important UGIB","Normal platelets make variceal bleeding the most likely source","Raised urea can occur in UGIB and must be interpreted in context","Low haemoglobin excludes acute bleeding because acute blood loss is always initially normal"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Upper GI bleeding can raise urea because digested blood protein is absorbed and metabolised.\nHypovolaemia may also contribute to renal hypoperfusion. The revised distractors are plausible competing\ndiagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"","skill":"Clinical reasoning"},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest immediate priority at this student/intern-level reasoning boundary?","options":["Stabilise, obtain urgent senior help, secure IV access and send FBC, coagulation profile, U&E/LFTs and\n crossmatch","Arrange outpatient endoscopy because the abdomen is not peritonitic","Start H. pylori eradication as the first step before haemodynamic assessment","Reassure because coffee-ground vomiting usually means old, self-limited bleeding"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"He has upper GI bleeding with shock physiology. The immediate priority is assessment and\nstabilisation: senior help, IV access, haemodynamic monitoring, blood investigations, crossmatch, and preparation\nfor urgent definitive evaluation. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or\nmanagement roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"","skill":"Clinical reasoning"},{"number":7,"type":"SBA","q":"Several hours later, he develops sudden severe generalised abdominal pain. On examination, his abdomen is rigid\nwith marked rebound tenderness and absent bowel sounds.\nWhich complication is now most concerning?","options":["Gastric outlet obstruction from chronic ulcer scarring","Ongoing uncomplicated ulcer pain","Acute pancreatitis with referred back pain","Perforated peptic ulcer with peritonitis"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Sudden severe generalised abdominal pain with rigidity, rebound tenderness and absent bowel\nsounds suggests peritonitis. In a patient with peptic-ulcer symptoms, perforation is a dangerous complication. The\nrevised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less\ndirectly supported by the full stem.","point":"","skill":"Clinical reasoning"},{"number":8,"type":"SBA","q":"Which statement best explains why melaena is usually associated with bleeding proximal to the distal colon?","options":["Bile pigment converts stool into melaena during cholestasis","Blood remains in the gut long enough to be digested and altered","Lower GI bleeding always produces black tarry stool","Unconjugated bilirubin causes black stool when liver conjugation fails"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Melaena results from digestion and chemical alteration of blood during intestinal transit. Upper GI\nsources are classic because blood has time to be altered before passage. The revised distractors are plausible\ncompeting diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"","skill":"Clinical reasoning"},{"number":9,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What is the term for black, tarry stool caused by digested gastrointestinal blood?","options":[],"answer":"Melaena","accepted":[],"explain":"Melaena refers to black, tarry, offensive stool caused by digested blood, usually from upper GI\nbleeding.\nPrimary skill tested: Core terminology","point":"Melaena = digested blood in stool, classically UGIB.","skill":"Clinical reasoning"},{"number":10,"type":"VSAQ","q":"In cirrhosis, what haemodynamic process drives formation of oesophageal and gastric varices?","options":[],"answer":"Portal hypertension","accepted":[],"explain":"Cirrhosis distorts hepatic architecture and increases resistance to portal venous flow. This causes\nportal hypertension, which drives portosystemic collateral formation, including oesophageal and gastric varices.\nPrimary skill tested: Mechanism recall in context","point":"Varices are a consequence of portal hypertension, not primary oesophageal disease.","skill":"Clinical reasoning"}],"source":"Student Tier 1 Teaching Bank"},{"id":20,"module":"Haem / Cancer","title":"Aplastic anaemia","scenario":"A 21-year-old woman presents to a district hospital with worsening fatigue, shortness of breath on exertion and\nrecurrent gum bleeding. Over the past week she has developed painful mouth ulcers and fever. She says she has\nbeen bruising easily after minor bumps. She has no known chronic illness. She has not had heavy menstrual\nbleeding. She has no abdominal pain, jaundice or weight loss. She recently helped on a farm where pesticides\nwere being sprayed, but she is unsure what was used. On examination, she looks pale and unwell.\n Finding Result\n Temperature 38.4 C\n Heart rate 122/min\n Blood pressure 104/66 mmHg\n Respiratory rate 22/min\n Oxygen saturation 97% room air\n\nShe has conjunctival pallor, gum bleeding, scattered petechiae and shallow oral ulcers. There is no\nlymphadenopathy. The liver and spleen are not enlarged.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest working pattern?","options":["Isolated iron-deficiency anaemia","Immune thrombocytopenia","Pancytopenia","Viral pharyngitis with incidental bruising"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"The presentation combines anaemia symptoms, bleeding features and infection or mucosal\nulceration. That suggests reduction across multiple blood-cell lineages: red cells, platelets and neutrophils. The\nrevised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less\ndirectly supported by the full stem.","point":"Fatigue + bleeding + infection = think pancytopenia, not just anaemia.","skill":"Recognition"},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which finding best reflects thrombocytopenia?","options":["Gum bleeding","Exertional dyspnoea","Mouth ulcers","Fever with rigors"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Platelets are essential for primary haemostasis. Thrombocytopenia commonly causes mucosal\nbleeding, petechiae, purpura and easy bruising. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses,\nmechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Platelet failure causes mucocutaneous bleeding.","skill":"Cell-lineage discrimination"},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which finding best reflects neutropenia?","options":["Petechiae","Pallor","Heavy menstrual bleeding","Mouth ulcers"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Neutropenia increases infection risk and can present with fever, oral ulcers and severe bacterial or\nfungal infection. In this patient, fever plus oral ulcers is a neutropenia red flag. The revised distractors are plausible\ncompeting diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Neutropenia presents with infection risk, fever and oral ulceration.","skill":"Cell-lineage discrimination"},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which finding best reflects anaemia?","options":["Petechiae","Pallor","Gum bleeding","Oral ulcers"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Pallor reflects reduced haemoglobin or reduced peripheral perfusion. In this case, exertional\ndyspnoea and fatigue also support anaemia. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses,\nmechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Anaemia causes pallor, fatigue and exertional dyspnoea.","skill":"Cell-lineage discrimination"},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"FBC shows low haemoglobin, low neutrophils, low platelets, normal MCV and low reticulocytes. Which\ninterpretation is best?","options":["Isolated thrombocytopenia","Haemolytic anaemia with appropriate marrow response","Three-lineage cytopenia","Microcytic iron-deficiency anaemia alone"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Low haemoglobin, low neutrophils and low platelets confirm pancytopenia. The low reticulocyte\ncount suggests inadequate marrow production rather than an appropriate marrow response to blood loss or\nhaemolysis. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but\nthey are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Pancytopenia requires documented reduction in multiple cell lines, especially neutrophils and platelets.","skill":"FBC/data interpretation"},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"What does the low reticulocyte count suggest?","options":["Reduced marrow output","Increased peripheral red-cell destruction","Appropriate marrow compensation after bleeding","Isolated platelet consumption"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Reticulocytes are newly produced red cells. If anaemia is present but reticulocytes are low, the\nmarrow is not responding appropriately. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms\nor management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Low retics in anaemia = production problem until proven otherwise.","skill":"Reticulocyte reasoning"},{"number":7,"type":"SBA","q":"Bone-marrow biopsy shows a hypocellular marrow with no malignant infiltrate. Which diagnosis best fits?","options":["Acute leukaemia","Iron-deficiency anaemia","Immune thrombocytopenia","Aplastic anaemia"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Aplastic anaemia is classically a hypocellular marrow with peripheral pancytopenia, without marrow\ninfiltration or increased fibrosis. It is a marrow-failure syndrome. The revised distractors are plausible competing\ndiagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Aplastic anaemia = hypocellular marrow + pancytopenia.","skill":"Diagnosis integration"},{"number":8,"type":"SBA","q":"Which marrow pattern would suggest acute leukaemia instead?","options":["Hypocellular fat spaces","Normal trilineage haematopoiesis","Marrow blast infiltration","Isolated iron depletion"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Acute leukaemia is suggested by marrow infiltration by blasts, often with cytopenias because normal\nhaematopoiesis is crowded out or suppressed. Peripheral smear may also show blasts. The revised distractors are\nplausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full\nstem.","point":"Pancytopenia can come from marrow emptiness or marrow replacement; those are different\nmechanisms.","skill":"Differential discrimination"},{"number":9,"type":"SBA","q":"Which exposure could support secondary aplasia?","options":["Recent chloramphenicol-type drug exposure","Toxic chemical or pesticide exposure","Chronic menstrual blood loss","Recent upper-respiratory viral symptoms only"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Secondary aplastic anaemia can be associated with drugs, toxins, irradiation, infections or\nautoimmune disease. The farm pesticide exposure is not diagnostic by itself, but it is a relevant exposure clue. The\nrevised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less\ndirectly supported by the full stem.","point":"In pancytopenia, ask about drugs, toxins, radiation, infections and autoimmune disease.","skill":"Risk-factor reasoning"},{"number":10,"type":"SBA","q":"Which finding is most urgent?","options":["Febrile neutropenia","Stable bruising without fever","Chronic fatigue with normal neutrophils","Normal oxygen saturation"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Fever in a neutropenic patient is dangerous because infection can progress rapidly with muted\ninflammatory signs. This requires urgent escalation and empiric antimicrobial principles under Tier 1D Integrated\nExam Teaching Bank supervision. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or\nmanagement roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Fever plus neutropenia is an emergency pattern.","skill":"Patient safety/danger recognition"},{"number":11,"type":"SBA","q":"Which treatment principle is most appropriate?","options":["Oral iron replacement alone","Platelet transfusion decision by student protocol","Watchful waiting until culture results return","Empiric antimicrobials"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"In febrile neutropenia, prompt empiric antimicrobial therapy is a core safety principle under senior\nguidance. This question tests the principle, not a specific antibiotic regimen or dose. The revised distractors are\nplausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full\nstem.","point":"Febrile neutropenia needs urgent antimicrobial cover, not watchful waiting.","skill":"Pharmacology principle"},{"number":12,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest immediate action?","options":["Routine clinic booking after repeat FBC","Urgent senior escalation","Reassurance and discharge if oxygenation is normal","Treat as viral pharyngitis and review next month"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"This patient has pancytopenia with fever, mucosal bleeding and suspected marrow failure. She\nneeds urgent senior or haematology-level assessment and emergency infection/bleeding risk management. The\nrevised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less\ndirectly supported by the full stem.","point":"Pancytopenia with fever or bleeding is not outpatient routine anaemia.","skill":"Patient safety/escalation"},{"number":13,"type":"SBA","q":"Which investigation helps confirm marrow failure?","options":["Bone-marrow biopsy","Peripheral smear alone without marrow assessment","Ferritin only","Coagulation screen only"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Bone-marrow aspirate and trephine biopsy help distinguish hypocellular marrow failure from marrow\ninfiltration, fibrosis, dysplasia or other causes of pancytopenia. Peak flow tests airway obstruction. The revised\ndistractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly\nsupported by the full stem.","point":"Pancytopenia often requires marrow assessment to define the mechanism.","skill":"Investigation selection"},{"number":14,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What term means reduction in red cells, neutrophils and platelets?","options":[],"answer":"Pancytopenia","accepted":[],"explain":"Pancytopenia is reduction in all three major blood-cell lineages. It should not be diagnosed from total\nWCC alone; neutrophils specifically matter.","point":"Pancytopenia = anaemia + neutropenia + thrombocytopenia.","skill":"Core terminology"},{"number":15,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What marrow cellularity pattern is typical of aplastic anaemia?","options":[],"answer":"Hypocellular marrow","accepted":["Hypocellular marrow","reduced marrow cellularity"],"explain":"Aplastic anaemia is characterised by peripheral pancytopenia with a hypocellular marrow.","point":"Aplastic anaemia is a hypocellular marrow-failure syndrome.","skill":"Pathology terminology"}],"source":"Student Tier 1 Teaching Bank"},{"id":10,"module":"Haem / Cancer","title":"Iron-deficiency anaemia","scenario":"A 24-year-old woman presents to a primary-care clinic with 4 months of worsening fatigue, reduced exercise\ntolerance and shortness of breath when climbing stairs. She sometimes feels her heart racing when walking\nquickly. She has started chewing ice frequently.\nHer periods have become progressively heavier over the past year. They now last 7 to 8 days, with clots and\noccasional flooding through pads. She has no haematemesis, melaena, haematuria or chronic diarrhoea. She is\nsexually active and uses condoms inconsistently.\nOn examination, she is alert but tired-looking.\n Parameter Finding\n\n Temperature 36.8 C\n\n Heart rate 104 beats/min\n\n Blood pressure 112/70 mmHg\n\n Respiratory rate 18 breaths/min\n\n Oxygen saturation 98% on room air\n\nShe has pale conjunctivae and mild flow murmur. There is no jaundice, lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly,\npetechiae or active heavy bleeding at the visit.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"Which diagnosis best explains the overall presentation?","options":["Acute haemolytic anaemia","Aplastic anaemia with marrow failure","Acute leukaemia with pancytopenia","Iron-deficiency anaemia from chronic menstrual blood loss"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"The core pattern is chronic fatigue, exertional dyspnoea, palpitations and pallor with a clear chronic\nblood-loss source: heavy menstrual bleeding. Pica, especially ice-chewing, is a classic clue toward iron deficiency.\nThe revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less\ndirectly supported by the full stem.","point":"","skill":"Clinical reasoning"},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest initial bedside or near-bedside investigation that must not be missed in this patient?","options":["Bone marrow biopsy","Pregnancy test","CT abdomen","Serum troponin"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"In any reproductive-age patient with abnormal vaginal bleeding, pregnancy status changes the\npathway immediately. Pregnancy-related bleeding, miscarriage and ectopic pregnancy must not be missed. The\nrevised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less\ndirectly supported by the full stem.","point":"","skill":"Clinical reasoning"},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Her urine pregnancy test is negative. FBC and iron studies return:\n Test Result\n\n Haemoglobin 7.9 g/dL\n\n MCV 66 fL\n\n MCH Low\n\n RDW Raised\n\n WCC Normal\n\n Neutrophils Normal\n\n Platelets Mildly raised\n\n Reticulocyte count Low/inappropriately normal\n\n Ferritin Low\n\nWhich interpretation is most appropriate?","options":["Pancytopenia due to global marrow failure","Macrocytic anaemia due to vitamin B12 deficiency","Microcytic anaemia with iron-deficiency pattern","Isolated thrombocytopenia from immune platelet destruction"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Low haemoglobin confirms anaemia. Low MCV and low MCH classify it as microcytic/hypochromic.\nThe revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less\ndirectly supported by the full stem.","point":"","skill":"Clinical reasoning"},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which mechanism best explains the low MCV in this patient?","options":["Inadequate iron availability impairs haemoglobin synthesis","Vitamin B12 deficiency impairs nuclear maturation","Increased bilirubin production damages circulating red cells","Neutrophil destruction reduces red-cell volume"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Iron is required for haemoglobin synthesis. When iron stores are depleted, haemoglobin production\nis impaired and red cells become smaller and paler, producing microcytic hypochromic anaemia. The revised\ndistractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly\nsupported by the full stem.","point":"","skill":"Clinical reasoning"},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Why is the low or inappropriately normal reticulocyte count important?","options":["Acute haemolysis with appropriate marrow response","Platelet clumping causing a false FBC pattern","Chronic blood loss cannot be contributing","Inadequate marrow response for the degree of anaemia"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Reticulocytes reflect marrow response. In anaemia, the marrow should increase reticulocyte\nproduction if it has enough substrate and is functioning. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses,\nmechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"","skill":"Clinical reasoning"},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"Which treatment principle most directly addresses the likely underlying deficiency, while the bleeding cause is\nassessed?","options":["Vitamin B12 replacement","Vitamin K therapy","Iron replacement therapy","Broad-spectrum antibiotics alone"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"The likely mechanism is iron depletion from chronic menstrual blood loss. Iron replacement\naddresses the substrate deficiency required for haemoglobin synthesis. The revised distractors are plausible\ncompeting diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"","skill":"Clinical reasoning"},{"number":7,"type":"SBA","q":"Which additional finding would most strongly shift concern away from uncomplicated iron- deficiency anaemia and\ntoward marrow failure or haematological malignancy?","options":["Raised reticulocytes with jaundice","Low ferritin with low MCV","Heavy menstrual bleeding with clots","Low MCH with raised RDW"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Reduction in red cells, neutrophils and platelets indicates pancytopenia. That is not simple isolated\niron deficiency. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but\nthey are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"","skill":"Clinical reasoning"},{"number":8,"type":"SBA","q":"A repeat FBC in another patient with fatigue shows:\n Test Result\n\n Haemoglobin Low\n\n Neutrophils Low\n\n Platelets Low\n\n Reticulocyte response Low\n\n Bone marrow description Hypocellular, no infiltrate, no increased\n\nfibrosis\nWhich condition best matches this pattern?","options":["Aplastic anaemia","Isolated iron-deficiency anaemia","Acute lower GI bleeding with normal marrow response","Immune thrombocytopenia alone"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Aplastic anaemia is a marrow-failure condition characterised by hypocellular marrow and peripheral\npancytopenia, without marrow infiltrate or increased fibrosis. The clinical danger comes from anaemia, bleeding\nrisk and infection risk. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management\nroles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"","skill":"Clinical reasoning"},{"number":9,"type":"SBA","q":"Which clinical feature would be most concerning for neutropenia-related infection risk in a pancytopenic patient?","options":["Pica","Mild exertional dyspnoea alone","Fever","Menstrual clots alone"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"In pancytopenia, neutropenia creates infection risk. Fever may be the only early sign of serious\ninfection and requires urgent escalation. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms\nor management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"","skill":"Clinical reasoning"},{"number":10,"type":"SBA","q":"Which statement best explains why transfusion thresholds should not be the main answer- critical focus in this\ncluster?","options":["Every iron-deficiency anaemia patient requires transfusion","Transfusion corrects the cause of iron loss","Haemoglobin alone determines transfusion in all patients","Transfusion decisions depend on severity, symptoms, haemodynamic status and local protocols\nprotocols"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"The exam-relevant principle is to recognise anaemia severity, instability and red flags, then escalate\nappropriately. Exact transfusion thresholds and product decisions are protocol-sensitive and should not be treated\nas simple recall items unless the threshold is supplied. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses,\nmechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"","skill":"Clinical reasoning"},{"number":11,"type":"SBA","q":"Which next-step plan is most appropriate for the original stable patient with heavy menstrual bleeding and\niron-deficiency anaemia?","options":["Treat with iron and ignore the bleeding source if symptoms improve","Assess haemodynamic status, confirm iron deficiency, start iron replacement, and investigate/manage heavy\n menstrual bleeding","Perform immediate bone-marrow biopsy before basic gynaecological or iron-deficiency assessment","Treat empirically for TB because fatigue is present"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"The patient is stable but significantly anaemic. The safe pathway is to assess severity and stability,\nconfirm iron-deficiency pattern, replace iron and address the source of blood loss. The revised distractors are\nplausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full\nstem.","point":"","skill":"Clinical reasoning"},{"number":12,"type":"VSAQ","q":"Which red-cell index is used to classify anaemia as microcytic, normocytic or macrocytic?","options":[],"answer":"MCV","accepted":[],"explain":"Mean corpuscular volume classifies red-cell size and is the first high-yield sorting tool after\nconfirming anaemia.\nPrimary skill tested: Core FBC terminology","point":"MCV = red-cell size classification.","skill":"Clinical reasoning"},{"number":13,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What is the term for reduction in red cells, neutrophils and platelets?","options":[],"answer":"Pancytopenia","accepted":[],"explain":"Pancytopenia means reduction in all three major blood-cell lineages. It requires attention to\nneutrophils and platelets, not only total WCC.\nPrimary skill tested: Core haematology terminology","point":"Pancytopenia is a multi-lineage problem and should trigger marrow/ systemic-disease thinking.","skill":"Clinical reasoning"}],"source":"Student Tier 1 Teaching Bank"},{"id":4,"module":"Endocrine","title":"Diabetic emergency","scenario":"Stage 1: A 21-year-old man with known type 1 diabetes is brought to the emergency unit by his mother. He has\nbeen increasingly weak and confused since the morning.\nFor 2 days he has had a feverish upper-respiratory illness, nausea, vomiting, abdominal discomfort, polyuria and\npolydipsia. He has eaten very little and skipped several insulin doses because he thought he should not inject while\nvomiting.\nOn examination: BP 104/68 mmHg, HR 124/min, RR 30/min, SpO2 97% room air, temperature 37.8 C,\ndrowsy/confused but rousable, dry mucous membranes and reduced skin turgor. Breathing appears deep and\nlaboured. Ketones, pH, bicarbonate and anion gap are not yet measured.\n\nQuestions and teaching explanations","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest broad working interpretation at this point?","options":["Gastroenteritis with dehydration only","Primary intoxication syndrome","Acute diabetic metabolic emergency","Stroke syndrome without focal neurological signs"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"A confused type 1 diabetic with missed insulin, vomiting, polyuria/polydipsia, dehydration and\nabnormal breathing is dangerous until proven otherwise.","point":"In a confused diabetic patient, first frame it as a possible diabetic metabolic emergency.","skill":"Recognition"},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Before full biochemical results are available, which immediate bedside assessment is most important?","options":["Check capillary blood glucose","Send ketones and VBG before bedside glucose","Request urgent CT brain first","Wait for laboratory glucose confirmation"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Bedside glucose rapidly separates must-not-miss hypoglycaemia from hyperglycaemic emergencies.\nKetones/VBG are important soon, but should not delay glucose assessment.","point":"Altered mental state plus diabetes means check glucose early.","skill":"Immediate safety assessment"},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"A capillary glucose is markedly elevated. The patient remains confused, thirsty and nauseated with deep laboured\nbreathing and very little urine. Which diagnosis is now most strongly suspected from the clinical pattern?","options":["Hyperosmolar hyperglycaemic state","Hypoglycaemia with neuroglycopenia","Gastroenteritis with dehydration","Suspected diabetic ketoacidosis"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Type 1 diabetes, missed insulin, marked hyperglycaemia, vomiting/abdominal pain, dehydration,\naltered mental state and deep laboured breathing strongly suggest DKA before full biochemical confirmation. Tier\n1A Teaching Bank | Page 21","point":"Before labs confirm it, DKA is suspected from the illness script.","skill":"Clinical pattern recognition"},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which mechanism best explains the ketosis and acidosis in DKA?","options":["Dehydration alone concentrates circulating glucose","Insulin deficiency drives lipolysis and ketone production","Excess dietary carbohydrate directly causes ketoacidosis","Renal failure alone prevents glucose excretion"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Insulin deficiency promotes lipolysis; free fatty acids are converted to ketone bodies, producing\nhigh-anion-gap metabolic acidosis.","point":"DKA is not high glucose only; insulin deficiency drives ketogenesis and acidosis.","skill":"Applied mechanism"},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Stage 3 biochemical data: plasma glucose markedly elevated, blood ketones high, venous pH low, bicarbonate\nlow, anion gap raised, sodium mildly low, potassium mildly high, urea/ creatinine raised from expected baseline,\nurine output minimal. What is the best interpretation and implication?","options":["HHS with dehydration/renal danger; escalate through a hyperosmolar emergency pathway","Hypoglycaemia with neuroglycopenia; use glucose-focused emergency treatment","DKA with dehydration/renal danger; escalate through an emergency metabolic pathway","Gastroenteritis with AKI; observe response to dehydration-focused care"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"The pattern is DKA: hyperglycaemia, high ketones, low pH, low bicarbonate and raised anion gap.\nRaised urea/creatinine and oliguria add renal danger.","point":"DKA pattern = hyperglycaemia plus ketones plus metabolic acidosis.","skill":"Formal data interpretation / biochemical pattern recognition"},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"Which statement best distinguishes this presentation from hypoglycaemia?","options":["The patient is confused","The patient has eaten poorly","The patient uses insulin","The measured glucose is markedly elevated"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Hypoglycaemia requires low glucose. Confusion, poor intake and insulin use are compatible with\nhypoglycaemia risk but the measured glucose discriminates.","point":"Do not diagnose hypoglycaemia from diabetic + confused + poor intake alone; measure glucose.","skill":"Comparator reasoning / hypoglycaemia discrimination"},{"number":7,"type":"SBA","q":"Which feature most strongly favours DKA over HHS in this case?","options":["High ketones with metabolic acidosis","Markedly elevated glucose","Dehydration and reduced urine output","Altered mental state"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"DKA and HHS both involve hyperglycaemia, dehydration and possible altered mental state. Ketones\nplus metabolic acidosis is the strongest discriminator.","point":"Glucose alone does not separate DKA from HHS; ketones and acid-base pattern do.","skill":"Comparator reasoning / DKA versus HHS"},{"number":8,"type":"SBA","q":"Profiles: 1 = high glucose, high ketones, low pH/bicarbonate, raised anion gap, vomiting/ dehydration/deep\nbreathing. 2 = very high glucose, minimal ketones, no major ketoacidosis, severe dehydration/marked confusion. 3\n= low glucose, no ketoacidosis, sweating/tremor/ confusion/seizure. Which labelling is most accurate?","options":["1 = HHS; 2 = DKA; 3 = hypoglycaemia","1 = DKA; 2 = HHS; 3 = hypoglycaemia","1 = DKA; 2 = hypoglycaemia; 3 = HHS","1 = hypoglycaemia; 2 = HHS; 3 = DKA"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Profile 1 shows the DKA triad; profile 2 shows an HHS-like pattern; profile 3 is hypoglycaemia.","point":"Classify diabetic emergencies by glucose, ketones, acid-base status, hydration and mental state.","skill":"Formal data interpretation / comparative diabetic-emergency profiles"},{"number":9,"type":"SBA","q":"His initial serum potassium is mildly high. Which interpretation is safest?","options":["Potassium stores are adequate because serum potassium is high","Potassium is irrelevant until renal function normalises","Total-body potassium may still be depleted","Potassium danger occurs only in hypoglycaemia"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"In DKA, osmotic diuresis and vomiting cause total-body potassium loss. Acidosis and insulin\ndeficiency may keep serum potassium normal/high initially.","point":"Normal/high serum potassium in DKA can hide total-body potassium depletion.","skill":"Safety physiology / potassium principle"},{"number":10,"type":"SBA","q":"Which action best reflects safe care at this point?","options":["Observe after antiemetic treatment and repeat glucose later","Arrange routine diabetic-clinic review once eating improves","Independently start the full insulin, fluid and potassium protocol","Escalate urgently through an emergency metabolic pathway"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Biochemically supported DKA with altered mental state, dehydration, renal danger and potassium\nrisk requires emergency metabolic escalation. GEMP 2 students should not independently run full protocols.","point":"DKA with confusion, dehydration and acidosis is an emergency: recognise and escalate.","skill":"Patient safety / systems-level escalation"},{"number":11,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What acid-base disorder is suggested by ketones with low pH/bicarbonate and a raised anion gap?","options":[],"answer":"High-anion-gap metabolic acidosis / HAGMA","accepted":["HAGMA","raised-anion-gap metabolic acidosis"],"explain":"Ketones are unmeasured anions that produce metabolic acidosis with a raised anion gap.","point":"DKA is a classic cause of high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis.","skill":"Acid-base terminology"},{"number":12,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What is the term for deep, laboured breathing caused by metabolic acidosis?","options":[],"answer":"Kussmaul respirations / Kussmaul breathing","accepted":[],"explain":"Kussmaul respirations are deep laboured respirations as respiratory compensation for metabolic\nacidosis, classically seen in DKA.","point":"Deep laboured breathing in a hyperglycaemic, ketotic, acidotic patient is a DKA clue.","skill":"Clinical sign terminology"}],"source":"Student Tier 1 Teaching Bank"},{"id":30,"module":"Endocrine","title":"Graves disease","scenario":"A 29-year-old woman presents to a primary-care clinic with 3 months of palpitations, fatigue and difficulty sleeping.\nShe says she feels constantly hot, has become irritable, and has lost weight despite eating more than usual. She\nhas also noticed that her eyes look more prominent in photographs.\nShe has no chest pain, syncope, fever, diarrhoeal illness or recent neck pain. She is not taking levothyroxine or\nweight-loss medication. She is worried that people are staring at her eyes and asks whether treatment is really\nnecessary if she can \"just manage the palpitations.\"\nOn examination, she appears anxious and has warm, moist hands.\n Finding Result\n Temperature 37.4 °C\n Heart rate 118/min\n Blood pressure 132/68 mmHg\n Respiratory rate 18/min\n Oxygen saturation 98% room air\n\nThere is a diffusely enlarged, non-tender thyroid gland. Bilateral eye prominence and lid lag are present. The\nanterior shins show thickened, non-pitting, orange-peel-like skin.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest working diagnosis?","options":["Panic disorder with weight loss from anxiety","Graves disease","Toxic multinodular goitre","Destructive thyroiditis"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Palpitations, heat intolerance, weight loss despite increased appetite, tremulous/anxious\nappearance, diffuse goitre, ophthalmopathy and pretibial skin changes strongly support Graves disease causing\nthyrotoxicosis. Panic disorder can cause palpitations and anxiety, but it does not explain goitre, ophthalmopathy,\npretibial dermopathy and weight loss with increased appetite. The revised distractors are plausible competing\ndiagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Thyrotoxicosis plus ophthalmopathy strongly points to Graves disease.","skill":"Recognition"},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which mechanism best explains the condition?","options":["TSH-receptor stimulation","Autonomous nodular thyroid hormone production","Thyroid follicle rupture with hormone leakage","Pituitary TSH hypersecretion"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Graves disease is caused by autoantibodies that stimulate the TSH receptor. This drives excessive\nthyroid hormone production and diffuse thyroid enlargement. The revised distractors are plausible competing\ndiagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Graves disease is receptor-stimulating autoimmunity.","skill":"Applied immunopathology"},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which feature best distinguishes Graves from other thyrotoxic causes?","options":["Increased appetite","Ophthalmopathy","Elevated heart rate","Poor sleep pattern"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Many causes of thyrotoxicosis can produce palpitations, weight loss, heat intolerance and insomnia.\nGraves ophthalmopathy is a more specific clue to Graves disease. The revised distractors are plausible competing\ndiagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Eye disease is the major Graves discriminator.","skill":"Differential discrimination"},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Thyroid function results return:\n Test Result\n TSH Low\n Free T4 High\n Free T3 High\n TSH-receptor antibodies Positive\n\nWhich interpretation is best?","options":["Primary hypothyroidism pattern","Central hypothyroidism pattern","Primary hyperthyroidism pattern","TSH-secreting pituitary pattern"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Low TSH with high thyroid hormone levels indicates primary thyrotoxicosis: the thyroid hormones are\nhigh and pituitary TSH is suppressed by negative feedback. Positive TSH-receptor antibodies support Graves\ndisease. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they\nare less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Low TSH plus high free T4 means primary hyperthyroidism/thyrotoxicosis until proven otherwise.","skill":"Endocrine data interpretation"},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"What hypersensitivity type is involved?","options":["Type II receptor-stimulating antibody reaction","Type I IgE-mediated reaction","Type III immune-complex reaction","Type IV delayed T-cell reaction"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Graves disease is a Type II hypersensitivity process because antibodies bind a cell-surface receptor\nand alter its function. In Graves, antibodies stimulate the TSH receptor rather than destroying the thyroid. The\nrevised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less\ndirectly supported by the full stem.","point":"Graves is Type II hypersensitivity with receptor stimulation.","skill":"Foundational immunology"},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"Which drug gives rapid symptomatic relief of palpitations?","options":["Carbimazole","Radioiodine","Levothyroxine","Propranolol"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Propranolol is a beta-blocker and can rapidly reduce adrenergic symptoms such as palpitations,\ntremor and anxiety. It does not cure the autoimmune disease, but it gives quick symptomatic control. The revised\ndistractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly\nsupported by the full stem.","point":"Beta-blockers rapidly control adrenergic symptoms in thyrotoxicosis.","skill":"Pharmacology principle"},{"number":7,"type":"SBA","q":"Which definitive option is safest with significant ophthalmopathy?","options":["Beta-blocker monotherapy","Thionamide therapy or thyroidectomy","Routine iodine supplementation","Radioiodine as automatic first choice"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"In Graves disease with significant ophthalmopathy, definitive disease-control planning should avoid\ntreating the case as \"palpitations only.\" Antithyroid medication such as a thionamide, or thyroidectomy in\nappropriate specialist contexts, may be considered. The exact selection depends on risk-benefit discussion,\nseverity, patient preference and local protocols. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses,\nmechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Symptom control is not definitive Graves disease control.","skill":"Management-principle reasoning"},{"number":8,"type":"SBA","q":"What explains the pretibial skin change?","options":["Dermal glycosaminoglycan deposition","Dependent venous oedema","Subcutaneous bleeding from thrombocytopenia","Cellulitis-related dermal inflammation"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Pretibial myxoedema in Graves disease is caused by glycosaminoglycan deposition in the dermis,\nproducing thickened, non-pitting, orange-peel-like skin. Keratin heat injury, venous pressure and subcutaneous\nbleeding do not explain the classic pretibial dermopathy pattern.","point":"Graves skin and eye findings involve fibroblast activation and glycosaminoglycan deposition.","skill":"Applied pathology"},{"number":9,"type":"SBA","q":"Which alternative mechanism suggests thyroiditis?","options":["TSH-receptor stimulation","Autonomous nodular hormone synthesis","Pituitary TSH excess","Follicular hormone leakage"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Thyroiditis can cause transient thyrotoxicosis by inflammatory destruction of thyroid follicles,\nreleasing preformed thyroid hormone into the circulation. That mechanism differs from Graves, where antibodies\nstimulate hormone synthesis through the TSH receptor. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses,\nmechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Graves makes hormone; thyroiditis leaks preformed hormone.","skill":"Differential mechanism"},{"number":10,"type":"SBA","q":"Which change suggests thyroid storm danger?","options":["Mild tremor with normal mentation","Cosmetic eye concern alone","Fever with confusion","Chronic weight loss with preserved hydration"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Severe thyrotoxicosis with fever, marked tachycardia, altered mental status, heart failure or\ngastrointestinal disturbance raises concern for thyroid storm. Exact scoring systems are not being tested, but the\ndanger pattern must be recognised. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or\nmanagement roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Thyrotoxicosis plus fever or altered mental state is an emergency pattern.","skill":"Danger recognition"},{"number":11,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the beta-blocker role here?","options":["Suppress TSH-receptor antibody production","Reduce adrenergic symptoms","Destroy overactive thyroid tissue","Prevent ophthalmopathy progression fully"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Beta-blockers reduce symptoms mediated by increased adrenergic sensitivity, including palpitations,\ntremor and anxiety. They do not directly cure the autoimmune thyroid disease. The revised distractors are plausible\ncompeting diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Beta-blockers are symptomatic control, not definitive Graves treatment.","skill":"Pharmacology role clarification"},{"number":12,"type":"SBA","q":"She is worried about her eye appearance and long-term treatment harms.\nWhich principle should guide the discussion?","options":["Confidentiality as the only relevant principle","Beneficence overriding consent in all cases","Patient autonomy","Public-health notification duty"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"The clinician should explain benefits, harms and alternatives in a way the patient can understand,\nthen support informed decision-making. Autonomy is central when balancing symptom control, cosmetic concerns\nand long-term complication prevention. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or\nmanagement roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Graves management choices require informed, patient-centred risk-benefit discussion.","skill":"Ethics / communication"},{"number":13,"type":"SBA","q":"Which phrase best describes treatment choice between radioiodine and surgery?","options":["Risk-benefit analysis","Syndromic diagnosis","Contact tracing process","Therapeutic trial"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Choosing between definitive treatment options requires balancing benefits, risks, contraindications,\nophthalmopathy, patient preference, access and specialist input. That is risk-benefit analysis. The revised\ndistractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly\nsupported by the full stem.","point":"Definitive endocrine treatment decisions should be framed as risk-benefit analysis.","skill":"Clinical decision-making / vertical theme"},{"number":14,"type":"SBA","q":"Which lab pattern suggests a TSH-secreting pituitary cause?","options":["Low TSH with low free T4","Low TSH with high free T4","High TSH with low free T4","High TSH with high free T4"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"A TSH-secreting pituitary adenoma would produce inappropriately high TSH despite high thyroid\nhormone. This is not the usual Graves pattern, where TSH is suppressed. The revised distractors are plausible\ncompeting diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"In Graves, high T4 suppresses TSH; high TSH with high T4 is inappropriate central drive.","skill":"Endocrine data discrimination"},{"number":15,"type":"VSAQ","q":"Which autoantibody drives Graves disease?","options":[],"answer":"TSH-receptor antibody","accepted":["Thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulin","TRAb","TSI"],"explain":"These antibodies stimulate the TSH receptor, increasing thyroid hormone synthesis and causing\ndiffuse thyroid enlargement.","point":"Graves disease is driven by TSH-receptor stimulation.","skill":"Immunology terminology"},{"number":16,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What eye finding most strongly points to Graves disease?","options":[],"answer":"Ophthalmopathy","accepted":["Exophthalmos","proptosis","eye prominence","lid lag"],"explain":"Graves ophthalmopathy is a distinguishing feature compared with many other causes of\nthyrotoxicosis.","point":"Ophthalmopathy is the classic Graves-specific clinical clue.","skill":"Clinical terminology"}],"source":"Student Tier 1 Teaching Bank"},{"id":29,"module":"Endocrine","title":"Hypoglycaemia","scenario":"A 59-year-old man with type 2 diabetes is brought to the emergency department by his daughter because he\nbecame confused and aggressive at home. Earlier that morning, he took his usual diabetes tablets but skipped\nbreakfast because he felt nauseated. He then walked to the taxi rank and became sweaty, shaky and light-headed.\nBy the time his daughter found him, he was speaking nonsense and struggling to stand. On the way to hospital, he\nbriefly became unresponsive but did not have clear tonic-clonic movements.\nHis medication list includes metformin and a sulfonylurea. He has chronic kidney disease and lives alone. He has\nno fever, no chest pain, no focal limb weakness and no recent head trauma.\nOn examination, he is drowsy but rousable.\n Finding Result\n Temperature 36.5 °C\n Heart rate 116/min\n Blood pressure 138/78 mmHg\n Respiratory rate 18/min\n Oxygen saturation 98% room air\n\nHe is sweating and tremulous. His speech is confused but there is no persistent facial droop, limb weakness or\nneck stiffness.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest working diagnosis?","options":["Acute stroke syndrome","Hypoglycaemia","Postictal confusion after unwitnessed seizure","Intoxication or behavioural disturbance"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"A diabetic patient who took glucose-lowering medication, missed a meal, exercised and then\ndeveloped sweating, tremor, confusion and collapse has hypoglycaemia until proven otherwise. This is a reversible\nemergency. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but\nthey are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Altered mental status in a diabetic patient means check for hypoglycaemia immediately.","skill":"Recognition"},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which immediate bedside test is most important?","options":["Serum bilirubin","Peak expiratory flow","Urine ketones only","Capillary glucose"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Bedside capillary glucose rapidly identifies hypoglycaemia and separates it from other diabetic\nemergencies. Urine ketones may help in DKA reasoning, but it must not replace immediate glucose testing in a\nconfused diabetic patient. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management\nroles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"The first dangerous reversible cause to check is low glucose.","skill":"Immediate safety assessment"},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which symptom group is autonomic?","options":["Sweating and tremor","Confusion and aggression","Focal facial droop","Neck stiffness with photophobia"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Autonomic symptoms of hypoglycaemia include sweating, tremor, palpitations, anxiety and hunger.\nThese occur because the body activates counter-regulatory responses to low glucose. The revised distractors are\nplausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full\nstem.","point":"Sweating, tremor and palpitations are autonomic hypoglycaemia clues.","skill":"Symptom classification"},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which symptom is neuroglycopenic?","options":["Palpitations","Hunger","Confusion","Tremor"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Neuroglycopenic symptoms occur when the brain does not receive enough glucose. They include\nbehavioural change, confusion, weakness, visual disturbance, seizure, loss of consciousness and coma. The\nrevised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less\ndirectly supported by the full stem.","point":"Confusion in hypoglycaemia is brain glucose deprivation.","skill":"Symptom classification"},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Which factor most likely triggered this episode?","options":["Symptoms with low glucose and improvement after glucose correction","Symptoms with normal glucose and persistent confusion","Low glucose without symptoms or change after correction","Confusion with normal glucose but raised ketones"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Taking glucose-lowering medication and then missing a meal is a classic hypoglycaemia trigger.\nExercise can worsen the risk by increasing glucose utilisation. The revised distractors are plausible competing\ndiagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Diabetes medication + missed meal + activity = hypoglycaemia risk.","skill":"Risk-factor reasoning"},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"Initial results return:\n Test/finding Result\n Capillary glucose Low\n Urine ketones Negative\n Respiratory pattern Normal depth\n Temperature Normal\n Focal neurological deficit Absent\n\nWhich interpretation is best?","options":["Diabetic ketoacidosis","Hypoglycaemic episode","Acute meningitis","Acute stroke syndrome"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Low bedside glucose with autonomic and neuroglycopenic symptoms confirms the key metabolic\npattern. The absence of ketones and Kussmaul-type breathing makes DKA less likely. The revised distractors are\nplausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full\nstem.","point":"Low glucose with symptoms is the central diagnostic data point.","skill":"Data interpretation"},{"number":7,"type":"SBA","q":"Which statement best describes Whipple's triad?","options":["Fever, rash and neck stiffness","Wheeze, cough and hypoxia","Symptoms, low glucose and relief","Jaundice, pruritus and pale stool"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Whipple's triad is: symptoms consistent with hypoglycaemia, documented low glucose, and relief of\nsymptoms after glucose is corrected. It links the symptoms to hypoglycaemia rather than treating a glucose value in\nisolation. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they\nare less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Whipple's triad connects symptoms to low glucose and symptom relief after correction.","skill":"Core concept recall"},{"number":8,"type":"SBA","q":"He is awake enough to swallow safely.\nWhich treatment principle is safest?","options":["Wait for CT brain","Start antibiotics first","Give bronchodilator only","Give oral glucose"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"If the patient is conscious and can swallow safely, oral glucose or a fast-acting carbohydrate principle\nis appropriate. Exact carbohydrate amount is protocol-sensitive and not being tested. The revised distractors are\nplausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full\nstem.","point":"Conscious hypoglycaemic patient who can swallow needs rapid oral glucose.","skill":"Management principle"},{"number":9,"type":"SBA","q":"He becomes too drowsy to swallow safely.\nWhich treatment principle is safest?","options":["Persistent unilateral weakness after glucose correction","Neck stiffness with fever","Low bedside glucose with symptom improvement after correction","Sudden thunderclap headache"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"If the patient cannot safely swallow, oral treatment risks aspiration. Severe hypoglycaemia needs\nsupervised parenteral glucose support, such as IV dextrose or glucagon according to local protocol. The revised\ndistractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly\nsupported by the full stem.","point":"Do not give oral glucose to a patient who cannot swallow safely.","skill":"Patient safety / management principle"},{"number":10,"type":"SBA","q":"Why is sulfonylurea use important?","options":["It blocks thyroid release","It causes bronchospasm","It stimulates insulin release","It prevents ketone use"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Sulfonylureas increase insulin secretion from pancreatic beta cells. This can cause hypoglycaemia,\nespecially with missed meals, older age, renal impairment, medication errors or recurrent episodes. The revised\ndistractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly\nsupported by the full stem.","point":"Sulfonylureas can cause hypoglycaemia by increasing insulin secretion.","skill":"Pharmacology mechanism"},{"number":11,"type":"SBA","q":"Why is chronic kidney disease relevant?","options":["It lowers seizure risk","It protects from tablets","It excludes diabetes emergencies","It increases hypoglycaemia risk"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Chronic kidney disease can increase hypoglycaemia risk through impaired medication clearance,\nreduced gluconeogenic reserve and greater vulnerability during poor intake or intercurrent illness. It does not lower\nseizure risk, protect from tablets or exclude diabetic emergencies.","point":"CKD makes glucose-lowering therapy more dangerous.","skill":"Risk-factor reasoning"},{"number":12,"type":"SBA","q":"Which pattern would suggest DKA instead?","options":["Low glucose with sweating","Ketones with acidosis","Tremor after skipped meal","Hunger with palpitations"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"DKA is characterised by hyperglycaemia, ketones and metabolic acidosis, often with dehydration,\nvomiting, abdominal pain and Kussmaul respirations. Hypoglycaemia is defined by low glucose with autonomic and\nneuroglycopenic symptoms. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or\nmanagement roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"DKA is ketotic acidosis; hypoglycaemia is low-glucose neuroglycopenia.","skill":"Differential discrimination"},{"number":13,"type":"SBA","q":"Which feature indicates severe hypoglycaemia?","options":["Mild hunger only","Brief palpitations","Sweating while alert","Needs physical assistance"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Severe hypoglycaemia is clinically important when the patient has altered mental status, seizure,\nloss of consciousness or needs help from another person. This patient needed assistance because he was\nconfused and nearly collapsed. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or\nmanagement roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Severe hypoglycaemia is defined clinically by impaired ability to self-treat.","skill":"Severity recognition"},{"number":14,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest disposition principle?","options":["Discharge immediately once awake","Stop all diabetes medication permanently","Review cause and recurrence risk","Ignore sulfonylurea history if glucose normalises"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"After correction, the cause must be reviewed: missed meal, sulfonylurea use, CKD, medication error,\nalcohol intake, poor access to food, cognitive impairment and recurrence risk. Sulfonylurea-associated\nhypoglycaemia may recur, so immediate discharge without review can be unsafe. The revised distractors are\nplausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full\nstem.","point":"Correcting glucose is not the end; identify why the episode happened.","skill":"Systems safety / recurrence prevention"},{"number":15,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What term describes brain dysfunction caused by low glucose?","options":[],"answer":"Neuroglycopenia","accepted":["Neuroglycopenic symptoms"],"explain":"Neuroglycopenia means inadequate glucose supply to the brain. It causes confusion, behavioural\nchange, weakness, visual disturbance, seizure, coma and death in severe cases.","point":"Confusion in hypoglycaemia is neuroglycopenia.","skill":"Core terminology"},{"number":16,"type":"VSAQ","q":"Which diabetes drug class stimulates insulin secretion and can cause hypoglycaemia?","options":[],"answer":"Sulfonylureas","accepted":["Sulphonylureas"],"explain":"Sulfonylureas stimulate pancreatic beta-cell insulin release. They can cause hypoglycaemia,\nespecially with missed meals, renal impairment, older age, dosing errors or recurrent episodes.","point":"Sulfonylureas are classic hypoglycaemia-risk drugs.","skill":"Pharmacology terminology"}],"source":"Student Tier 1 Teaching Bank"},{"id":27,"module":"Neuro","title":"Stroke","scenario":"A 64-year-old man is brought to the emergency department by his wife because his face suddenly \"pulled to one\nside\" while he was eating breakfast. He also dropped his cup from his left hand and started speaking unclearly.\nThe symptoms began 50 minutes ago. He has hypertension, type 2 diabetes and smokes daily. He has no\nheadache, seizure, head trauma, ear pain or vesicular rash. He has not had similar episodes before.\nOn examination, he is alert but anxious.\n Finding Result\n Temperature 36.8 °C\n Heart rate 94/min\n Blood pressure 176/96 mmHg\n Respiratory rate 18/min\n Oxygen saturation 97% room air\n\nNeurological examination shows left lower facial weakness. He can wrinkle both sides of his forehead, but the left\ncorner of the mouth droops when he smiles. There is left arm weakness and mild left leg weakness. Sensation is\nreduced on the left side. Speech is slurred but he follows commands.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest working diagnosis?","options":["Acute stroke syndrome","Bell's palsy","Ramsay Hunt syndrome","Hypoglycaemic stroke mimic"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Sudden unilateral facial droop with ipsilateral arm and leg weakness strongly suggests an acute\nstroke syndrome. Forehead sparing indicates an upper motor neuron facial pattern, not a lower motor neuron facial\nnerve palsy. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but\nthey are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Sudden focal neurological deficit is stroke until proven otherwise.","skill":"Recognition"},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which feature most strongly supports central facial weakness?","options":["Ear vesicles","Forehead sparing","Whole-face paralysis","Hyperacusis"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"In a UMN facial weakness pattern, the contralateral lower face is weak but forehead movement is\nrelatively preserved because the upper face receives bilateral cortical input. This is the classic stroke-versus-Bell's\npalsy discriminator. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles,\nbut they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Forehead sparing points to UMN facial weakness.","skill":"Localisation"},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which finding would support Bell's palsy instead?","options":["Left arm weakness","Slurred speech with limb weakness","Inability to close one eye","Sudden unilateral sensory loss"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Bell's palsy is a lower motor neuron CN VII palsy. It affects the entire ipsilateral half of the face,\nincluding the forehead and eye closure. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or\nmanagement roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Bell's palsy affects the forehead and eye closure; stroke facial weakness often spares the forehead.","skill":"Differential discrimination"},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which vascular risk factor is present?","options":["Childhood asthma","Atopic rhinitis","Recent dyspepsia","Hypertension"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Hypertension is a major vascular risk factor for stroke. Diabetes and smoking also increase vascular\nrisk in this patient. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles,\nbut they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Hypertension, diabetes and smoking raise stroke risk.","skill":"Risk-factor recognition"},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Which immediate bedside test must not be missed?","options":["ECG rhythm strip","Capillary blood glucose","Serum sodium","Urine ketones"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Hypoglycaemia is a reversible stroke mimic and can cause altered mental state, weakness, seizures\nor focal- appearing deficits. Bedside glucose should be checked early while stroke escalation continues. The\nrevised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less\ndirectly supported by the full stem.","point":"Every acute focal neurological deficit needs early glucose assessment.","skill":"Stroke-mimic safety"},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"Which examination bundle best fits CN VII testing?","options":["Wrinkle forehead, close eyes, smile","Check pupils, fundoscopy, visual acuity","Test gag, cough, palate movement","Assess tongue protrusion and shoulder shrug"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"CN VII supplies muscles of facial expression. A focused facial nerve motor examination includes\ninspecting facial symmetry, wrinkling the forehead, closing the eyes tightly, showing the teeth or smiling and puffing\nout the cheeks. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but\nthey are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"CN VII examination directly separates upper-face and lower-face weakness.","skill":"Clinical-skills examination"},{"number":7,"type":"SBA","q":"Which localisation best fits left face-arm-leg weakness?","options":["Left peripheral facial nerve","Left neuromuscular junction","Right lower motor neuron","Right cerebral hemisphere"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Motor pathways decussate, so left-sided weakness suggests a lesion in the right cerebral\nhemisphere or right descending motor pathway above the decussation. The forehead-sparing facial weakness also\nfits a contralateral UMN lesion. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or\nmanagement roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Body weakness is usually contralateral to a supranuclear cerebral lesion.","skill":"Neuroanatomical localisation"},{"number":8,"type":"SBA","q":"Initial emergency data return:\n Assessment Result\n Capillary glucose Normal\n CT brain No haemorrhage seen\n Weakness Persistent left arm weakness\n Facial pattern Forehead-sparing lower-face weakness\n Time course Sudden onset, less than 1 hour\n\nWhich interpretation is safest?","options":["Bell's palsy confirmed","Hypoglycaemia confirmed","Acute ischaemic stroke possible","Chronic neuropathy likely"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Normal glucose reduces hypoglycaemia as the explanation. CT showing no haemorrhage does not\nexclude early ischaemic stroke. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or\nmanagement roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Early CT may exclude haemorrhage but does not rule out acute ischaemia.","skill":"Data interpretation"},{"number":9,"type":"SBA","q":"Which symptom would suggest aphasia rather than dysarthria?","options":["Impaired word comprehension","Nasal voice quality","Hoarse voice only","Slow tongue movement"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Aphasia is a language disorder involving expression, comprehension or both. Dysarthria is a motor\nspeech articulation problem; the patient understands language but speech is slurred or poorly articulated. The\nrevised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less\ndirectly supported by the full stem.","point":"Aphasia is language impairment; dysarthria is articulation impairment.","skill":"Neurological symptom discrimination"},{"number":10,"type":"SBA","q":"Which feature would suggest Ramsay Hunt syndrome?","options":["Ear pain or vesicular rash with facial palsy","Forehead sparing with arm weakness","Sudden dysarthria with hemiparesis","Preserved eye closure with sensory loss"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Ramsay Hunt syndrome is a facial nerve palsy associated with herpes zoster involvement, often with\near pain or vesicles. It produces an LMN facial palsy pattern, not a forehead-sparing central pattern. The revised\ndistractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly\nsupported by the full stem.","point":"Ear pain or vesicles shift facial palsy reasoning toward Ramsay Hunt syndrome.","skill":"Differential discrimination"},{"number":11,"type":"SBA","q":"Which action is safest now?","options":["Reassure as Bell's palsy after facial exam only","Wait for outpatient neurology review","Treat eye exposure first and reassess later","Activate stroke pathway"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"He has a sudden focal neurological deficit within a potentially time-sensitive window. The correct\nGEMP 2 action is urgent stroke-pathway escalation, with supervised imaging, glucose check and senior review.\nThe revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less\ndirectly supported by the full stem.","point":"Stroke-pattern deficits need urgent pathway activation, not delayed confirmation.","skill":"Patient safety / escalation"},{"number":12,"type":"SBA","q":"Which statement about Bell's palsy is safest?","options":["It commonly causes limb weakness","It is a lower motor neuron facial palsy","It proves stroke is absent","It can be diagnosed without neurological examination"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Bell's palsy is a lower motor neuron facial nerve palsy affecting the whole ipsilateral face. It should be\ndiagnosed carefully after central and atypical features have been considered. The revised distractors are plausible\ncompeting diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Bell's palsy is an LMN facial palsy, not a limb-weakness syndrome.","skill":"Concept clarification"},{"number":13,"type":"SBA","q":"Which finding makes Bell's palsy less likely?","options":["Left arm weakness","Hyperacusis","Loss of taste","Eye closure weakness"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Bell's palsy affects CN VII and can involve whole-face weakness, impaired eye closure, hyperacusis\nor altered anterior tongue taste depending on branch involvement. It does not explain acute unilateral arm\nweakness. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they\nare less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Limb weakness is a stroke clue, not Bell's palsy.","skill":"Discriminating feature"},{"number":14,"type":"SBA","q":"Which statement about imaging is safest?","options":["Early CT confirms all ischaemic strokes","CT replaces bedside glucose testing","CT helps exclude intracranial haemorrhage","Normal CT excludes all stroke mimics"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Early CT is important to look for haemorrhage or other structural causes. A normal early CT does not\nreliably exclude acute ischaemic stroke. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms\nor management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Early CT may be normal in ischaemic stroke; do not falsely dismiss the clinical syndrome.","skill":"Imaging principle"},{"number":15,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What term describes forehead-sparing facial weakness from a central lesion?","options":[],"answer":"Upper motor neuron facial weakness","accepted":["UMN facial palsy","supranuclear facial weakness"],"explain":"A UMN lesion affects the contralateral lower face more than the forehead because upper facial\nmuscles receive bilateral cortical input.","point":"Forehead sparing is the classic UMN facial weakness clue.","skill":"Localisation terminology"},{"number":16,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What bedside test should be checked early in any suspected stroke mimic?","options":[],"answer":"Capillary blood glucose","accepted":["Bedside glucose","finger-prick glucose"],"explain":"Hypoglycaemia is a reversible neurological emergency and can mimic stroke. It must be checked\nearly while urgent stroke assessment continues.","point":"Do not miss hypoglycaemia in focal neurological presentations.","skill":"Emergency safety terminology"}],"source":"Student Tier 1 Teaching Bank"},{"id":28,"module":"Neuro","title":"Seizure","scenario":"A 19-year-old university student is brought to the emergency department after collapsing during a lecture.\nWitnesses say he suddenly stopped responding, fell to the floor, became stiff, and then had rhythmic jerking of all\nfour limbs for about 2 minutes. He bit the side of his tongue and passed urine during the episode.\nAfter the jerking stopped, he remained confused and drowsy for about 20 minutes. He now has a headache and\nmuscle aches. He has no known epilepsy. He has been sleeping poorly while studying for exams. He denies\nalcohol withdrawal, recreational drug use, head trauma, fever, neck stiffness, rash, diabetes medication use or\nprevious similar events.\nOn examination, he is drowsy but rousable.\n Finding Result\n Temperature 36.9 °C\n Heart rate 112/min\n Blood pressure 126/74 mmHg\n Respiratory rate 20/min\n Oxygen saturation 97% room air\n\nThere is a lateral tongue bite. Neck stiffness is absent. No focal neurological deficit is found once he is fully awake.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest working interpretation?","options":["Convulsive syncope","Generalised tonic-clonic seizure","Panic or psychogenic attack","Acute stroke syndrome"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Sudden loss of awareness, tonic stiffening, rhythmic bilateral limb jerking, lateral tongue bite, urinary\nincontinence and post-event confusion strongly support a generalised tonic-clonic seizure. Syncope can cause\nbrief jerks, but prolonged postictal confusion and lateral tongue bite push toward seizure. The revised distractors\nare plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the\nfull stem.","point":"Tonic-clonic movements plus postictal confusion is seizure until proven otherwise.","skill":"Recognition"},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which feature best supports seizure over syncope?","options":["Brief collapse","Standing position before collapse","Rapid recovery to baseline","Postictal confusion"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"A prolonged period of confusion or drowsiness after the event is a strong seizure clue. Syncope\nusually has more rapid recovery once cerebral perfusion returns. The revised distractors are plausible competing\ndiagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Postictal confusion is one of the best seizure-versus-syncope discriminators.","skill":"Differential discrimination"},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which immediate bedside test must not be missed?","options":["Capillary glucose","Serum sodium","ECG rhythm strip","CT brain"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Hypoglycaemia is a reversible emergency cause of seizure, collapse and altered mental status.\nBedside glucose should be checked early in any seizure or unexplained loss-of-consciousness presentation. The\nrevised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less\ndirectly supported by the full stem.","point":"Seizure plus altered mental status means check glucose early.","skill":"Immediate safety assessment"},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which symptom group suggests hypoglycaemia?","options":["Neck stiffness with photophobia","Focal facial droop with arm weakness","Sweating and tremor","Sudden palpitations with rapid recovery"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Hypoglycaemia may cause autonomic symptoms such as sweating, tremor, palpitations, anxiety and\nhunger, plus neuroglycopenic symptoms such as confusion, weakness, seizure or loss of consciousness. Painless\njaundice and pale stools suggest hepatobiliary disease. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses,\nmechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Hypoglycaemia causes both autonomic and neuroglycopenic features.","skill":"Metabolic mimic recognition"},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Which feature would raise concern for meningitis?","options":["Poor sleep only","Fever with neck stiffness","Lateral tongue bite alone","Muscle aches after jerking"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Fever, headache, neck stiffness, photophobia, rash, altered mental state or seizures can suggest\nmeningitis or encephalitis. Infection-related seizure must be treated as dangerous. The revised distractors are\nplausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full\nstem.","point":"Seizure plus fever or meningism is a CNS-infection danger pattern.","skill":"Infection danger recognition"},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"Which mechanism best explains a seizure?","options":["Transient global cerebral hypoperfusion","Peripheral muscle membrane instability","Brainstem respiratory drive suppression","Synchronous cortical neuronal firing"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"A seizure reflects abnormal excessive or synchronous neuronal activity in the brain. A generalised\ntonic-clonic seizure involves widespread cortical network activation producing loss of awareness, stiffening and\nrhythmic jerking. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles,\nbut they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Seizures are electrical cortical events, not primary muscle disease.","skill":"Applied neurophysiology"},{"number":7,"type":"SBA","q":"What does the drowsy period after jerking represent?","options":["Aura phase","Syncopal prodrome","Postictal state","Panic recovery"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"The postictal state is the recovery period after a seizure. It may include confusion, drowsiness,\nheadache, muscle aches and temporary neurological symptoms. The revised distractors are plausible competing\ndiagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Confusion after a seizure is called the postictal state.","skill":"Seizure terminology"},{"number":8,"type":"SBA","q":"Initial results return:\n Test/finding Result\n Capillary glucose Normal\n Sodium Normal\n Temperature Normal\n Neck stiffness Absent\n\n Test/finding Result\n Focal deficit after recovery Absent\n Witnessed movements Tonic-clonic\n\nWhich interpretation is best?","options":["Hypoglycaemia confirmed","First unprovoked seizure possible","Meningitis confirmed","Stroke confirmed"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Normal glucose reduces hypoglycaemia as the cause. Normal temperature and absent neck\nstiffness reduce CNS infection concern, though they do not replace clinical judgement. The revised distractors are\nplausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full\nstem.","point":"A normal glucose does not erase the seizure; it removes one reversible cause.","skill":"Data interpretation"},{"number":9,"type":"SBA","q":"Which statement about epilepsy is safest?","options":["Recurrent unprovoked seizures support epilepsy","One provoked seizure proves epilepsy","A normal glucose result proves epilepsy","Syncope with brief jerks is always epilepsy"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Epilepsy implies a tendency to recurrent unprovoked seizures. A single seizure may be provoked by\nhypoglycaemia, infection, intoxication, withdrawal, electrolyte disturbance or trauma and does not automatically\nequal epilepsy. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but\nthey are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Do not label every first seizure as epilepsy.","skill":"Concept clarification"},{"number":10,"type":"SBA","q":"Which feature suggests focal seizure onset?","options":["Generalised body aches after the event","Urinary incontinence during collapse","Drowsiness after a bilateral convulsion","One-arm jerking first"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Jerking that begins in one limb, one-sided sensory symptoms, focal motor activity, deja vu, focal\nautomatisms or speech arrest can suggest focal seizure onset. Oedema, abdominal pain and jaundice do not\nlocalise seizure onset.","point":"Focal onset means symptoms begin in one brain network before possible spread.","skill":"Localisation / seizure classification"},{"number":11,"type":"SBA","q":"Which pattern is most dangerous?","options":["Brief postictal headache","Muscle ache after seizure","Repeated seizures without recovery","Single tongue bite only"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Repeated seizures without return to baseline, or a prolonged ongoing seizure, is a seizure\nemergency pattern. The exact status-epilepticus timing threshold is protocol-sensitive, but the danger pattern is\nanswer-critical. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but\nthey are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Persistent or recurrent seizure activity without recovery is an emergency.","skill":"Emergency severity recognition"},{"number":12,"type":"SBA","q":"Which first-aid action is safest during the seizure?","options":["Force the mouth open to prevent tongue swallowing","Protect from injury","Restrain all limbs until jerking stops","Give oral tablets during the convulsion"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"During a convulsive seizure, protect the patient from injury, maintain safety, position appropriately\nwhen possible and call for help. Do not force objects into the mouth or restrain limbs. The revised distractors are\nplausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full\nstem.","point":"Do not put anything in the mouth during a convulsive seizure.","skill":"Immediate safety / first aid"},{"number":13,"type":"SBA","q":"Which finding after recovery needs urgent escalation?","options":["Persistent focal weakness","Mild headache after the seizure","Muscle soreness after jerking","Tiredness that steadily improves"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Persistent focal weakness after a seizure may be postictal Todd paresis, but stroke or structural\nbrain disease must be considered. The safe response is urgent neurological assessment rather than assuming it is\nbenign. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they\nare less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Persistent focal deficit after seizure is not automatically \"just postictal.\"","skill":"Post-seizure danger recognition"},{"number":14,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest disposition principle?","options":["Discharge without review if fully awake","Ignore witness history once glucose is normal","Diagnose panic disorder if examination normalises","Senior assessment and work-up"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"A first seizure requires senior assessment, careful history from witnesses, examination, glucose and\nrelevant investigations for reversible or dangerous causes. The student-level task is to recognise and escalate, not\nindependently decide long-term antiseizure therapy. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses,\nmechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"First seizure needs structured assessment for provoked and unprovoked causes.","skill":"Patient safety / escalation"},{"number":15,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What bedside test should be checked early after seizure or unexplained collapse?","options":[],"answer":"Capillary blood glucose","accepted":["Bedside glucose","finger-prick glucose"],"explain":"Hypoglycaemia is a reversible emergency cause of seizure, altered mental status, collapse and\ncoma.","point":"Always check glucose early in seizure or altered consciousness.","skill":"Emergency terminology"},{"number":16,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What term describes the confused recovery period after a seizure?","options":[],"answer":"Postictal state","accepted":["Postictal phase"],"explain":"The postictal state is the period after a seizure when the patient may be confused, drowsy, weak,\nheadachey or sore.","point":"Postictal confusion strongly supports seizure over simple faint.","skill":"Seizure terminology"}],"source":"Student Tier 1 Teaching Bank"},{"id":9,"module":"Reproductive","title":"Pre-eclampsia","scenario":"A 31-year-old woman, G2P1, presents to the maternity assessment unit at 33 weeks' gestation with a severe\nfrontal headache and flashing lights in her vision. She also reports nausea and pain under her right ribs. Her ankles\nhave become more swollen over the past week.\nShe attended booking late and has missed several antenatal visits because of transport costs. Her previous\npregnancy was uncomplicated.\nOn examination, she looks uncomfortable and anxious.\n Parameter Finding\n\n Blood pressure 172/112 mmHg\n\n Heart rate 96 beats/min\n\n Respiratory rate 20 breaths/min\n\n Oxygen saturation 97% on room air\n\n Temperature 36.9 C\n\nThere is facial and pedal oedema. She has right upper-quadrant tenderness. Reflexes are brisk. Fetal movements\nare reduced compared with usual.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"Which diagnosis best explains the overall presentation?","options":["Severe pre-eclampsia with imminent eclampsia risk","Migraine with coincidental gestational hypertension","Hyperemesis-related dehydration with headache","Uncomplicated gestational oedema"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"The pattern is pregnancy after 20 weeks with severe hypertension and multisystem warning features:\nsevere headache, visual symptoms, RUQ pain, oedema, brisk reflexes and reduced fetal movements. This is\nsevere pre-eclampsia until proven otherwise, with imminent eclampsia risk. The revised distractors are plausible\ncompeting diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"","skill":"Clinical reasoning"},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which feature most strongly indicates severe maternal danger?","options":["Mild ankle swelling alone","Nausea without vomiting","Severe headache with visual symptoms","Anxiety during maternity assessment"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Severe headache and visual symptoms indicate central nervous system involvement and possible\nimminent eclampsia. In the context of very high BP, this is a maternal emergency. The revised distractors are\nplausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full\nstem.","point":"","skill":"Clinical reasoning"},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which pathophysiological sequence best explains pre-eclampsia as a multisystem disease?","options":["Placental malperfusion causing systemic endothelial dysfunction","Primary maternal renal infection causing proteinuria","Migraine-related cerebral vasospasm without placental disease","Normal pregnancy plasma-volume expansion alone"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Pre-eclampsia begins with abnormal placental development and malperfusion. This drives\ninflammatory and anti-angiogenic effects, causing widespread maternal endothelial dysfunction. The revised\ndistractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly\nsupported by the full stem.","point":"","skill":"Clinical reasoning"},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Her investigations return:\n Test Result\n\n Urine protein Positive\n\n Platelets Low\n\n Creatinine Raised\n\n AST/ALT Raised\n\n Haemoglobin Mildly low\n\n Fetal assessment Estimated fetal growth below expected range\n\nWhich interpretation is most appropriate?","options":["Reassuring pregnancy variation because proteinuria can occur normally","Isolated iron-deficiency anaemia with incidental hypertension","Cholestatic liver disease unrelated to pregnancy hypertension","Multisystem pre-eclampsia with maternal and fetal involvement"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Proteinuria, thrombocytopenia, renal dysfunction, raised liver enzymes and fetal growth restriction fit\npre-eclampsia as a multisystem maternal-placental disease. The reduced fetal growth reflects uteroplacental\ndysfunction. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but\nthey are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"","skill":"Clinical reasoning"},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Which medication is most directly used to prevent or treat seizures in severe pre- eclampsia/eclampsia under\nsupervised care?","options":["Labetalol","Nifedipine","Magnesium sulphate","Diazepam as routine first-line prevention"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Magnesium sulphate is the key anticonvulsant drug used for seizure prevention in severe\npre-eclampsia and for treatment/prevention of recurrent seizures in eclampsia. This question tests the drug's role,\nnot its dose or regimen. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management\nroles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"","skill":"Clinical reasoning"},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"A junior colleague says, \"Her BP is high, so magnesium sulphate will fix the blood pressure.\" Which correction is\nbest?","options":["Magnesium sulphate is a first-line antibiotic for hypertensive disorders of pregnancy","Magnesium sulphate is mainly used for seizure prevention/treatment; severe BP control\nrequires appropriate antihypertensive therapy under supervision","Labetalol, nifedipine and hydralazine are used to prevent eclamptic seizures directly","No medication is needed if the fetus is still alive"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Magnesium sulphate is used for seizure prophylaxis/treatment in severe pre-eclampsia/eclampsia.\nSevere hypertension is controlled with antihypertensive agents such as labetalol, nifedipine or hydralazine under\nprotocol and senior supervision. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or\nmanagement roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"","skill":"Clinical reasoning"},{"number":7,"type":"SBA","q":"While waiting for senior review, she has a generalised tonic-clonic seizure.\nWhat is the safest interpretation?","options":["Epilepsy unrelated to pregnancy until proven otherwise","Postictal syncope from dehydration","Fetal assessment should precede maternal stabilisation","Eclampsia until proven otherwise"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"A generalised seizure in a patient with hypertension/pre-eclampsia features is eclampsia until proven\notherwise. Immediate priorities are airway, breathing, circulation, preventing further seizures, controlling severe BP\nand urgent obstetric escalation. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or\nmanagement roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"","skill":"Clinical reasoning"},{"number":8,"type":"SBA","q":"Which complication is most directly suggested by the combination of low platelets, raised liver enzymes and RUQ\npain in severe pre-eclampsia?","options":["HELLP-spectrum disease","Physiological anaemia of pregnancy","Reflux disease with incidental LFT changes","Benign gestational thrombocytopenia without risk"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"HELLP refers to haemolysis, elevated liver enzymes and low platelets. RUQ/ epigastric pain in\npre-eclampsia is concerning because it can reflect liver involvement. The revised distractors are plausible\ncompeting diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"","skill":"Clinical reasoning"},{"number":9,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What is the name for generalised seizures occurring in a patient with pre-eclampsia?","options":[],"answer":"Eclampsia","accepted":[],"explain":"Eclampsia is the occurrence of generalised tonic-clonic seizures in the context of\npre-eclampsia/hypertensive disease of pregnancy, after other causes are considered but without delaying\nemergency care.\nPrimary skill tested: Core terminology","point":"Pre-eclampsia + seizure = eclampsia.","skill":"Clinical reasoning"},{"number":10,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What is the key vascular/pathological process in pre-eclampsia that links placental disease to maternal\nhypertension, proteinuria and organ dysfunction?","options":[],"answer":"Endothelial dysfunction","accepted":[],"explain":"Abnormal placental perfusion triggers systemic endothelial dysfunction. This produces\nvasoconstriction, capillary leak, renal glomerular injury, liver involvement, platelet activation/consumption and fetal\nplacental insufficiency.\nPrimary skill tested: Mechanism recall","point":"Endothelial dysfunction is the bridge between placental pathology and maternal multisystem disease.","skill":"Clinical reasoning"}],"source":"Student Tier 1 Teaching Bank"},{"id":22,"module":"Reproductive","title":"Pelvic inflammatory disease","scenario":"A 23-year-old woman presents to a primary healthcare clinic with 4 days of worsening bilateral lower abdominal\npain. The pain is dull and constant, with sharper pain during intercourse. She has yellow-green vaginal discharge\nwith an unpleasant smell. Her last menstrual period was 2 weeks ago. She is sexually active and has had a new\npartner in the past month. Condom use has been inconsistent. She is unsure of her HIV status. She has mild\ndysuria but no flank pain. She has not fainted and has no shoulder-tip pain.\n Finding Result\n Temperature 38.2 C\n Heart rate 106/min\n Blood pressure 108/70 mmHg\n Respiratory rate 20/min\n Oxygen saturation 98% room air\n\nAbdominal examination shows suprapubic and bilateral lower abdominal tenderness without guarding. Pelvic\nexamination shows mucopurulent cervical discharge, cervical motion tenderness and bilateral adnexal tenderness.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest working diagnosis?","options":["Uncomplicated lower UTI","Pelvic inflammatory disease","Ectopic pregnancy","Acute appendicitis"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Lower abdominal pain, mucopurulent cervical discharge, fever, dyspareunia, cervical motion\ntenderness and adnexal tenderness strongly support pelvic inflammatory disease. UTI, dysmenorrhoea and\ngastroenteritis do not explain the full pelvic examination pattern.","point":"Lower abdominal pain plus cervical motion tenderness and discharge = PID until proven otherwise.","skill":"Recognition"},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which finding best supports PID?","options":["Mild dysuria with leukocyturia","New sexual partner with discharge only","Cervical motion tenderness with adnexal tenderness","Normal oxygen saturation"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Cervical motion tenderness is a key pelvic examination sign suggesting upper genital tract\ninvolvement. In the context of discharge and adnexal tenderness, it strongly supports PID.","point":"Cervical motion tenderness is a high-yield PID discriminator.","skill":"Discriminating feature"},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which mechanism best explains PID?","options":["Ascending polymicrobial infection","Localised lower cervical infection only","Ovarian vascular occlusion","Implantation of endometrial tissue outside the uterus"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"PID usually results from ascending infection from the lower genital tract into the upper genital tract. It\nmay involve endometritis, salpingitis, pelvic peritonitis or tubo-ovarian abscess.","point":"PID is an ascending upper-genital-tract infection.","skill":"Applied pathology"},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which organism pair is most classically implicated?","options":["E. coli and Klebsiella","S. aureus and S. pyogenes","Candida and Trichophyton","N. gonorrhoeae and C. trachomatis"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Neisseria gonorrhoeae and Chlamydia trachomatis are classic STI pathogens associated with PID.\nThe syndrome is often polymicrobial, so anaerobes and other genital-tract organisms may also contribute.","point":"PID is commonly linked to gonorrhoea, chlamydia and polymicrobial genital-tract infection.","skill":"Microbiology linkage"},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Why is a pregnancy test important?","options":["It confirms ovarian torsion","It checks ectopic risk","It excludes all infection","It rules out endometriosis"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"In any reproductive-age patient with lower abdominal or pelvic pain, pregnancy must be assessed\nearly because ectopic pregnancy is dangerous and can mimic pelvic infection.","point":"Pelvic pain requires early pregnancy-status assessment.","skill":"Safety/differential prioritisation"},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"Which complication is most directly linked?","options":["Tubal infertility","Renal calculi","Liver failure","Pneumothorax"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"PID can damage fallopian tubes through inflammation and scarring. This can cause infertility, chronic\npelvic pain and increased ectopic pregnancy risk.","point":"PID damages tubes; damaged tubes cause infertility and ectopic pregnancy risk.","skill":"Complication reasoning"},{"number":7,"type":"SBA","q":"Initial data show a negative pregnancy test, mild urine leukocytes, purulent cervical discharge, cervical motion\ntenderness and bilateral adnexal tenderness. Which interpretation is best?","options":["Pregnancy is confirmed","Cystitis is proven","PID remains likely","HIV explains pain"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"The negative pregnancy test reduces ectopic pregnancy concern, but the pelvic findings still strongly\nsupport PID. Mild leukocyturia can occur from urethritis or contamination and should not distract from the\ncervical/adnexal pattern.","point":"Mild urine leukocytes must not override a clear PID pelvic-exam pattern.","skill":"Data interpretation"},{"number":8,"type":"SBA","q":"Which treatment principle is safest?","options":["Analgesia with delayed review","Antifungal cream alone","Wait for all cultures","Empiric syndromic treatment"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"PID is treated on a syndromic/empiric basis when the clinical pattern is convincing, especially where\ndelayed laboratory confirmation could allow progression. The exact antibiotic regimen is protocol- sensitive and is\nnot being tested here.","point":"Suspected PID is treated empirically; do not wait for perfect confirmation.","skill":"Pharmacology/treatment principle"},{"number":9,"type":"SBA","q":"Why is combination therapy needed?","options":["Usually polymicrobial infection","Usually viral-only infection","Usually autoimmune disease","Usually fungal-only disease"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"PID often involves more than one organism, including gonorrhoea, chlamydia, anaerobes and other\ngenital-tract bacteria. Combination therapy targets the polymicrobial syndrome.","point":"PID treatment covers a polymicrobial upper-genital-tract infection.","skill":"Microbiology/pharmacology reasoning"},{"number":10,"type":"SBA","q":"Her HIV test returns positive. She is clinically stable with suspected PID. Which statement is safest?","options":["Delay PID antibiotics until HIV staging is completed","HIV diagnosis does not remove the need for immediate PID treatment","Treat HIV first and reassess PID later","PID is unlikely if HIV is positive"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"A positive HIV test does not remove the need to treat suspected PID. HIV diagnosis should trigger\ncounselling and linkage to HIV care, but it should not delay PID treatment.","point":"HIV care is integrated with PID care; it does not replace immediate PID treatment.","skill":"Integrated care/vertical theme"},{"number":11,"type":"SBA","q":"Which partner-care approach is best?","options":["Publicly identify the partner to prevent transmission","Withhold the patient's treatment until the partner attends","Encourage partner notification with confidentiality and support","Ignore partner exposure if the patient receives treatment"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Partner notification is important for STI control and preventing reinfection. The safest approach is\npatient-centred disclosure support and partner notification while respecting confidentiality and consent.","point":"STI care includes partner notification without violating confidentiality.","skill":"Communication/public health"},{"number":12,"type":"SBA","q":"Which change suggests severe PID?","options":["Mild dyspareunia with stable observations","Bilateral adnexal tenderness only","Discharge without systemic features","Hypotension with guarding"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Hypotension with guarding suggests systemic deterioration, peritonitis, sepsis or complicated pelvic\ninfection. That requires urgent escalation beyond routine outpatient syndromic care.","point":"PID with hypotension or peritonitis is not routine outpatient disease.","skill":"Severity/escalation recognition"},{"number":13,"type":"SBA","q":"Which finding suggests tubo-ovarian abscess?","options":["Adnexal mass with fever","Dysuria after intercourse","Thin clear discharge","Vulval itch without pelvic pain"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Tubo-ovarian abscess is a complicated PID pathway. Persistent fever, adnexal mass, severe pelvic\npain or systemic toxicity should raise concern and trigger urgent escalation.","point":"PID plus adnexal mass or persistent fever raises concern for tubo-ovarian abscess.","skill":"Complication recognition"},{"number":14,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What mechanism links PID to ectopic pregnancy?","options":[],"answer":"Tubal scarring","accepted":["Tubal scarring","fallopian-tube scarring","tubal damage"],"explain":"Inflammation damages the fallopian tubes. Scarring can impair embryo transport, increasing the risk\nof ectopic pregnancy.","point":"PID causes ectopic risk through tubal scarring.","skill":"Mechanism terminology"},{"number":15,"type":"VSAQ","q":"Why is PID antibiotic coverage broad?","options":[],"answer":"Polymicrobial infection","accepted":["Polymicrobial infection","mixed infection","multiple organisms"],"explain":"PID commonly involves gonorrhoea, chlamydia, anaerobes and other genital-tract organisms. That is\nwhy broad or combination coverage is used.","point":"PID is not a single-organism syndrome.","skill":"Microbiology terminology"}],"source":"Student Tier 1 Teaching Bank"},{"id":18,"module":"Paediatrics","title":"Neonatal jaundice","scenario":"A 2-day-old girl is brought to the emergency department because her mother noticed yellowing of the eyes and\nskin. The baby has become sleepier today and is not feeding well. She has vomited twice and seems less\nresponsive than usual. She was born at term by vaginal delivery. Pregnancy was uncomplicated. The mother says\nher blood group is O positive. She is unsure of the baby's blood group. The baby passed meconium after birth, but\nthe mother says the stools now look normal in colour. There is no obvious fever. On examination, the neonate is\njaundiced to the abdomen. She is lethargic and has a weak suck.\n Finding Result\n Temperature 36.2 C\n Heart rate 168/min\n Respiratory rate 48/min\n Oxygen saturation 95% room air\n Capillary refill Slightly delayed\n\nThere is no hepatosplenomegaly. The abdomen is soft. No rash is seen.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest working pattern?","options":["Physiological jaundice in a well neonate","Breast-milk jaundice","Pathological jaundice","Conjugated obstructive jaundice"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"This baby is jaundiced very early and has poor feeding, lethargy and vomiting. Those are warning\nsigns. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are\nless directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Neonatal jaundice plus poor feeding or lethargy is pathological until proven otherwise.","skill":"Recognition"},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which feature most clearly shows danger?","options":["Poor feeding","Yellow sclera","Term birth","Normal stool colour"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Poor feeding is a neonatal danger sign, especially when combined with lethargy and jaundice. It may\nindicate bilirubin neurotoxicity, sepsis, dehydration or another serious illness. The revised distractors are plausible\ncompeting diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"In neonates, poor feeding is never a casual symptom.","skill":"Safety/risk prioritisation"},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which bilirubin type causes kernicterus risk?","options":["Conjugated bilirubin","Unconjugated bilirubin","Urinary conjugated bilirubin","Intestinal stercobilin pigment"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia can cross into the brain and deposit in vulnerable areas\nsuch as the basal ganglia and brainstem nuclei. This causes kernicterus. The revised distractors are plausible\ncompeting diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Kernicterus risk comes from severe unconjugated bilirubin.","skill":"Applied biochemistry"},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which mechanism helps explain physiological jaundice?","options":["Extrahepatic bile-duct obstruction","Immune-mediated red-cell haemolysis","Neonatal hepatitis causing cholestasis","Immature hepatic conjugation with high bilirubin load"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Physiological neonatal jaundice is mainly unconjugated. It reflects normal neonatal bilirubin\nphysiology: high bilirubin production from neonatal red-cell turnover, immature hepatic conjugation and increased\nenterohepatic circulation. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management\nroles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Physiological jaundice is mostly unconjugated and reflects immature bilirubin handling.","skill":"Mechanism reasoning"},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Labs show predominant unconjugated bilirubin, low haemoglobin, raised reticulocytes and positive DAT. Which\ninterpretation is best?","options":["Cholestatic jaundice","Hepatocellular injury pattern","Haemolytic jaundice","Physiological transition without danger"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Predominantly unconjugated bilirubin with anaemia, raised reticulocytes and a positive direct\nantiglobulin test supports immune-mediated haemolysis. This can produce early pathological jaundice. The revised\ndistractors are plausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly\nsupported by the full stem.","point":"Unconjugated jaundice plus anaemia and reticulocytosis suggests haemolysis.","skill":"Laboratory/data interpretation"},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"Which cause best fits the data?","options":["Blood-group incompatibility","Breast-milk jaundice","Biliary atresia","Neonatal hepatitis"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Blood-group incompatibility can cause immune haemolysis in the newborn. The positive direct\nantiglobulin test supports antibody-mediated red-cell destruction. The revised distractors are plausible competing\ndiagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Positive DAT in neonatal jaundice points toward immune haemolysis.","skill":"Cause-pattern matching"},{"number":7,"type":"SBA","q":"Which sign suggests bilirubin neurotoxicity?","options":["Mild yellow sclera only","Normal stool colour","Good feeding after feeds","Opisthotonos"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Opisthotonos is a late neurological warning sign in severe bilirubin neurotoxicity. Earlier warning\nsigns include poor feeding, lethargy and vomiting. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses,\nmechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Poor feeding and lethargy are early warning signs; opisthotonos is late and dangerous.","skill":"Danger recognition"},{"number":8,"type":"SBA","q":"Which finding suggests conjugated jaundice?","options":["Yellow sclera alone","Pale stools","Poor feeding","Mild jaundice on day 3"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Pale stools suggest reduced bile pigment reaching the intestine. This points toward cholestasis or\nbiliary obstruction and should not be dismissed as physiological jaundice. The revised distractors are plausible\ncompeting diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Pale stools in a jaundiced neonate suggest cholestasis.","skill":"Differential discrimination"},{"number":9,"type":"SBA","q":"Which treatment principle lowers bilirubin?","options":["Antibiotics for all jaundiced neonates","Iron replacement therapy","Phototherapy","Antihistamine therapy"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Phototherapy is a core treatment principle for significant unconjugated neonatal jaundice. It changes\nbilirubin into forms that can be excreted more easily. The revised distractors are plausible competing diagnoses,\nmechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Phototherapy is a bilirubin-lowering treatment principle, not a threshold-recall topic.","skill":"Therapy principle"},{"number":10,"type":"SBA","q":"Why is exchange transfusion not routine?","options":["It treats physiological jaundice in all neonates","It is safer than phototherapy for mild jaundice","It is used only for conjugated jaundice","It is selected rescue therapy"],"answer":"D","accepted":[],"explain":"Exchange transfusion is a high-intensity intervention used only in selected severe cases under\nspecialist protocol. It is not routine for every jaundiced neonate. The revised distractors are plausible competing\ndiagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Know the principle of exchange transfusion, not the nomogram thresholds.","skill":"Management-boundary reasoning"},{"number":11,"type":"SBA","q":"Which pattern suggests biliary atresia?","options":["Anaemia with raised reticulocytes","Persistent pale stools","Early positive DAT","Jaundice worsened by sepsis"],"answer":"B","accepted":[],"explain":"Persistent pale stools in a jaundiced infant suggest impaired bile flow into the gut. Biliary atresia is an\nimportant cholestatic cause and needs urgent recognition and referral-level escalation. The revised distractors are\nplausible competing diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full\nstem.","point":"Persistent pale stools in neonatal jaundice are a serious cholestatic clue.","skill":"Cholestatic red-flag recognition"},{"number":12,"type":"SBA","q":"Which statement is safest?","options":["Early jaundice is always physiological","Term birth removes serious risk","Conjugated jaundice needs review","Good tone excludes pathological jaundice"],"answer":"C","accepted":[],"explain":"Conjugated neonatal jaundice is pathological until proven otherwise. It may reflect neonatal hepatitis,\nbiliary atresia or metabolic disease and requires proper assessment. The revised distractors are plausible\ncompeting diagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Conjugated neonatal jaundice is never dismissed as physiological.","skill":"Safety principle"},{"number":13,"type":"SBA","q":"Which factor increases kernicterus risk?","options":["Sepsis","Physiological weight loss only","Mild rhinitis without systemic illness","Simple reflux with normal feeding"],"answer":"A","accepted":[],"explain":"Sepsis increases risk of bilirubin neurotoxicity. Other risk-enhancing conditions include prematurity,\nlow albumin, acidosis, hypoxia, hypoglycaemia and hypothermia. The revised distractors are plausible competing\ndiagnoses, mechanisms or management roles, but they are less directly supported by the full stem.","point":"Sick neonates are more vulnerable to bilirubin neurotoxicity.","skill":"Risk-factor reasoning"},{"number":14,"type":"VSAQ","q":"What neurological complication can follow severe unconjugated neonatal jaundice?","options":[],"answer":"Kernicterus","accepted":[],"explain":"Kernicterus is bilirubin-induced brain injury from severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia.","point":"Kernicterus is the feared brain complication of severe neonatal jaundice.","skill":"Core terminology"},{"number":15,"type":"VSAQ","q":"Which bilirubin fraction predominates in physiological neonatal jaundice?","options":[],"answer":"Unconjugated bilirubin","accepted":["Unconjugated bilirubin","indirect bilirubin"],"explain":"Physiological neonatal jaundice is predominantly unconjugated because neonatal bilirubin\nconjugation and excretion are still maturing.","point":"Physiological neonatal jaundice is mostly unconjugated.","skill":"Biochemistry terminology"}],"source":"Student Tier 1 Teaching Bank"}];
